import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "2 Corinthians",
  display: "Second Corinthians",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"In Paul's first letter, he had a lot of corrections to this church that needed to be addressed. He seemed to desire to come to this church and visit it, but there has been something hindering him from doing it for quite some time. So the next best thing was to send his trusted ministers and write them a very important letter about how to live in liberty with the Lord and abstain from sin being a controlling factor of their lives. Paul wrote with care and grace so that the church didn't feel completely discouraged, but so that they could understand that there is a victory that they can hold firm to."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this letter, Paul seems to be writing from a different spiritual viewpoint. He has love and grace for these men because they have changed their stances and taken Paul's advice. He will be writing this letter in a very different manner. It's a personal one. You can feel Paul's desire and leading in this letter. Actually, I could argue that this letter is the most personal letter we are going to get from Paul because it demonstrates some of his own personal struggles as well as God's obvious leading for him. These are things set forth as an example for this church. This church can utilize what Paul has gone through and learned from his mistakes and hangups, and use them to avoid or correct their own pains and hangups."},{"kind":"para","text":"Every chapter seems to be geared towards a different theme. It's a little more all over the place than the previous letter. The theme was pretty clear in the last letter (liberty and holy living), but the theme in this letter touches on personal attributes and encouraging topics for each chapter. It's almost as if the chapters don't really go together. Paul is just illustrating the things they need to take note of and pay attention to so they can grow and strengthen their walks in the Lord as he has learned to do in his own missionary journey. He will focus a lot on the comforts and encouragement of church ministry and how to keep yourself focused on eternity so you are not encumbered by the present. This chapter focuses on promises and affirmations. It's a very important chapter for any minister of God who struggles with keeping promises to their congregants."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"The Apostle","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we question the opening of this letter being written by Paul, then we should question the previous ones writing as well. Paul calls himself \"an apostle\" right off the bat. That is to demonstrate that he is called as someone with knowledge. Someone with given power. Authority. He says he is called this \"by the will of God.\" When I was first called into ministry, I told some of the most trusted counsellors I knew at the time. Instead of them encouraging me into it, they simply said, “You won’t be able to do it. You are not ready. Stuff like that doesn’t work.” Or they have asked \"How do you know this is God's calling?\" I can't really explain how to answer that even still today. I am certain that He called me, but I couldn't tell you how I could prove it."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"He has unsettled me. He has given me a burden. He has led me in a direction. He has opened up opportunities to do what He has called me to."},{"kind":"para","text":"How else could I say it?"},{"kind":"para","text":"He has given me no choice but to pursue after His Will in the matter, and that is the way in which I really need to go. I can't help but follow it. If I don't, I will live in depression and regret. That is the best way to put it. Paul had a calling, and he went for it because he couldn't help himself but follow. He knew for sure what God wanted him to do, and he was going to do it even if it meant that his life would be in danger. This calling was to preach the gospel to every creature. That is why He says, \"Grace and peace\" in the next verse:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"\"Grace and peace\" is the general greeting of Paul. It symbolizes unity between the Jewish church and the Gentile church. Grace is how the Gentiles greeted each other, and peace is how the Jews greeted each other."},{"kind":"para","text":"He was demonstrating the importance of combining as one. He sets up this reminder in nearly every letter he writes to the churches. Of course, in this letter, we will find that Paul desperately means this greeting."},{"kind":"para","text":"In his first letter, it might have seemed as if the greeting was emphasized by the fact that they were lacking that in their code of conduct in the church. They had a lot of very simple and childish things to deal with in the church, and so grace and peace was the basis of it all, after they had dealt with the problem at hand. In this letter, Paul seems to flip the script a little bit. There are still things (of course) that need to be trained and better dealt with, but Paul seems to be writing from a mode of sensitivity. Grace and peace is quite the theme coming from Paul in this letter."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":11,"title":"The Comforter","entries":[{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"This verse is not meant to be funny, but it's a little funny (at least to me) because of the amount of times Paul says the word \"comfort.\" He says it a total of five times in two verses."},{"kind":"para","text":"He wants it to be understood that God is a God of comfort."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Perhaps Paul was feeling a need for a comforting Spirit by the Lord. Or maybe he was personally in a season of comfort, and hearing about the news of the Corinthian church gave him an increased comfort."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"So the best thing to do with something that God gives you is to turn around and give it to others who are in need of it as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"And who ISN'T in need of some comfort? Jesus promised to send comfort when He ascended, and it is certainly something that is here for every Christian that accepts salvation for themselves."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Comforter, which is the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he shall teach you all things, and bring all things to your remembrance, whatsoever I have said unto you.","ref":"John 14:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps that is the reminder you need today as you are reading it. I know it's something I need to be consistently reminded of."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I need the comfort of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"I need to know He is there, and I need to feel His presence for me. I need to have His comfort in my life. Without it, I will feel defeated."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are a lot of seasons in my life where there are pressures from every side. People are terrible. They are hateful. They are hurtful. They are purposely out to try and destroy and kill people who acclaim Christ personally. I don't even have to know the person, and he can still hate me simply because I believe in the love and comfort of God. Without God's comfort, those people might seriously get to me. They might make me feel useless and burdened. They might even lead me to suicide."},{"kind":"para","text":"But knowing God's comfort has kept me in check. It has allowed me a reason to live and a purpose to continue to love others even if there are a certain few who don't really seem to care. God's comfort gives us motivation to stay the course while the rest of the world around us tells us to quit. That is ultimately what tribulation looks like. Tribulation is the world’s way of trying to force Christians out of the way. The Corinthian church (if you don't recall) had a very large community encircled around them that was calling them out of holy living and trying to entice them to accept terrible and sinful practices inside the church. They were struggling while they did that (however), and so they needed direction and leadership from a church veteran (which was Paul). Paul addressed those issues, and now I assume they are getting heat from the community around them because they are starting to reject that culture and way of living. At the very least, they are keeping it out of the church now, and the people around them are not happy about it. So Paul is sending them this letter out of a mode of comfort. He is reminding them that God is going to comfort them, even in the middle of their trials and tribulations."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If trials and sufferings come, we are assured (here and in other parts of Scripture) that Christ will bring us consolation."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"God sends consolation when you are in most need of it."},{"kind":"thought","text":"You might miss it because of all the terribly loud and lying voices screaming at you, but there will be consolation there."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what happened to Paul when he was feeling very alone and shunned by the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church was under the impression that Paul was a psychopath looking to kill the Christians at their church. However, Paul had a very changed life and needed the Lord to provide some consolation. So God DOES:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Joses, who by the apostles was surnamed Barnabas, (which is, being interpreted, The son of consolation,) a Levite, and of the country of Cyprus...And when Saul was come to Jerusalem, he assayed to join himself to the disciples: but they were all afraid of him, and believed not that he was a disciple. But Barnabas took him, and brought him to the apostles, and declared unto them how he had seen the Lord in the way, and that he had spoken to him, and how he had preached boldly at Damascus in the name of Jesus.","ref":"Acts 4:36; 9:26-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"God provided a man who was literally named \"consolation\" to Paul to encourage Paul and help him get started. Now Paul is telling them about Christ who is their consolation. He reminds them that they have a continuous consolation. Barnabas will eventually die, and that consolation will end with him. But Christ will never die. So He is in a constant mode of encouraging, equipping, and comforting."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The things that befell the Apostles were set as an example for the rest of the churches. The Apostles were at the front lines. They were taking the brunt of the hits. They were being attacked for the sake of the Christians that were following them."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The attacks on the Apostles were for the consolation and salvation of the churches. The attacks on the churches were set by people who didn't love the church. They were people who wanted to stop consolation and salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there are people who desire to stop that around your church, that is more of a reason that we should be locking arms with brothers and sisters in the faith. Sadly, I don't see that in the church. I see people running and hiding because there are sufferings from people in the church who hate the church and are trying to destroy the church."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I pray that suffering brings us closer to people of the faith, not further away."},{"kind":"para","text":"But the thing we must remember (as Paul is writing the theme of this chapter) is God provides comfort during times of suffering. The Apostles’ suffering was bringing comfort to the churches. The churches’ suffering should bring comfort to the congregation. It should bring unity and strength."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Apostles felt comfort during the suffering. The churches should feel comfort as well. They should all know that God will not leave them without comfort."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Suffering for the sake of holy living is a great indication that you are doing something right."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world hates people who are set apart."},{"kind":"thought","text":"With that said, I think there are a lot of Christians who are purposely just downright mean. They act the \"holier-than-thou\" bit far too much and it just rubs people the wrong way intentionally. They are not people of \"consolation.\" They are people of law and rule, but not people of grace and peace."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Corinthian church was not just out debating people about their wayward living. Rather, they were changing the way they did things in their own church, and the people didn't like that. They probably didn't like that because they didn't have the right to delve into their sins that they loved so much anymore. Honestly, they could continue to do whatever they wanted outside of those four church walls, but they simply were not allowed to bring it into the church. Conviction set in, however, and so they would go home feeling convicted about their sins, and they would hate the fact that they could no longer comfortably dabble in what they once thought was fun. They fight against it, and the best people to blame is the church who has been preaching against it."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"I had a friend who was addicted to pornography. He was not a Christian man, and he didn't see anything wrong with it. His wife came to me about the issue and asked how I would address the problem. I told her that pornography is equivalent to \"adultery\" according to Christ. Looking on a woman to lust after her is adultery. I came to talk to my friend about it, and explained to him the problem with it, and he didn't want to hear it. Eventually, his wife told him the same thing after he was caught again watching it. He said, \"Shut up! Every time you bring that word up ‘adultery,' you sound like Jonathan. Now I can't even watch it without thinking of him.\" All I can say to that is... GOOD! I hope I'm the one that ruins his thoughts whenever he falls into that sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many people who fight against their guilt and convictions, and they shouldn't be. They should be accepting the Spirit's working on them and be willing to change because they understand how terrible it is to delve into these things."}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When Paul wrote the first letter to them, he was in Ephesus. He stayed there for a long time and ministered to those people. According to Acts, they became a rowdy bunch towards the end. He had changed their way of living nearly completely, and the people making idols didn't like this and enticed a riot against Paul and the Christians ministering there. Paul barely escaped with his life. He was likely very close to death. He probably even thought he would die and not get to start church abroad. He seriously thought that was the last of his ministry. Yet, the Lord provided an escape for them. He provided a means to continue forward with what He called them to do. That is the great hope for the church today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We don't know what the future holds, but if the Lord so desires, He can continue to make it happen in our lives and in our churches. I see churches fall apart left and right in our society today. When a pastor passes or leaves, the church seems to waver and disperse. The Lord will allow the church to live further if He so desires. That is the direction we should all be looking in."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Not to the pastor, but to the Lord."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have been in so many situations that I could not depend on anyone else to help me out of."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"One particular occasion was getting in a car accident. I was stopped at a stop sign waiting for a car to go across (they did not have a stop), and the person decided that he wanted to turn at the last minute and just cranked the wheel right into us completely bashing us off the road and destroying our car. The first thing I did when I got out of the car was text my prayer group. I needed the prayers first and foremost. Then I called the police. Honestly, I don't know why my prayer group came to mind first. They just did, and they started praying right away. They were more helpful than the police were. The police were trying to figure out who was in the wrong even after I explained to them what had taken place. It was also obvious that the other car hit us because of where our car ended up. The police were not men of God and it was evident."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I'm thankful for people who take the chance to pray for me. I need the prayers. We all do."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says that the prayers of the Corinthian church were part of the reason that they were able to escape with their lives from Ephesus. They were literally \"helping\" Paul and his men as they were getting out of there."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They didn't have to physically be there to help, they just needed to pray. That's the great power of prayer."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Rejoicing Testimony","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's break this down so we don't get confused (because it's a bit of a mouthful that Paul says here). He has a rejoicing. The rejoicing is what Paul considers to be \"the testimony of our conscience...\""},{"kind":"thought","text":"I could only hope that rejoicing is our testimony."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what will ultimately give us a good conscience in this life. We will know we are doing the right thing when we have rejoicing in doing it (even if we are in the middle of attack for it)."},{"kind":"thought","text":"But they rejoice from \"simplicity\" and \"godly sincerity.\" It's not complicated, and it's not based on \"fleshly wisdom.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul and his band could have done things through their own understanding. They could have drawn swords and fought their way out in their own strength, but they see that God led them out a different way. Through HIS power, rather than their own. God's grace has given them life. It has given them continuance to be an example, to everyone (and especially to the Corinthian church). That is their rejoicing. They rejoice because they get to continue to be an example of what is good, holy, and righteous."}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When Paul came to them the first time, he did it with rejoicing. They may have made things a little difficult, but he was able to give them truth and direction, and it seems to have been a benefit to them. He wrote his first letter as a means of victory. That is a reason for them to rejoice. He has given them necessity to pursue liberty, and righteousness for the sake of their testimony and for the salvation of others around them. Paul gave them rejoicing news. That is what he is giving them this time around as well. Perhaps in a different way, but it's rejoicing nevertheless."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":22,"title":"Promises Kept","entries":[{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul really wanted to meet with this church and see how they were doing. He kept getting stopped, however, and so he had to send his trusted ministers to them and bring word again to him. There were people there that were claiming that Paul was a liar and a hypocrite for not coming to visit. In all honesty, it probably certainly seemed that way because Paul was saying that he'd come to visit and then never actually did it. That was not Paul's intention and he was willing to take the fall for it. He would certainly take the responsibility for that, but it was not because he was lying to them or deceiving them. He was constantly in battle with the religious sects of those lands, and they were always trying to put a stop to the word of God."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That is how Satan works, by the way. Satan will do whatever it takes to make sure God's Word is rejected and ignored. Satan doesn't ever win, but he really tries."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was not making light of his desire to come visit them. He was not just putting it off because he was too concerned about other churches or other things. He was sincere when he wrote to them, and he was sincere when he said he wanted to come see them. He meant what he said, but there was always some sort of evil force preventing him. The Lord, however, was also at work, and that is evident by the fact that the Corinthian church took Paul's letter seriously the first time. They needed to hang on to the fact that Paul is making mention of it here. He truly wants to see them and strengthen them. He truly wants to come back to them if there is any way possible to do it. That is what he will do one way or another."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Sadly, there are a lot of ministers who make light of their promises. They promise something and then they just let it go as if it doesn't really matter. Ministers should not be in that game. They should not be toying with promises, emotions, or ministry like that. They should say what they mean, and mean what they say. Otherwise, they shouldn't say anything."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a topic we will talk quite a lot about in this letter."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He wasn't going back on what he said. He knew where the Lord was going to lead, and the desires that the Spirit put on his heart, and it was all from the workings of the Lord, but the workings of this world were hindering him. He never said that he wasn’t going to come. He has made every effort and intention to go their way. That is still his desire even while writing this letter from Philippi. Things just had to be taken care of first."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That's another thing a minister should not be in the business of doing. They should not be telling you something that they are going to do, then turn around and tell you that they are not going to do it anymore. There are a lot of ministers that do this sadly."},{"kind":"para","text":"They make promises about something and they change their minds at the last minute, or they just completely forget about the promise they made. That is a sign of a vain minister. They are not cautious about their words, and they are not concerned about keeping promises. They might mean well at the first, but if they don't keep their promises they aren't going to gain support or comfort from those that they made the promises to. Don't make a guarantee or promise that you are not able to keep. If there is the slightest of a chance that you can't keep the promise, then it's best you don't make the promise at all."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay: for whatsoever is more than these cometh of evil.","ref":"Matthew 5:37"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The preaching of Christ was affirmative. This is why Paul can confidently call himself an Apostle at the beginning of this letter. It was affirmative. There was no taking back Christ's gospel."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There was no \"Oh, Christ has changed his mind about saving the world.\" No. It was set in stone, and it was a message that can be accounted for forever."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A preacher should be affirmative in his promises and affirmative in his preaching. If he isn't in one or the other, then you can't trust either one."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a good verse! God is affirmative. Period."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"When He says something, it is \"yea\" and \"Amen.\" It is official and finished. It is promised and guaranteed. It is directed and completed. God's affirmations are permanent and eternal."},{"kind":"para","text":"We may not make eternal promises, but we certainly can keep the simple worldly promises we make (especially if they are made in the Lord). Everything in Christ is \"yes.\" It is affirmative. It is promised, and it will continue to be promised for all of eternity."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Christ's promises are always kept promises."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ is the promise keeper, He is the promise maker, and it is because He is trustworthy to keep all promises that we are able to stand affirmative in promises on Earth. If we make promises, may it only be in the will and directive of the Lord. He will establish promises, keep promises, and anoint us for those promises. The greatest promise of all to the believer is the promise of salvation to any and all that would believe. That promise is kept. I know (personally) that the promise is kept because (against my sin) I am still saved today. It's a promise that Christ made, and it's a promise that Christ will keep no matter how terribly sinful and rejectful I may become. He has \"sealed\" us in His promises, and they are guaranteed to be kept. The Spirit is the evidence of the promise that is kept."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In whom ye also trusted, after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation: in whom also after that ye believed, ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise,","ref":"Ephesians 1:13"}]}]},{"start":23,"end":24,"title":"Building Joy","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the first epistle to this church, Paul didn't have the most friendly things to say. He was very direct. If he had come in person the first time and said these things, it might not have left the church in good spirits. They probably wouldn't have wanted Paul to return after that."},{"kind":"thought","text":"When you are writing things, it makes it a little easier to do it with grace because you can change your words before you release them to whom you are writing. If you are speaking to someone face to face, it is a little harder to correct yourself. Perhaps that is part of the reason that the Lord kept Paul away for that time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now Paul can come to this church with some hope, charity, grace, and peace because they were on the right track following after the Lord and ridding the sin that was plaguing the church."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another great verse. We cannot control the faith of others."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We can control our own promises and what promises we might keep, but we cannot control the confidence of those people whom we make promises to. We can only act upon our own faith and promises as a means of confidence and assurance."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If we keep the promises we make, it brings \"joy.\" We literally help people have joy when we keep promises. That is something we should all be desiring to give people."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are not able to keep promises, we are going to do the opposite of produce joy. We will rob people of joy. That is not a minister’s stance. Kept promises produce joy (it shows the person you care), and it helps the person the promise was made to stand a little stronger in their faith."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If you want to build a man’s joy and faith, then it would be wise of you to keep your promises."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If we are not able to keep promises, we are going to do the opposite of producing joy. We will rob people of joy."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy our brother, unto the church of God which is at Corinth, with all the saints which are in all Achaia:","2":"Grace be to you and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.","3":"Blessed be God, even the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies, and the God of all comfort;","4":"Who comforteth us in all our tribulation, that we may be able to comfort them which are in any trouble, by the comfort wherewith we ourselves are comforted of God.","5":"For as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so our consolation also aboundeth by Christ.","6":"And whether we be afflicted, it is for your consolation and salvation, which is effectual in the enduring of the same sufferings which we also suffer: or whether we be comforted, it is for your consolation and salvation.","7":"And our hope of you is stedfast, knowing, that as ye are partakers of the sufferings, so shall ye be also of the consolation.","8":"For we would not, brethren, have you ignorant of our trouble which came to us in Asia, that we were pressed out of measure, above strength, insomuch that we despaired even of life:","9":"But we had the sentence of death in ourselves, that we should not trust in ourselves, but in God which raiseth the dead:","10":"Who delivered us from so great a death, and doth deliver: in whom we trust that he will yet deliver us;","11":"Ye also helping together by prayer for us, that for the gift bestowed upon us by the means of many persons thanks may be given by many on our behalf.","12":"For our rejoicing is this, the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity and godly sincerity, not with fleshly wisdom, but by the grace of God, we have had our conversation in the world, and more abundantly to you-ward.","13":"For we write none other things unto you, that what ye read or acknowledge; and I trust ye shall acknowledge even to the end;","14":"As also ye have acknowledged us in part, that we are your rejoicing, even as ye also are our's in the day of the Lord Jesus.","15":"And in this confidence I was minded to come unto you before, that ye might have a second benefit;","16":"And to pass by you into Macedonia, and to come again out of Macedonia unto you, and of you to be brought on my way toward Judaea.","17":"When I therefore was thus minded, did I use lightness? or the things that I purpose, do I purpose according to the flesh, that with me there should be yea yea, and nay nay?","18":"But as God is true, our word toward you was not yea and nay.","19":"For the Son of God, Jesus Christ, who was preached among you by us, even by me and Silvanus and Timotheus, was not yea and nay, but in him was yea.","20":"For all the promises of God in him are yea, and in him Amen, unto the glory of God by us.","21":"Now he which stablisheth us with you in Christ, and hath anointed us, is God;","22":"Who hath also sealed us, and given the earnest of the Spirit in our hearts.","23":"Moreover I call God for a record upon my soul, that to spare you I came not as yet unto Corinth.","24":"Not for that we have dominion over your faith, but are helpers of your joy: for by faith ye stand."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul opens this letter up about the importance of keeping promises. This church (at the time) felt kind of forgotten by Paul. Paul had every intention to come visit this church, but something kept stopping and hindering him from coming in time. They were not forgotten though, and Paul sent some of his most trusted ministers to keep them up to date and deliver his letters to them. He has made it clear that (as a minister) he loves this church."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he continues to make it clear by explaining to them that he has wept for them over both their sin and the desire to come and see them. He is asking that the church learn something important about forgiveness now, and that they would forgive their enemies, as well as his lack of opportunity to come and see them."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul opens this letter up about the importance of keeping promises. This church (at the time) felt kind of forgotten by Paul. Paul had every intention to come visit this church, but something kept stopping and hindering him from coming in time. They were not forgotten though, and Paul sent some of his most trusted ministers to keep them up to date and deliver his letters to them. He has made it clear that (as a minister) he loves this church. In this chapter, he continues to make it clear by explaining to them that he has wept for them over both their sin and the desire to come and see them. He is asking that the church learn something important about forgiveness now, and that they would forgive their enemies, as well as his lack of opportunity to come and see them."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Weeping For Them","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So I know I said that the chapters don't really match the same themes. It seems as if each chapter has its own topic. I believe that to still be the case even though this chapter is leading off of our last."},{"kind":"thought","text":"As I've mentioned before, Paul didn't want to come to this church with burdens and scorching affliction. He wanted to come to this church with joy and pleasure."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church was not on the right track until Paul's first letter to them, and so now he has a positive reason to come visit them. Even if he wanted to keep his promise to visit them long ago, he wouldn't want to do it out of the idea that he has to discipline them and correct them. He wanted to come with news of encouragement and strengthening."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's nothing worse than coming to a person (or people) with nothing but bad news."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"Lately, our church has been experiencing some \"growth pains.\" We are growing at a rapid rate, and there are a lot of people who have been there for a while, being thrown into the background. I have felt the pains of it as well as my family. I (however) know how to fix it. I have been praying and planning for the better part of three months now so I can have a fix for the situation rather than just coming to my pastor with all my hurts and discomfort. I wanted to come to him with joy and a fix. I am hopeful that I don't have to come to him with weeping and pain because that will not be profitable for either of us. If I am pouring out my hurts and discomfort to him without a fix and forward momentum of joy, then I will not receive joy and encouragement in return. I know that to be true in all cases when it comes to counseling and equipping the saints."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We must be ready to give joy to those that we minister to. That is hard to do when the people you are planning to minister to are not producing joy."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church is producing joy now, but it wasn't when Paul wrote the first letter. Thankfully, God hindered Paul from being able to visit them; otherwise, it would just be pain poured upon pain for Paul."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"This is going to sound strange, but it's the truth: Ministers experience joy when others are following the Lord. We find more joy in the salvation and growth of others than we do in our own walks of faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we see someone changing and growing for the Lord, it is a blessing to us to witness (even if we are not the ones responsible for the growth). To be involved with it is all we should want."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers that don't want to be involved with the faith growth of others are not out to minister to others. They are out to minister for themselves. They are only focused on themselves. We should want to see others grow before we desire to see ourselves grow."},{"kind":"para","text":"That includes in our own family. The desire for our families to grow in the faith should be top priority in our lives."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth.","ref":"3 John 1:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"That's the greatest joy a minister of God can ever receive."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul cried for this church when he wrote to them the first time. He cried because he was burdened for them. That is how much they meant to him. He was in literal anguish over their sin and waywardness. He cared about them like a father cares about a child. He wanted them to flourish and prosper, and they were not able to do that because of the amount of sin they were allowing to throw their doors and into their own hearts."},{"kind":"para","text":"This goes to further prove that the promise that Paul made was not just a superficial one. He truly meant the promise. He cried over their troubles. He cried to see them. He was in genuine prayer and reliance on the Lord to give this church mercy and grace because he wanted the Lord to see it flourish. That should be the heart of every minister. It is not, however."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Most ministers today don't seem to really care if people are even attending the church anymore. As long as they meet their numbers quota, it doesn't matter who shows up, just how many. What a failed and flawed culture we are creating."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"# Ver. 5-11: Forgiving Spirits"}]}]},{"start":5,"end":11,"title":"Forgiving Spirits","entries":[{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So when Paul wrote to them the first time, he wrote to them about a few problems they were facing. There was some in the church that was trying to incorporate sin into their faith, and that was not going to work for the Lord. It wouldn't work for them either as they would have to reject the Word of truth in order to follow their fleshly lusts. One particular group was trying to entice the Corinthians into fornication (specifically with prostitution). That is something we studied in depth in the last letter. It seems that they took the letter of Paul seriously and kicked that group out of the church. They were wise to do that, and they needed to separate the problem so they don't grow deeper in it."},{"kind":"para","text":"But Paul is saying now that they shouldn't end it there."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul is strong enough (according to this verse) to not be grieved by the bad seeds of the church swaying people away from the truth. He knows what is truth, and that will not stop him from following after it even if the whole church were against it."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is one thing that is grieving him though. He talks about that in the next verse:"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people (or person) they kicked out needs to be forgiven. Paul is kind of putting himself in the person’s shoes. If he was kicked out of the church, he would want to be forgiven if he was desirous to repent and turn from his sins. To be completely shunned and never forgiven again would be a grievance to the person, and possibly to the people that he is around."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Unforgiveness could be a hindrance to the salvation of others."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's a rule of thumb to live by. Their sorrow can be too much for them to bear."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Showing love to sinful people is what this world needs."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a hard thing for many to do. I had heard one particular preacher state that when you find a fault with someone in the church (or a sin that they are experiencing), you are liable to kick them out and keep them out of the church. He said that scripture tells us to do that (and he quoted 1 Corinthians for it). There is a responsibility to the leadership to take the proper steps and direction to seeing a person’s sin in the church. If they are struggling with a sin, it is best to address that with that person before having them serve in something in the church. If they are not willing to work on that, then perhaps it’s best to keep them from service for a time. However, we do not get to just kick them out of the church."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The only reason kicking someone out would be mandatory is if they are enticing other Christians to follow them into that same sin. If they are starting a rebellion or causing people to stumble (like was addressed in the last letter), then it is wise to separate them from the church so that people don't fall for it. However, after some time, we should be willing to love them and forgive them so that they know they are accepted into the church if they are willing to change and repent from their sins."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Christians are to be the most forgiving people on the planet. If Christians are not forgiving, it is either because others have done something dangerously serious, or because those Christians are not living right before the Lord."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This will test the church’s affirmation. If they were genuine about their following of the Lord, and about the correction that Paul was giving them, then they would have no problem listening to Paul's guidance to forgive these people."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The purpose of this letter (at least the reason that Paul originally wanted to write the letter) was to let them know that they needed to be in a mode of forgiveness. He wanted to see how much they were willing to stretch their faith in all things."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a biblical command to forgive, by the way. I know there are preachers who think we must be strict on our convictions, and not willing to waver at all costs, but that does not mean we should leave no room to forgive anyone and everyone when the time calls for it. We should be in a constant mode of forgiveness."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Even if there is nothing to forgive, we should be ready with forgiveness at all times."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when ye stand praying, forgive, if ye have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.\" Mark 11:25 \"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you.\" Ephesians 4:32 \"Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye.","ref":"Colossians 3:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when ye stand praying, forgive, if ye have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.","ref":"Mark 11:25"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you.","ref":"Ephesians 4:32"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye.","ref":"Colossians 3:13"}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we are not willing to forgive, then Satan will always find a foothold to use it and increase bitterness. I've seen bitterness root in people who are not willing to forgive. It is an obvious sign of unforgiveness."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If there is bitterness in a person's life, you can guarantee it is because they have not forgiven someone (even if that someone is themselves). Satan loves dwelling in unforgiven relationships."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever he can do to drive a wedge with others is something he will work at non-stop."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"# Ver. 12-14: A Preaching Opportunity"}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"A Preaching Opportunity","entries":[{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Paul originally sent Titus to go speak to this church in Corinth. He was expecting Titus to come back and tell him the news of Corinth, but was sent out of Ephesus to Troas for a time. He took that opportunity to preach the gospel to these people, and expected Titus to find his way to him there. Titus never came, though. So he was concerned about it, but needed to move on to Macedonia until word came back to him about this church at Corinth. He wanted to come to them with a ready spirit, but they were seemingly not ready yet."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When Paul went to Macedonia, it was another working of the Spirit's moving. It was obvious to Paul that it was the Lord that led him there to preach, and it was a ministry that really encouraged him. He was able to preach the gospel, and the people were able to receive the gospel with readiness. That is (once again) the most joyful thing a minister can experience."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I would love to preach to crowds who are ripe for the gospel message. Most crowds today have become calloused to the gospel, and so they reject it before I can even open my mouth to say anything. They are quite miserable people."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"# Ver. 15-17: Keeping Truth"}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"Keeping Truth","entries":[{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Preachers are a sweet odor to those that take Christ."},{"kind":"thought","text":"To those that reject Christ, they are just passing from spiritual death to physical (and eternal) death. But those that accept Christ (that is, the gospel message of Christ), they are passing from physical life to spiritual (that is, eternal) life."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Those that accept Christ gain life. Those that reject Christ don't gain anything."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life.","ref":"1 John 5:12"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Preachers are not in the business of twisting scripture."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If you are a preacher that twists scripture (that is, to make it make sense to your own desires or ideas), you are not a preacher of truth. You are the devil's preacher. You are preaching lies and using the truth to make it sound like God's Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of people who have this kind of preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"We live in an era where we have to be more cautious than ever to keep the truth front and center and to make sure that we are not led astray by the lies of this world."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are a lot of lies being spread from \"spiritual\" people. Be very cautious. We should be taking God's Word and being as sincere in speaking the truth as possible, by the working of God's Spirit, and as if God is watching every single thing we do and say."},{"kind":"para","text":"For He is watching. He is paying attention to your words and your preaching."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, That every idle word that men shall speak, they shall give account thereof in the day of judgment.","ref":"Matthew 12:36"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17}],"verses":{"1":"But I determined this with myself, that I would not come again to you in heaviness.","2":"For if I make you sorry, who is he then that maketh me glad, but the same which is made sorry by me?","3":"And I wrote this same unto you, lest, when I came, I should have sorrow from them of whom I ought to rejoice; having confidence in you all, that my joy is the joy of you all.","4":"For out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote unto you with many tears; not that ye should be grieved, but that ye might know the love which I have more abundantly unto you.","5":"But if any have caused grief, he hath not grieved me, but in part: that I may not overcharge you all.","6":"Sufficient to such a man is this punishment, which was inflicted of many.","7":"So that contrariwise ye ought rather to forgive him, and comfort him, lest perhaps such a one should be swallowed up with overmuch sorrow.","8":"Wherefore I beseech you that ye would confirm your love toward him.","9":"For to this end also did I write, that I might know the proof of you, whether ye be obedient in all things.","10":"To whom ye forgive any thing, I forgive also: for if I forgave any thing, to whom I forgave it, for your sakes forgave I it in the person of Christ;","11":"Lest Satan should get an advantage of us: for we are not ignorant of his devices.","12":"Furthermore, when I came to Troas to preach Christ's gospel, and a door was opened unto me of the Lord,","13":"I had no rest in my spirit, because I found not Titus my brother: but taking my leave of them, I went from thence into Macedonia.","14":"Now thanks be unto God, which always causeth us to triumph in Christ, and maketh manifest the savour of his knowledge by us in every place.","15":"For we are unto God a sweet savour of Christ, in them that are saved, and in them that perish:","16":"To the one we are the savour of death unto death; and to the other the savour of life unto life. And who is sufficient for these things?","17":"For we are not as many, which corrupt the word of God: but as of sincerity, but as of God, in the sight of God speak we in Christ."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, the theme was placed on forgiving wicked people. It was about trying to be peaceable. Paul wants the church to know how to separate the wicked, but he also wants them to know how to forgive those that need it. Churches should be very in tune to knowing how to correct sin and how to forgive sin. I don't feel the church is very good at it even still today."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul talks about the importance of being an example in our ministry and walks. We are always being watched, and people are always going to pick apart what you are (or are not) doing for the Lord. People will always be looking for the Christian (especially) to slip up and fall."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, the theme was placed on forgiving wicked people. It was about trying to be peaceable. Paul wants the church to know how to separate the wicked, but he also wants them to know how to forgive those that need it. Churches should be very in tune to knowing how to correct sin and how to forgive sin. I don't feel the church is very good at it even still today. In this chapter, Paul talks about the importance of being an example in our ministry and walks. We are always being watched, and people are always going to pick apart what you are (or are not) doing for the Lord. People will always be looking for the Christian (especially) to slip up and fall."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Walking Epistles","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"Who do YOU need approval from? I've fallen for the same dumb traps as many others in the ministry. It seems like we constantly need approval from specific people who are higher than us."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the Catholic Church, they need approval from their priests. The priests need approval from the bishops. The bishops need approval from the Pope. Mormons need approval from their elders. The elders need approval from their deacons. The deacons need approval from their church leader. In our church, we go to our pastor for approval."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is asking a fair question here. Should we approve ourselves? Would it be better if the church approves us? How do we go through the ministry? Are we the best judge of our own character, or does the church make that decision for us? I'd say... Neither."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are neither the best judge of our own character, AND the church is not a good judge of us. We must know the Lord's mind. We must follow after the Lord's actions. We must search the Will of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"We can search what God has for OUR lives (that is completely fine)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know the Lord has placed me in ministry. I know He has made me a teacher and leader (though I don't understand WHY He has). But what is God's will alone? What would God want? I've spent a lot of time explaining this through separate parts of Scripture, and perhaps we won't fully understand it until Revelation, but God ultimately wants (and is planning everything out) to spend time with us. He wants to develop us. He wants to move US forward. He cares very deeply about us. I don't understand why, but that is what He wants. He wants us to be in His presence. I have nothing against getting encouragement from leaders of the Church (I want to get that straight)."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"When I was in ministry, I would get a lot of flyers and cards from local businesses about job fairs and seasonal hiring. I never really had to say much. One time I was at Taco Bell and the lady said, \"You know any teens that might be looking for a summer job?\" I said, \"Well, it just so happens that I do. I have a classroom full of them.\" She handed me a bunch of fliers to take to my class. I had them in a pile at the front and said, \"These are available to you if you would like to work at Taco Bell. If there is any job you want, let me know (Taco Bell or not) and I can write a letter of recommendation for you.\" I knew how difficult it was to get your first job these days. It was a better thing for me to write these places a letter of recommendation. They would take that as a help in their hiring process (these teens were serving everywhere in the church)."},{"kind":"para","text":"But it's not PEOPLE that gets you into the work of the Lord. It's not YOURSELF that keeps you going. It can only be the Lord's calling. It can only be approved of by God alone. Take it from someone who has rejected ministry with every ounce of their being. I am still in it, and it was not by my own approval, or that of anyone else. It was not as if God was sending a letter down to prove my following. Rather, He made it clear through my lifestyle and actions!"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Your walk is proof of Christ's ministry. Your life will speak louder than your words."},{"kind":"para","text":"People can have a great sermon on Sundays, and a terrible lifestyle throughout the rest of the week."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Don't preach a message about unity if you plan on never using that message in your personal life. Don't preach on loving one another and then never showing that in any other fashion. Don't preach on soulwinning if you are not going soulwinning. Don't tell others to do something that YOU are not either already doing yourself, or are willing to learn to do yourself."},{"kind":"thought","text":"You want to know how I can tell the difference between a genuine minister of God and a fake one? One will be doing His calling whether anyone else recognized it or not. The other will be doing His calling only to be recognized."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have to have everyone watch you while you do what God has called you to do, you are just doing the work for people, not for the Lord. It is pretty rare that the church would ever really pay attention to what you are doing anyway. They don't really care. It's the unbeliever that takes better notice of your actions (or lack thereof) than it is the believer. That's just the sad reality of ministry. They can tell what you are really after."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Believers usually just critique your methods, while unbelievers usually just watch your consistency. Both are usually waiting for you to mess up."},{"kind":"para","text":"I will just tell you now, there is a strong chance you will see me mess up a lot in life and ministry. You probably won't have to wait too long, honestly. I am a struggling person. Yet, God has chosen me to be an example. He has chosen me to lead other people to become leaders. Chosen me to help people study and read their Bibles. Given ME (of all people) knowledge to understand His Word and then help others understand it as well. I, personally, think He chose the wrong guy. But God knows who He chose."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are a written letter from God to the world."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If we are written with \"ink\" or “stone,\" we would just have a \"do-as-I-Tell-You\" type of mentality. Instead, we are written with the \"Spirit\" and \"heart\" and must have a \"Do-as-I-Do\" type of mentality. Our lives should be walking examples. People will read you more than they read their Bibles."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"You are a representative of God's Word in action. You are not to just preach it. You are not to just read it. You are not to just pray about it. You are to DO it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves. For if any be a hearer of the word, and not a doer, he is like unto a man beholding his natural face in a glass: For he beholdeth himself, and goeth his way, and straightway forgetteth what manner of man he was. But whoso looketh into the perfect law of liberty, and continueth therein, he being not a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work, this man shall be blessed in his deed.","ref":"James 1:22-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of consumer Christians in the church today. They want to come in, sit in the back, listen to the message, and then leave as soon as it's done. That is not how a minister should be. A minister of God's Word should be someone that takes action. Someone that sticks around. A minister of the church should be someone helping others fit into the ministry. They should be people who are continuously encouraging those that are trying to find their place in life and ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are far too many ministry leaders today who are only sticking to themselves and their small group of influencers. It seems like pastors only surround themselves with \"yes\" people. People that will just bend to the leadership of the pastor, and not the leadership of the Lord. We should want people around us that think outside the box. That give us new ideas to work on. That are like-minded (sure), but also willing to take chances for the Lord. People that want to add something new to ministry. We will never have that if we are always the ones that control the ministry. We will never have walking epistles of the Lord, if WE are the ones writing the letters on the hearts of people. We must allow the LORD to do the work, rather than robbing Him of it."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"Life Giving Spirit","entries":[{"start":4,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Who is it that has commissioned you?"},{"kind":"conversation","text":"When I first had the calling for ministry, I had no idea what that would entail, or how that would actually become a reality. I just knew that God called me, and I knew I had to do something about it. So I waited on the Lord, and ultimately it brought plenty of openings for me. There was no mystical letter that fell from the sky, no obvious signs that said, \"Go this way.\" No, it was just one thing leading to another to another and I had no choice but to follow. I told about three different mentors about what God was doing in my life, and spoke to my pastor about it. All four of these people told me I was in over my head, and I needed to take college classes before going into it. Looking back now, I still don't know why they recommended that. I knew where the Lord was leading me, and college courses could never prepare me for what I was about to experience. There is no college course that would tell me how to deal with a rape victim. No college course could teach me how to speak to teens who have been stealing from their job. No college course could help me give counsel to a teen who wanted to join the military. No college course about how to lead a hurting church through music. No college course could teach me how to preach and love people at a retirement home. No college course about how to make connections to my local public school. No college course could tell me how to live my ministry out."},{"kind":"para","text":"College is great. I don't want to reject it for those that don't know where the Lord would have them. But your commission comes from God, ultimately."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"College will not teach you boldness in soulwinning, compassion for the lost and your brothers in Christ, or passion for the preaching of God's Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, many colleges teach you how to preach, but they don't teach you how to fix your heart. They can't teach you that. That is something the Lord has to work on in you. Give me a preacher that loves people, not one that is just good with words."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I'd take a preacher who mumbles or stutters but has a loving heart and spirit for others over a preacher that is eccentric and ignores his congregation any day. I want a Pastor who works alongside me, not one that just tells me what to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"If your pastor is not like this, then YOU should take it upon yourself to lead people like this. Otherwise, we are robbing people of life. \"The letter killeth...\" Just to tell people what to do and not actually go alongside them preaches death. \"... the spirit giveth life.\" We should live our actions, and it will provide life to others."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":11,"title":"The Ministration of the Spirit","entries":[{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"The ministration of death is the works of the law."},{"kind":"para","text":"That should be well understood at this point, I think."},{"kind":"thought","text":"All that Paul ever talks about is unhitching from the works of the law to live in the works of righteousness (or the Spirit, rather)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The ministration of death is still given in glory. This was a symbol of glory. Imagine a world that lived solemnly by the law of God. Every person on the planet was so in-tuned with God's law that they didn't have to even think about it to follow it; they just did what was right, all the time, and there was no sin! What a glorious world it would be."},{"kind":"thought","text":"However, it is referenced as \"the ministration of death\" because we are not capable of living lives that way. We always fail, and it always leads to death. Work as we might, all the law does is show us how wicked and sinful we are, and sin always leads us to death."},{"kind":"para","text":"So we must have the ministration of the Spirit."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If we are going to minister, we can either minister to death, which is what a good portion of religions do today, or we can minister to Spirit, which is what this world needs if we are going to be fruitful and wise."},{"kind":"para","text":"Minister by the law, or minister by faith. Those are your two choices. Those were your two choices the moment Jesus came back from the grave. You can either hold onto your traditions and laws, or you can let go of them and hold onto Christ. You cannot do both, though. You must choose. That is a warning to every reader through every chapter."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Choose your works, or trust the finished work of Christ."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Christ's finished work EXCEEDS glory."},{"kind":"para","text":"We might glory from our own works. We might feel good about the good we do. But it does not even come close to the works of Christ. Does this mean we should no longer work because Christ has finished it on the cross? No, that's not what I'm saying."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We work because of Christ, not FOR Him. We don't work to receive Him, we work because we are already received by Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is best looked at in the epistle of James."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The law is complete today. Don't let any other church or religion tell you otherwise."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Religions will tell you that there is still work to be done, but there isn't. Either Jesus finished the work, or He was not successful."},{"kind":"para","text":"If He was not successful, then there is still work to be done. If there is still work to be done for the world’s salvation, we will always be doomed for an eternity in Hell."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The promise of salvation was made, the prophets of the Old Testament believed it, taught it, and lived in it. The keeping of that promise has already come to pass, and the saints of the New Testament believed it, taught it, and lived in it."},{"kind":"para","text":"We get to do the same. That is what is being taught by Paul in every letter. That is also what the other Apostles teach, and every gospel account teaches it as well."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":16,"title":"Veil The Heart","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"conversation","text":"I'm referred to as a \"simple\" man. It's because I don't use any sort of difficult words or speech. When I speak, I speak like a dumb child. Like I don't know very much. The truth is, I really DON'T know very much. People call me stupid all the time. I grew up with that word being thrown at me even from my own family. They are right. Yet it's this stupid, dumb, simple, child that the Lord seems to use. How does He use me? Well, He uses the simplicity of my speech. God loves that. He likes things to be easy to understand."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are plenty of complex preachers out there, and all they do is muddy up the simplicity of the gospel. Really, what they are doing is turning people away from the truth because they want to sound sophisticated."},{"kind":"para","text":"All they do is make themselves confusing. People turn a deaf ear to that. The hope of the gospel is so rich, and so good, but we tend to rob the richness of it by trying to expound or over-explain it. We need to make it real simple. The simpler, the better in my opinion."}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When Moses went up the mount to receive the law, he came down with a shining bright light upon his face. He had the presence and power of God radiating from him. Jesus had the same thing happen to Him when He was in the Lord's presence at the mount of transfiguration. I believe it's the same thing when He rose from the dead again, but that's a story for a different time. When Moses started to lose that holy glow, he started to fear that the people wouldn't respect him like they did before, so he covered his face so people thought that the spirit of God was still upon him. He stayed like that for a while until people started to point it out. Of course, he couldn't hide that the power and presence of God wasn't forever on him. Just like the law of God, it could not be held forever. It was there at the beginning, but our old sinful flesh makes it vanish away."},{"kind":"para","text":"God's glory disappears in this world. It does not (however) disappear from a man whose Spirit is set on the Lord."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"When we have the Lord, we have His glory shining in our lives forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a wonderful thing. We never have to cover our faces again because we can have the glow of the Lord shining through our actions. Let's not forget, that is what Paul is getting at. The Spirit of the Lord shines from us because we have the presence of the Lord. He is with every child that accepts the gift of salvation. We are all in the ministry then."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Again, we have two ministries to choose from. The ministry of death (we can call it the “unministry”), or the ministry of the Spirit (that can just be the “ministry”)."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"The religionists keep their veils on. They hide their faces and their hearts."},{"kind":"para","text":"We read in the last letter that there were certain women that had to cover their heads (the prostitutes would shave their heads, and so they would stick out like prostitutes in the church as well when they were supposed to be called away from that work)."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are many religions today who still cover their heads from traditional standpoints. They are wearing a veil to cover their heads. They do not want their heads to exemplify Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"People put the veil of their religion and traditions over their lives so that they don't have to fully trust in the Lord alone."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The only way we can accept Christ is to accept Him without the veil."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why? Well, that is what Christ did at His death. He tore the veil."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom; and the earth did quake, and the rocks rent;","ref":"Matthew 27:51"},{"kind":"para","text":"This symbolizes access to salvation. It symbolizes full free entrance to God's holy salvation and presence. We have full access because the veil is torn."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The heart will always be covered. It will be covered by the veil of sin. That is what the veil is. It's an entrance into the holiest part of worship. But in order for you to enter there, you must cleanse yourself. No matter how hard the priests tried to cleanse themselves, they always continuously failed. But Jesus... He didn't fail. He died for the sins of the whole world, and anyone that comes to Him in faith has full access to the throne of mercy. They can come before Him and worship WITHOUT a veil separating them. That is a great promise from the Lord, and it's a great reminder from Paul here."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The veil of our heart must be taken away if we are going to trust in the Lord. We must allow Him full access if we are going to turn to Him, otherwise we will always be stuck and separated from Him."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"title":"Our Liberty","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes it feels as if we have no freedom here in America. It feels like our freedom is falling apart. We have no liberties. But the government does not dictate a man's liberty. It is not the leaders of this world that can give you freedom. It is only the Spirit of the Lord that can do that. When we have His Spirit, we have liberty."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a large organization (several in fact) that believes they are oppressed. They tout a message of oppression. You know why they are in bondage? It's not because the white political evangelist elite has robbed them of their liberty. No. It's because they don't have the Spirit of God. If you want to argue with that logic, you will have to argue with Paul here. I'm just stating facts. If you feel like you are not free, it might be because you do not follow the Spirit of the Lord. Even in prison, you can be free with God's Spirit."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It feels like our freedom is falling apart. We have no liberties. But the government does not dictate a man's liberty. It is not the leaders of this world that can give you freedom. It is only the Spirit of the Lord that can do that. When we have His Spirit, we have liberty. There is a large organization (several in fact) that believes they are oppressed. They tout a message of oppression. You know why they are in bondage? It's not because the white political evangelist elite has robbed them of their liberty. No. It's because they don't have the Spirit of God."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we have God's Spirit, we begin to change. We can look into the mirror of our souls and notice, absolutely, that there is something different. Things that we used to enjoy suddenly become a drag to us. We find no reason or happiness in them. When we truly experience the Spirit of God in our lives, nothing else will ever satisfy us. We will only be satisfied with being present with God. The change will be noticeable. It might not be to everyone else, but you will know the presence of God when you experience it. You will know the lack of it too when you are away from Him. God's presence gives us so much more fulfillment than anything else this world can offer."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"I really enjoy playing music. Music has been a big part of my life since I was very little. My dad was really into music, and my brother liked to write music. When I became a teenager, I started to write music. I would play live shows, and I was very involved with the music scene. Recording in studios, releasing new songs, experimenting with different rhymes. There was something very fulfilling about music to me. The presence of the Lord has been far more fulfilling than that even. I could never play music again and still be extremely blessed and happy if all I have is God's presence."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18}],"verses":{"1":"Do we begin again to commend ourselves? or need we, as some others, epistles of commendation to you, or letters of commendation from you?","2":"Ye are our epistle written in our hearts, known and read of all men:","3":"Forasmuch as ye are manifestly declared to be the epistle of Christ ministered by us, written not with ink, but with the Spirit of the living God; not in tables of stone, but in fleshy tables of the heart.","4":"And such trust have we through Christ to God-ward:","5":"Not that we are sufficient of ourselves to think any thing as of ourselves; but our sufficiency is of God;","6":"Who also hath made us able ministers of the new testament; not of the letter, but of the spirit: for the letter killeth, but the spirit giveth life.","7":"But if the ministration of death, written and engraven in stones, was glorious, so that the children of Israel could not stedfastly behold the face of Moses for the glory of his countenance; which glory was to be done away:","8":"How shall not the ministration of the spirit be rather glorious?","9":"For if the ministration of condemnation be glory, much more doth the ministration of righteousness exceed in glory.","10":"For even that which was made glorious had no glory in this respect, by reason of the glory that excelleth.","11":"For if that which is done away was glorious, much more that which remaineth is glorious.","12":"Seeing then that we have such hope, we use great plainness of speech:","13":"And not as Moses, which put a veil over his face, that the children of Israel could not stedfastly look to the end of that which is abolished:","14":"But their minds were blinded: for until this day remaineth the same vail untaken away in the reading of the old testament; which vail is done away in Christ.","15":"But even unto this day, when Moses is read, the vail is upon their heart.","16":"Nevertheless when it shall turn to the Lord, the vail shall be taken away.","17":"Now the Lord is that Spirit: and where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.","18":"But we all, with open face beholding as in a glass the glory of the Lord, are changed into the same image from glory to glory, even as by the Spirit of the Lord."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul uses the example of the believer as the important picture of truth. People are going to read you more than they read their Bibles. He talks about the Spirit providing life in the believer, the ministry that the Spirit provides for those that are exercised in the faith, the necessity to uncover your heart’s veil, and keeping from abusing our liberty for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"He continues in this chapter by talking about taking your liberties and pursuing mercy and ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul will give you ample reason to give it to others rather than hoard it for yourself."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul uses the example of the believer as the important picture of truth. People are going to read you more than they read their Bibles. He talks about the Spirit providing life in the believer, the ministry that the Spirit provides for those that are exercised in the faith, the necessity to uncover your heart’s veil, and keeping from abusing our liberty for the Lord. He continues in this chapter by talking about taking your liberties and pursuing mercy and ministry. We can either use our liberties for selfish gain (which is much of the world’s goal at this point) or we can use it to give others freedom and encouragement. Paul will give you ample reason to give it to others rather than hoard it for yourself."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Ministry of Mercy","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has not been quiet about the liberties in which the Lord has graciously bestowed upon His children. We don't deserve the liberty and freedom that we are given, but it's still given to us by God's grace. The problem is, we usually abuse those liberties. That is why we have to view it as Paul does. He says, \"We have received mercy.\""},{"kind":"thought","text":"Mercy is a ministry, according to Paul. It's a nonexistent one, honestly. I don't know many people who are eager to show mercy to other people."},{"kind":"para","text":"I hope I could be a man of mercy, but I see so many people (people of the faith even) who are terribly passionate about tearing me and others down that it's very difficult to live in this ministry of mercy. God is a merciful God. We abuse our liberty, and He shows us mercy. It would only make sense that we could do the same thing for others when we are abused by their liberties."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christians are to be EXTRA cautious in their lifestyles. We don't intend to abuse our liberties. We don't want to walk in \"craftiness.\" \"Craftiness\" means \"a specious or false wisdom.\" We are not trying to have our actions be something phony. If I can be honest, there are a lot of phony ministers in this world. It has been a real problem for a long time now. It's usually why people don't trust ministers today."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They don't trust the church because the leaders there are purposely walking in \"craftiness.\" They are looking to minister to you, only to get something OUT of you. They often lure you in with a false sense of hope and a plea for direction, just to take what you have and throw you to the side."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was a difficult thing to be a minister in Corinth. Paul says that he gave them an example and went to them with a genuine love and labor for their souls, not with some sort of ulterior motive. He did a real work of love for them. He preached to them with sincerity and didn't handle the Word of God with deceit. It's another problem in the church today. We have a lot of pastors and preachers preaching DOWN on people rather than preaching them up."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I see a lot of pastors preaching GREAT messages... but they don't actually live those messages themselves. Preaching must affect you before it can affect others. If your own preaching doesn't change your lifestyle, then you shouldn't expect it to change anyone else's either."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Paul basically says he could have easily deceived these people. There are plenty of things to rob from these people. It wouldn't have taken too much to abuse his own power against them. The only reason he doesn't do that is because he is genuinely convinced by the Lord's ministry and completely convicted by the Holy Spirit's conscience."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He is too indebted to the Lord to abuse His leadership in ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"He has no room to abuse the ministry because of how passionate he is for God and His people. That should be the example of every preacher."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only reason that someone is not going to receive the gospel should not be because the preacher is abusing the gospel message or preaching down on people. The only people who will not accept the gospel are those that don't know the Lord personally. Before I was saved, the gospel fell on deaf ears. Like literally. People would quote scripture and salvation to me, and I would literally shut my ears off. It was as if my brain had some sort of defense mechanism in it that kept the Word of God from being grasped. I could understand any other form of conversation, but the Word of God went right over my head no matter how simple someone made it to be. It is quite a mystery how I have been saved at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, it took trust for me to be saved. I had to trust that what the person was telling me was real. I didn't know if it was or not. I rejected what others were saying to me because I didn't want to believe that they could be absolute about something that seems impossible to know. It wasn't until I just trusted that maybe they knew what they were talking about that I started looking into it myself with genuine fervency. The light of the gospel finally shined through, and it was as if something just clicked. I suddenly understood and paid attention to what scripture was saying, and then I started digging into myself. That is what salvation does to someone. It was only through salvation, by the way. It was when I finally said, \"Okay. I will just accept Christ. I can't do anything else, and I have no reason to reject Him any longer. I will accept it.\" That was when God opened His Word to me."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"Before I was saved, the gospel fell on deaf ears. Like literally. People would quote scripture and salvation to me, and I would literally shut my ears off. It was as if my brain had some sort of defense mechanism in it that kept the Word of God from being grasped. I could understand any other form of conversation, but the Word of God went right over my head no matter how simple someone made it to be. It is quite a mystery how I have been saved at all. Honestly, it took trust for me to be saved. I had to trust that what the person was telling me was real. I didn't know if it was or not. I rejected what others were saying to me because I didn't want to believe that they could be absolute about something that seems impossible to know. It wasn't until I just trusted that maybe they knew what they were talking about that I started looking into it myself with genuine fervency. The light of the gospel finally shined through, and it was as if something just clicked. I suddenly understood and paid attention to what scripture was saying, and then I started digging into myself. That is what salvation does to someone. It was only through salvation, by the way. It was when I finally said, \"Okay. I will just accept Christ. I can't do anything else, and I have no reason to reject Him any longer. I will accept it.\" That was when God opened His Word to me."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers have nothing to gain from their congregants, but everything to give."},{"kind":"para","text":"We give ourselves to others all the time."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It is impossible to be a minister and not put the needs of others first."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know how many times I have given up my own comforts and my own time spent with my family to put ministry needs first. You know how much recognition I have received? None. Actually, in the past six years of being in the ministry, it feels like I have less to show for it than I do for the countless hours of counsel and prayer for the efforts I have made. This is not a boast or a woe. This is just the reality of ministry. And four of those six years were done for absolutely free. Even the first two years were financially unstable. I took my paycheck from ministry and put it right back into ministry. I have been used, abused, thrown to the side, taken advantage of, and held back from doing so many things that the Lord has called me to do, and yet (for some reason higher than my understanding) I am still in ministry. I can't seem to get rid of it. It keeps coming back, and I have been sucked into it to continue forward. The only thing I can do is, as Paul says, and that is to preach Christ. It has nothing to do with me. If it did, I would not be doing it. I would have no reason to be abused like this in life for free. I would have kept away from it, but I cannot let it go. I cannot help but give the truth and love people. I cannot help but be a servant for the sake of Jesus Christ. I love Him too much to stay away."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Darkness is the sin of this world. It's the world in general."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It might be a bright, beautiful day where you are reading this right now, but the people are dark."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are walking around without a concern for the Lord or a care for their sins. The church in Corinth was surrounded by real darkness. I think we have a little brighter of a world today."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Though our politicians might throw around how free you are to murder children, the majority of the people in this country know that this is a gross misuse of power. They know how disgusting and hateful someone would have to be to kill such an innocent being."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the land of Corinth, I think most of the people there had really calloused themselves to sin. They were living so freely in it that nothing really bothered them when it came to abusing their freedom and liberty. That was the culture that the church had to navigate. They were free to do those things too, but they had the Lord keeping them from abusing their liberty. They had LIGHT in the world of darkness. Because of that, all they could do (against their own sinful flesh) was bring glory to the Lord. It might have sounded fun to roll around sin for a while, or to leave ministry to live like the world, but something was keeping the church from going too far into it. It was the same thing that kept Paul in the ministry. The amount of pain and hurting that Paul had to suffer is certainly enough to keep MOST (sane) people away from ministry forever. Yet, Paul is still in it. We will read about that in chapter 11 though."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":15,"title":"Valued Ministry","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a small and insignificant creation we are in the grand scheme of things. Yet the Lord has allowed us to be used as something valuable."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He's given us a \"treasure\" which is found in the gospel. It's found in preaching and teaching. It's a treasure to have that privilege."},{"kind":"para","text":"We might be the vessel used, but it's God that is USING the vessel. He gets all the glory."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"As I've said before, I have tried to leave ministry on more than one occasion. It's not as if I just HATE ministry, I just don't have the mental capacity to be beaten by people whom I'm trying to serve and love over and over again. It is a very trying thing to be used until there is nothing left in you. Yet, that is my treasure."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul calls it a treasure. I can't fathom it. The Lord keeps bringing me back, and for some reason I feel rewarded every single time I am hurt and abused. I understand that there is value to others that may not say anything openly. I think they know the value, they just don't want to say it. It's not until I am gone that they might actually consider it to be of value, and I can only hope that they would continue to build and continue to move forward because of what I have taught them. Ministry is a treasure... go figure. Paul demonstrates how we can have that mindset while we are used up by the world:"}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are worn, yet God is faithful. We might feel as if there is no end, yet God reminds us He IS the end."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God.","ref":"Hebrews 12:2"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are rejected of men, yet accepted by Him;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To the praise of the glory of his grace, wherein he hath made us accepted in the beloved.","ref":"Ephesians 1:6"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are hated by the world, yet loved by the Father;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called the sons of God: therefore the world knoweth us not, because it knew him not.","ref":"1 John 3:1"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are beaten by the world, yet healed by the Lord;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness: by whose stripes ye were healed.","ref":"1 Peter 2:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Accused of the world, yet excused by the Father; Taken advantage of, yet given more wealth; Turned aside, yet led in the way; Forgotten by all, yet in the center of His mind. The Lord is able to sustain His children, so while the world makes everything in your life utterly difficult, the Lord gives you joy in the abuse. He makes it worth it for the minister to go back. That is a hard thing for me to say at this moment in my ministry. But I know it is the truth, and that is the exact reason I will end up in ministry again and again, even if the people I trust the most are completely against it and discouraging."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"**Highliht** Accused of the world, yet excused by the Father; Taken advantage of, yet given more wealth; Turned aside, yet led in the way; Forgotten by all, yet in the center of His mind."}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"A minister dies for others. We die to ourselves. We die to our sin. We die to our flesh. We die to our needs. We die to our hurts and anguish. We die to our desires. We die to our time. We die to our relaxation and comfort. We die so that others can live."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have talked about the importance of building bridges before in ministry. I said that I had read somewhere that there are many ministers who build ladders when they should be building bridges. I still hold to that. I think there are far too many people building ladders in the ministry today. It's usually the pastor's and teachers who do it. That is not a wise move for church ministry. What we must do is build bridges."},{"kind":"para","text":"What I have not said, though, is HOW to build the bridge. There's only one way to do it. It's on the backs of ministers. You either climb on top of the minister’s back to get to a higher position, or you play a game of leapfrog to help us move forward."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Building bridges must be done at the expense of your own building up. You must lay YOURSELF down if you want to build a bridge, rather than laying the congregation down so people you think deserve to be on top can climb on top of the ministers like a ladder. A minister should not think that his own life is more important than the ones he is ministering to."}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"All we can say is what we know."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are telling people about things that we don't know anything about, we are either gossipers, or liars (perhaps both)."},{"kind":"para","text":"SIDE NOTE: Gossiping is talking to others about things that you don't know anything about. I think that's important to understand because there are a lot of people who think gossiping is just saying what comes to mind. That's not exactly true. If something happened to you, and someone else did it to you, but you speak to other people about it to receive counsel and help, that is not gossip. That's confession, plain and simple. James gives us exactly how we are supposed to deal with that, but Paul has made mention of contention in the church before in Philippians and to this church in Corinth (his first letter). The point is, gossip can only happen when people are talking about things they are not involved in."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says he has only told them about the things he is absolutely sure about. Things he's experienced himself. He believes them because they have been revealed to him. He knows that Jesus died, but He was also raised from the dead, and that same promise is given to us as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live:","ref":"John 11:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Death (and whatever trials we might face here on Earth) is not the end of the road for the Christian. There is a life that is coming after this. A BETTER life. One where there are no trials for our efforts. We can minister to others and we will never be abused for it or grow weary in it. One where we will never have the desire to quit and draw back. That should give us hope in our ministries today."},{"kind":"thought","text":"**SIDE NOTE:** Gossiping is talking to others about things that you don't know anything about. I think that's important to understand because there are a lot of people who think gossiping is just saying what comes to mind. That's not exactly true. If something happened to you, and someone else did it to you, but you speak to other people about it to receive counsel and help, that is not gossip. That's confession, plain and simple. James gives us exactly how we are supposed to deal with that, but Paul has made mention of contention in the church before in Philippians and to this church in Corinth (his first letter). The point is, gossip can only happen when people are talking about things they are not involved in."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Apostles have set the foundation for us today. Of course, Jesus is the cornerstone; without Him, we cannot build anything structural. But the Apostles are the stepping stones laid within the foundation. They are what has given us the ability to build a spiritual home. They died to give us this luxury. They worked tirelessly and left their comfort to start us off strong in the faith. If they did not do that, we would have a hard time continuing in the ministry today. They showed by their faith in the Lord that ministry is worth it and that we are able to continue on to death."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If they can do it with what had been thrown at them, there is plenty of reason for us to do it with strides today. We have a higher liberty than they did, and that should be what keeps us going."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"Renewed Again","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We need an inward renewal. Our churches call it a \"revival\" today. There are a lot of people who are nearly dead all over the church spectrum. We are not quite dead, but we are almost there."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It's when we die that the reviving will commence."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must die first, right? We must die to a great deal of things that bring us selfish gain. But we can't just do it once (Paul has already talked about this before):"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I protest by your rejoicing which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are constantly put in danger by the world, but we are renewed by the Lord every single day. He gives us fresh life. Tomorrow will be the first day of our life with the Lord, and the day after that will be the first day of our life with the Lord, and the day after that will be the first day of our life with the Lord, and so on..."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We must renew our lives every single morning we wake up. Yesterday’s problems are simply that. They are yesterday’s problems. Tomorrow’s problems will be tomorrow’s problems. Focus on what you have today, and use what you can to fill your Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Take therefore no thought for the morrow: for the morrow shall take thought for the things of itself. Sufficient unto the day is the evil thereof.","ref":"Matthew 6:34"}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul says that his current trials (or today’s troubles) are nothing but a \"light affliction.\" He will go on later to list the \"light affliction\" that he has faced, and I will tell you that none of it is \"light.\" They are heavy things to carry. Compared to our afflictions today, Paul's afflictions would probably nearly destroy us. We would not be able to handle it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Paul could handle it most of the time either. But he reminded himself of the things that are coming. He reminded himself of eternity. He says, \"The things we face here, they are temporal. They are only going to last for this lifetime. But the things we look for in Heaven, they are eternal, they will last for eternity.\" That is what kept Paul going."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps that is what can keep us going. Maybe that's what will give us hope through the pain of ministry."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"May we think about the eternal more than the temporal."},{"kind":"para","text":"Something is going to occupy our thoughts and time, and I pray that we are able to focus on what is to come for eternity, rather than the problems we face right now. We will have a peaceful ministry when we are dead. Until then, there is a work to be done while we are here."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18}],"verses":{"1":"Therefore seeing we have this ministry, as we have received mercy, we faint not;","2":"But have renounced the hidden things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness, nor handling the word of God deceitfully; but by manifestation of the truth commending ourselves to every man's conscience in the sight of God.","3":"But if our gospel be hid, it is hid to them that are lost:","4":"In whom the god of this world hath blinded the minds of them which believe not, lest the light of the glorious gospel of Christ, who is the image of God, should shine unto them.","5":"For we preach not ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord; and ourselves your servants for Jesus' sake.","6":"For God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.","7":"But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us.","8":"We are troubled on every side, yet not distressed; we are perplexed, but not in despair;","9":"Persecuted, but not forsaken; cast down, but not destroyed;","10":"Always bearing about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus, that the life also of Jesus might be made manifest in our body.","11":"For we which live are always delivered unto death for Jesus' sake, that the life also of Jesus might be made manifest in our mortal flesh.","12":"So then death worketh in us, but life in you.","13":"We having the same spirit of faith, according as it is written, I believed, and therefore have I spoken; we also believe, and therefore speak;","14":"Knowing that he which raised up the Lord Jesus shall raise up us also by Jesus, and shall present us with you.","15":"For all things are for your sakes, that the abundant grace might through the thanksgiving of many redound to the glory of God.","16":"For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day.","17":"For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, worketh for us a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory;","18":"While we look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen: for the things which are seen are temporal; but the things which are not seen are eternal."}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul made a lot of really great points about how valuable a minister is, and how important it is to take ministry seriously. He talked about the ministry of mercy, and the importance of renewing the Spirit every day."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will get a look at the importance of humility in the life of a minister. We are not much of anything. We might think we are something special because the Lord has placed us into a work, but we are really nothing but lowly servants who don't deserve to be called by the Lord at all."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul made a lot of really great points about how valuable a minister is, and how important it is to take ministry seriously. He talked about the ministry of mercy, and the importance of renewing the Spirit every day. I think ministry is wasted on a lot of people today who don't have the desire or ability to actually minister to the needs of people. They want to just be a recognized face instead of being a compassionate minister. In this chapter, we will get a look at the importance of humility in the life of a minister. We are not much of anything. We might think we are something special because the Lord has placed us into a work, but we are really nothing but lowly servants who don't deserve to be called by the Lord at all."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"A Groaning Spirit","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Is there something on this Earth that we can lose that would be worth suffering over? There are many things I care about while I'm here, but ultimately nothing will be more prominent than what I am setting up for eternity."},{"kind":"thought","text":"These notes that I'm writing are not going to be eternal. But they might lead a sinner to the Lord! Perhaps a great-grandchild picks up this book and reads through it! That would certainly be an eternal reward. They will know that their grandfather cared very much about what the truth said, and was willing to leave his thoughts for them so they can not just get to know me, but get to know the Lord as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Everything in this world can be taken, but the house that we should be focused on building is the one that no one else can touch, which is reserved for us in Heaven."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In my Father's house are many mansions: if it were not so, I would have told you. I go to prepare a place for you.","ref":"John 14:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"No one can take that home away from you. No one can destroy it or rob it. No one can try and change it or move it. The Lord builds the house, and it will be eternal if He does."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except the LORD keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain.","ref":"Psalm 127:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is applicable as your spiritual building and your eternal one."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, Paul is not talking about a literal house. We could certainly apply it in that way, but he is talking about a personal shelter. What you see today is my dwelling. This is what my dwelling looks like. It's not much. But my eternal house is far more glorious and useful."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The house is a dwelling place for the Spirit. My current home is temporal. My eternal home is forever. That is the place in which my spirit will dwell. This house here is falling apart. The house in Heaven lasts forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only way we will be \"naked\" (as it’s referenced by Paul here) in eternity is if we have no place for our soul to dwell. We will be \"homeless,\" that is what \"naked\" means. If we are homeless, we will be spending our eternity in a vast ocean of fire and pain. That is where the rest of the spiritually homeless will dwell."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There's nothing more miserable to be physically naked and homeless. I can't imagine how much MORE miserable it is to be spiritually naked and homeless."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Life has plenty of burdens. There are plenty of reasons for man to groan today. Just getting out of bed in the mornings gives me reason to groan. Everything pops and aches. Dealing with arrogant and selfish people makes me groan. Being used and rejected makes me groan. Being constantly blocked from what is good and what brings me joy causes me to groan. There are a lot of reasons to groan. I'm so thankful that the new home I will be in will constantly be joyous."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"There is no groaning in Heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says here, it's okay to groan in this mortal body. You have reason to groan. But we are not groaning because we don't have a future immortal body. We groan from this Earth, but we don't groan from eternity."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The only thing that keeps us in joy here is knowing there is a better home waiting for all of us after this."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"More than my groaning, I desire the Spirit of the Lord. It's an \"earnest\" pursuit in this life. It's what allows me to keep going."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I may be groaning, but at least I'm getting up."},{"kind":"para","text":"I groan through the pain of my deteriorating body, but the Lord renews my Spirit. I groan through the annoyance of rejection, but the Spirit of God keeps me going (we spoke about that in the last chapter). I groan through the heartache of being misused, but the Spirit gives me purpose."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The Spirit is just a small glimpse at what we are getting in eternity. We cannot access the fullness of the Spirit of God because we have groaning bodies while we are here."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are in eternity without pain, we will have the full Spirit of God, and we will have joy in everything we do without aches and pains. We get a small glimpse here of what we will have full access to in eternity."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":10,"title":"Present With the Lord","entries":[{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are walking with the Lord, but we are not present with the Lord. His Holy Spirit is certainly present. But, again, we don't have full access to His Spirit in our current groaning bodies. We won't have access to it until we are with Him in eternity. That is what gives us confidence though."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We are confident that FAITH is what keeps us going. FAITH is what renews our Spirit. FAITH is how we keep walking in this groaning body."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's what gives us access to the Spirit today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Without faith, we are not going to have confidence. We will just have groaning, and that groaning will lead us to depression, and that depression will lead us to loneliness, and that loneliness will lead us to bitterness, and that bitterness will lead us to death."},{"kind":"thought","text":"For the time being, we walk by faith. When we have our eternal home (that is our eternal body), we will walk by sight."},{"kind":"para","text":"For now, our confidence should be in two things. First, it should be in while we are here, that we walk by faith because we are not present with the Lord. Second should be this:"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Our confidence, either way (whether we are here on Earth, or we are in the life after this), is in eternity. I think we would all be willing to be present with the Lord. I am ready to leave this old worn house and put on the new house that God has gifted me with in eternity. I can't wait to put on the Spiritual (that is, the eternal) body."},{"kind":"para","text":"I really enjoy this verse, but there is something that we should take into consideration when reading it. There is NO fast-tracking this confidence. We cannot take our own life to be with the Lord faster. It's tempting, but the Lord has us here for a purpose. While we are here, we are groaning, but we have a Spiritual renewing every single day."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day.","ref":"2 Corinthians 4:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Spirit never gets old. We can be patient in entering into eternity when God is ready to take us there. Does this mean that people who have committed suicide are not saved today? I don't believe that."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I believe that salvation happens in a moment, not in our actions. It happens the moment we put our faith in the Lord. Faith is as much an \"action\" as breathing."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not something we have to really exert our energy into. It's something that should just be natural and continual."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Faith is an action, sure, but it's not a physical one. It's more of a thought."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.","ref":"Hebrews 11:1"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"You can't \"do\" faith, you can only \"have\" faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"So it's faith that saves a man, not any actual physical \"action.\" If someone truly has trusted in Jesus, suicide is not going to separate that salvation. However, those that are saved know that they are here for a purpose. It is up to the Lord to do something with our faith. It is up to us to simply allow Him to use it. Once we are face to face with Him (in His presence), it will be easier to put our faith into action. In fact, I believe faith will be something you \"do\" rather than just \"have\" when we are present with the Lord. It will be a literal lifestyle. But until then, we labor:"}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have things to do while we are here. We (that is all of creation, saved or unsaved) will stand before the Lord and give an account of what we have done."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We must give an account of our actions here by faith if we have Christ. If we don't have Christ, we must give an account of our sins. I can't imagine having to give an account for my sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"How terrible it would be and feel to have all of my terrible deeds out on display, and the Lord to be upset with me. He would be groaning over our actions without Him. He IS groaning over our actions without Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thankfully, the Lord gives us a means to salvation, and protects us from His groaning."},{"kind":"thought","text":"His Spirit does the groaning on behalf of our Spirit. Our physical body groans, but our spiritual body is saved from that because the Lord's Spirit groans FOR us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered.","ref":"Romans 8:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"God's Spirit groans for us. It relieves the pain from us."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I am so thankful I do not have to groan both in my body and in my spirit."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":15,"title":"Not Abusing Liberty","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God should be reverenced in awe. But the word used here is \"terror.\" The terror of the Lord is His judgement."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Without Christ, we are in danger of judgement for our sins. That is a great terror because He has the power and responsibility to throw you into a place of eternal torment and torture. Yes, it's His responsibility to do so. He cannot allow sin to enter Heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those that have Christ are considered \"sinless\" in the sense that their sins have been forgiven. Sins can only be forgiven through repentance, and repentance can only take place through salvation. If you are not saved, you cannot have your sins forgiven. That's how that works. You are saved from God's judgement of sin. That is what you are saved from. His judgement leads to an eternity in Hell without Christ. This is why we need the payment for our sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"How do we know this to be true? Well, your conscience makes it manifest. \"Manifest\" means \"make visible.\" We know what is against the Lord because our conscience tells us what's against the Lord. We cannot comfortably lie. There will always be something that wears on you when you lie. We cannot comfortably murder someone. There will always be something that wears on you when you kill. God has written His law on your heart:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whereof the Holy Ghost also is a witness to us: for after that he had said before, This is the covenant that I will make with them after those days, saith the Lord, I will put my laws into their hearts, and in their minds will I write them; And their sins and iniquities will I remember no more. Now where remission of these is, there is no more offering for sin.","ref":"Hebrews 10:15-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what conscience means. \"Con\" means \"with\" and \"science\" means \"knowledge.\" So when you sin, you will always do it with knowledge that it’s a sin. In other words, you know it's wrong, and you will still do it knowing it's wrong. So because we know about the \"terror\" of the Lord, we preach freedom in His salvation so no man has to face that."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of hellfire and brimstone preachers out there. I don't have an issue with them. There is a time and place for such preaching."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Practical preaching leaves the hearer with a thought, though. If we are just preaching Hell all the time, we will not have much thought on Heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a level of grace that the Christian should be living in, and that won't happen if all we hear and think about is torment and torture. Those that already have the Lord need to be preaching in a loving manner. There is a loving way to preach, mind you."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not all about how much you can say or what kind of conviction you can force. No, the message (honestly) should convict the preacher first. Once YOU feel convicted, then others will feel convicted by the message. That is what Paul is preaching here. He is preaching a self-convicted message to this church, and he is very obviously doing it in a loving way."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If we can read this without the love of Paul in it, then we have missed the tone of this letter."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church in Corinth will receive this letter in love, that is for sure. They will take it and connect to Paul spiritually as they face the trials of their faith in a city that is given to all sorts of sinful living. Paul knows exactly how they feel. He knows how difficult it is. He knows how easy it is for the Christian to get their mind off focusing on the main thing. That is why Paul writes this letter. He sympathizes with this church."},{"kind":"thought","text":"People think they are being a prime example of the perfect Christian, and they live a lawful life and can prove it, but Paul tells this church to be humble."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, humility is the theme of this second letter. Paul is writing in humility, and I think that is what he is trying to get the people of Corinth to understand how necessary humility is in the church house."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Going back to liberty, we know what we are capable of doing and how far we are capable of going. I think we could take alcohol as a great example of liberty."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I think if anyone has liberty to drink, it would be the Christian. However, we should not abuse that liberty just because we have it. We should be very cautious about drinking, especially if it's in front of people who are not as strong in the faith (or of no faith at all). You can pull them into something they cannot get out of."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says, \"If people see us as crazy, it is because we are crazy about the Lord, and if we are sober (or refraining from using or abusing our liberty), it is for YOU.\" Paul (and the Apostles) want to be an example for the churches to follow. They will give up things that are seemingly \"fun\" in order for the churches to have an example that is steady and strong. Paul didn't waver."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Honestly, there was so much against him that there would have probably been plenty of times for Paul to call it quits, but he never does. He stays the course until his death. He does it so that there is no one in the faith (or in the church) that can say it's not possible."},{"kind":"para","text":"He proved through his own work and life that he is able to continue forth with the Lord in liberty and joy, refraining from sin and anything evil."}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"If we are going to live, it will be for Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is where the ultimate satisfaction comes from anyway. Sin has its satisfactions, but it doesn't last long enough. With sin, you have to keep dousing yourself in it to be satisfied. With God, you only have to take it once and satisfaction lasts for an eternity."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"As long as we are alive on Earth, we will live for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the only reason the Lord has kept us here. He could have just called us home already, but obviously He is not done with us, and so we are going to live while we are here. He keeps us from doing things that are ungodly and displeasing. He keeps us from falling victim to the swaying of the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are to be constantly under His control so we are not overtaken by fleshly, sinful desires. When we have taken Him, we have died to those things that we thought were pleasure, and have been made alive to Christ alone."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"You were purchased by the Lord. Therefore, you are to do what He has paid for you to do. And you will do it with joy! Why? Well, because you love Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you truly and genuinely love Christ, then you are going to want to live in a way that pleases Him."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":19,"title":"Eyes of Faith","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"How do you know men? How do you view people? I think a lot of \"Christians\" look at people based on their flesh. They look to the outward appearance. They look at what they can see on the outside, and they don't dare try and figure out a man's spiritual nature."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Most Christians are good judges of what people look like, but they don't seem to be good judges of who people really are."},{"kind":"thought","text":"A lot of pastors make that mistake, sadly. They view their congregants and workers based on what they are good at doing, instead of looking at who the Lord has made them to be. They take their jobs, their income, and their work ethic, and that is how they can determine what position they best fill in the church. That is the wrong way to do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should not know men after the flesh. If that doesn't make sense to you, let me dumb it down like Paul is trying to do here. Look at your relationship with Christ. You likely had a very fleshly relationship with the world before Christ, and when you came to Christ, it was likely a fleshly search for Him, but once you had received Him, it was no longer in the flesh."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"So now you don't see Christ through the eyes of flesh. You see Christ through the eyes of faith. That is how you should be looking at man as well. Not faith in man, but faith in the Lord for the direction of man."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why? Well, it's because you cannot do anything EXCEPT have eyes of faith now that you are in the Lord. Paul illustrates further:"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great verse! I especially like the \"behold\" part. He's drawing attention to a major change. He says, \"You used to have eyes of the flesh. You saw people only on the outside. You saw the Lord through His sacrifice and nothing further. But now that you are IN Christ, you are a new creature. You see things differently. The old views are gone, and the new view is here.\" When we are in Christ, it's not just our vision that becomes new. It's our actions as well!"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"So when we view other people's actions based on the flesh, we only see what they are skilled at. When we view other people's actions based on the faith, we will see what they can become skilled at."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see they have a greater purpose than their fleshly actions."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"Listen, I love guitar. I grew up playing guitar. I played at a lot of venues and bars and all sorts of ungodly places. I won competitions, wrote music, recorded records, and spent nights upon nights finishing recordings, and hanging out with friends. That was my life. I don't want to boast, but I was pretty good at it. I enjoyed it very much. But then I became a Christian, and my longing changed. I wanted to teach scripture instead of play guitar. And wouldn't you know it, nearly every pastor I meet would rather have me play guitar instead of teach. Yet it's the people I teach that would rather have me teach than play guitar. My spiritual gift is teaching. My worldly gift is music."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If church leaders and pastors don't see your gifts through the eyes of faith, it should not keep you from pursuing what the Lord has pressed upon your heart."},{"kind":"para","text":"How you pursue it is between you and the Lord. Just know that you should take what you can and go after it."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"Here's a new ministry that Paul talks about. It's the ministry of \"reconciliation.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Reconciliation\" means \"restoration to favor.\" It's restoring favor of God to sinners who have trusted in Christ. It's a ministry. It's the Lord's ministry. That is what His sole focus is on. He wants to restore you to Him, but He wants to do it so that you have a Heavenly home and a new life."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He wants to give you eyes of faith so that we are no longer walking simply by sight. He wants to give you the ability to see things in a spiritual way instead of just a physical way."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a power granted to every single child of God, and yet it's a neglected power. I don't see many people using this gift of sight for Heavenly purposes. We are all still too caught up on what we can physically see to ever look deeper than that. We are too simple..."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Jesus did what He did to keep us from having to suffer in our sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a glorious revelation! That is a guaranteed promise to every person that turns to the Lord in faith. The purpose of it, ultimately, is to be in a personal relationship with the Lord."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"That's really what God wants. He wants YOU to be in HIS presence. He has done everything possible to make that happen, even though we have done everything possible to keep it from happening."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's kind of interesting to think about. We have hated the Lord, and He has loved us and pursued us without reciprocation. It is sad."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It's sad to not be loved in return for your endless love for others. That is how God views things..."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"title":"Ambassadors For Christ","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's not forget the theme of this chapter. Humility is certainly a theme. Paul says that he is an \"ambassador\" for Christ. Christ has chosen Paul to do that. He chose the Apostles to be ambassadors. Now the Lord has chosen US for it. \"Ambassadors\" means \"an accredited diplomat sent by a country as its official representative to a foreign country.\" (New Oxford American Dictionary) The foreign country we have been called to by the Lord is this entire world. Yet we are sent here by Him to represent Heaven. We represent the Kingdom of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is something that Jesus talked very much about. If I could, I would tell you to go and directly talk to Christ about this matter, but the lost of this world will not do that. So, we must be the ambassadors for Him in order to direct the lost."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We can only say, \"Please! Reconcile yourself to the Lord! Reconnect with Him. Turn to Him! You still have time!\" And then live what that looks like. That is all I can do. That's all any of us can do. We cannot force anyone to be in the kingdom as much as we can force anyone to live in America. We can only tell them about the land of the free and hope that they reconsider it."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a truth."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Jesus WORE our sin. He literally bore the weight of our sin (past, present, and future) so that He could nail it to the cross and forgive it. That was the purpose of His coming to the cross."},{"kind":"para","text":"That was why He died that way. He displayed His love for sinful humanity on the cross. That should be a no-brainer to us at this point, yet it's something we seem to keep forgetting."},{"kind":"para","text":"We might remember that He died for us, but we don't explore the depths of it as often as we should. It is a good thing for us to keep going back to the gospel messages as often as possible. It's a good thing for us to be reminded of the Lord's death for you and why He did it. The people at Corinth probably understood this, but they needed a refresher. They needed a reminder from Paul. Again, I believe that Paul had dealt with some pretty obvious struggles in his own walk and ministry, and these are reminders that have helped HIM (personally) stay the course. But that is the only way we can actually be ambassadors."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If we try and display Christ without having the righteousness of Christ, we will never give a good impression of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will be fake ambassadors that allow people to have a sour taste in their mouth about the Lord rather than a joyous view of what the Spirit sees. I believe that is why so many have such a bad view of the church today."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We have too many worldly ambassadors pretending to be ambassadors of Christ. They don't have the righteousness of Christ stamped on their lives and so they are just flagrant about their example and calling."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even church leaders, honestly."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"For we know that if our earthly house of this tabernacle were dissolved, we have a building of God, an house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens.","2":"For in this we groan, earnestly desiring to be clothed upon with our house which is from heaven:","3":"If so be that being clothed we shall not be found naked.","4":"For we that are in this tabernacle do groan, being burdened: not for that we would be unclothed, but clothed upon, that mortality might be swallowed up of life.","5":"Now he that hath wrought us for the selfsame thing is God, who also hath given unto us the earnest of the Spirit.","6":"Therefore we are always confident, knowing that, whilst we are at home in the body, we are absent from the Lord:","7":"(For we walk by faith, not by sight:)","8":"We are confident, I say, and willing rather to be absent from the body, and to be present with the Lord.","9":"Wherefore we labour, that, whether present or absent, we may be accepted of him.","10":"For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ; that every one may receive the things done in his body, according to that he hath done, whether it be good or bad.","11":"Knowing therefore the terror of the Lord, we persuade men; but we are made manifest unto God; and I trust also are made manifest in your consciences.","12":"For we commend not ourselves again unto you, but give you occasion to glory on our behalf, that ye may have somewhat to answer them which glory in appearance, and not in heart.","13":"For whether we be beside ourselves, it is to God: or whether we be sober, it is for your cause.","14":"For the love of Christ constraineth us; because we thus judge, that if one died for all, then were all dead:","15":"And that he died for all, that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves, but unto him which died for them, and rose again.","16":"Wherefore henceforth know we no man after the flesh: yea, though we have known Christ after the flesh, yet now henceforth know we him no more.","17":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","18":"And all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation;","19":"To wit, that God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them; and hath committed unto us the word of reconciliation.","20":"Now then we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God did beseech you by us: we pray you in Christ's stead, be ye reconciled to God.","21":"For he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him."}},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul spoke about our groaning. He talked about how our bodies groan, and then how the Spirit of God groans for us. He reminded us that we should live like we are currently present with the Lord until the day we are physically present with Him in Spirit. He continues to hammer in the important fact that we have liberty, but we should not abuse it, and how to walk in the eyes of faith. He ended the chapter reminding the church that we are ambassadors of Christ, and that we are representations of His love and ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul will give a very important detailed list of what a true minister is. He talks about the necessity in the church, and why we must be okay with being abused for the ministry, and to focus our love on the Lord so we can have the proper love for the church."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul spoke about our groaning. He talked about how our bodies groan, and then how the Spirit of God groans for us. He reminded us that we should live like we are currently present with the Lord until the day we are physically present with Him in Spirit. He continues to hammer in the important fact that we have liberty, but we should not abuse it, and how to walk in the eyes of faith. He ended the chapter reminding the church that we are ambassadors of Christ, and that we are representations of His love and ministry. In this chapter, Paul will give a very important detailed list of what a true minister is. He talks about the necessity in the church, and why we must be okay with being abused for the ministry, and to focus our love on the Lord so we can have the proper love for the church."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"A Labor of Love","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"Is it possible to receive God's grace in “vain“? Vain simply means that it's meaningless. Almost to say that it has no power or value. God's grace is the power of salvation, so how is it possible to have it in \"vain”? This is observable in the church house, honestly. It's the people who are not \"workers together.\" They don't work with their congregants and they don't work with the Lord. If they cannot personally get something out of the effort, then they don't want to put IN the effort."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, there are some in leadership of the church who are receiving God's grace in vain. They have forsaken it. They may have started their ministry with grace, but they are not ending it with grace. They have been in it for so long that grace is a lost trait in their lives. They don't have it in their ministering, and they don't have it in their families. They don't even have it in their friendships. I think most Pastors (if I can be honest) don't have friends. They have superficial friends. They have \"ministry\" friends. That is not a genuine friend. That is a co-laborer."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Co-laborers are absolutely necessary, but they cannot replace what only a friend can give you. Friendship is a MUST if you are going to be a minister in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is why Scripture talks SO STRONGLY about having the proper friends;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A man that hath friends must shew himself friendly: and there is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother.","ref":"Proverbs 18:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Iron sharpeneth iron; so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend.","ref":"Proverbs 27:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ointment and perfume rejoice the heart: so doth the sweetness of a man's friend by hearty counsel.","ref":"Proverbs 27:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To him that is afflicted pity should be shewed from his friend; but he forsaketh the fear of the Almighty.","ref":"Job 6:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even Jesus illustrated the importance of friendship:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are my friends, if ye do whatsoever I command you. Henceforth I call you not servants; for the servant knoweth not what his lord doeth: but I have called you friends; for all things that I have heard of my Father I have made known unto you.","ref":"John 15:14-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had friends, Peter had friends, John had friends, all the Apostles had friends. Even David had a best friend (whose name was Jonathan)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Jonathan and David made a covenant, because he loved him as his own soul.","ref":"1 Samuel 18:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even a marriage is a deeper connection to a friend."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Friendship is a great sign of genuine love."},{"kind":"para","text":"A pastor who does not have friends does not know love (even if they are married). The reason they avoid making friends is because they simply can't trust other people with who they truly are. They think they are held to the highest standard, and therefore they are not allowed to be vulnerable (or human). All that does is disconnect themselves from their congregants, not minister to them. We must be \"workers together\" with Christ, and that means we have to be transparent and willing to be vulnerable."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Succoured\" means \"to help.\" The Lord tells each of us that He has given us time. He has helped us even while we were against Him. But there is no time left. There is no promise of any more time. So TODAY, right NOW is the time for salvation. Christ has already come for us, He already died for us, He already saved us from our sins, so there is no reason to put off salvation for the future, when Jesus came to save you in the past. He rescued those that accept Him. He's helped you, there's no reason to reject Him any longer. Those that accept Him today (and His help) are those that can be co-laborers with Him. We become more than that, even. We become friends with the Lord."}]},{"start":3,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This list, honestly, can go on even longer. But Paul hits the list of a minister powerfully in these verses. I don't have the time to go through each individual thing on this list, but I will take a moment to reference them as best and quickly as possible. The reason we are able to have these examples, and go through these things is so that others can have comfort and encouragement when they face them. They are not alone. It might feel as if we are alone when we go through a trial or burden, but there are others who have been through it, and it would do them well to help you with your burden. That is WHY they went through it. They went through it to help others. And we do it without GIVING offense back."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We take the hit, and don't start swinging."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's a tough thing to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"We handle it with patience;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Rest in the LORD, and wait patiently for him: fret not thyself because of him who prospereth in his way, because of the man who bringeth wicked devices to pass.","ref":"Psalm 37:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"We deal with the afflictions;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Many are the afflictions of the righteous: but the LORD delivereth him out of them all.","ref":"Psalm 34:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"We pray for our necessities;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus.","ref":"Philippians 4:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"We hope in our distresses;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In my distress I called upon the LORD, and cried to my God: and he did hear my voice out of his temple, and my cry did enter into his ears.","ref":"2 Samuel 22:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"We humble ourselves to stripes;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he that knew not, and did commit things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few stripes. For unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required: and to whom men have committed much, of him they will ask the more.","ref":"Luke 12:48"},{"kind":"para","text":"We accept our imprisonments;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Remember them that are in bonds, as bound with them; and them which suffer adversity, as being yourselves also in the body.","ref":"Hebrews 13:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"We deal with our tumults;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For, lo, thine enemies make a tumult: and they that hate thee have lifted up the head.","ref":"Psalm 83:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We thrive in our labors;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye stedfast, unmoveable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as ye know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:58"},{"kind":"para","text":"We care for our watchings;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Continue in prayer, and watch in the same with thanksgiving;","ref":"Colossians 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We lean in our fastings;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When I wept, and chastened my soul with fasting, that was to my reproach.","ref":"Psalm 69:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"We stick with our pureness;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are the pure in heart: for they shall see God.","ref":"Matthew 5:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"We gain in our knowledge;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The heart of the prudent getteth knowledge; and the ear of the wise seeketh knowledge.","ref":"Proverbs 18:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"We wait in our longsuffering;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"With all lowliness and meekness, with longsuffering, forbearing one another in love;","ref":"Ephesians 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We endure in our kindness;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you.","ref":"Ephesians 4:32"},{"kind":"para","text":"We refresh in the Holy Ghost;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Comforter, which is the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he shall teach you all things, and bring all things to your remembrance, whatsoever I have said unto you.","ref":"John 14:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"We grow in our love;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Lord make you to increase and abound in love one toward another, and toward all men, even as we do toward you:","ref":"1 Thessalonians 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"We stand on the Word of truth;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Sanctify them through thy truth: thy word is truth.","ref":"John 17:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"We walk with the power of God;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thine, O LORD, is the greatness, and the power, and the glory, and the victory, and the majesty: for all that is in the heaven and in the earth is thine; thine is the kingdom, O LORD, and thou art exalted as head above all.","ref":"1 Chronicles 29:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"We affirm in the armor of righteousness;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If ye know that he is righteous, ye know that every one that doeth righteousness is born of him.","ref":"1 John 2:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"We persevere in our left and right hand, by honor and dishonor, evil or good reports. People will try and call us deceivers, yet we are telling the truth. We will not be known, but known by those that matter. We die to ourselves, yet live to the Lord and service. We are beaten, but not killed. We are sorrowful in the rejection, yet rejoicing in the salvation of the Lord. We are poor so others can be rich. We have nothing, but we have everything with the Lord. That is the over-encompassing life of a minister. That is who you are if you are going to minister to people. You are nothing, and yet everything all at once. What a beautiful power that the Lord has laid on us."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":13,"title":"The Heart of Pastor","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If there was any guess as to whether or not Paul loved this church, this verse answers it. You can feel the longing in this verse for this church by Paul. He is truly pouring his heart out for them. I think Paul would have been a great pastor to this church if the Lord had called him to settle there. Obviously, the Lord won't, but Paul shows immense desire to see this church succeed. He is opening his heart for the church here, and it's something we should take note of for our own pastors."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Let's measure the heart of Paul to the heart of your pastor today. Does he long for you? Does he keep communication (our mouth is open) and show his love (our heart is enlarged) for you? If not, there is something wrong with your pastor."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let's continue:"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Strained\" means \"Compressed\" or \"cramped.\" In other words, Paul says that he wants to give them freedom and encourage their ability to serve the Lord. Does your pastor do that? Does he play on your spiritual strengths? Does he even notice your spiritual strengths? That is a problem if your pastor is not paying attention to what the Lord is doing in your life and utilizing that in the church."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now Paul flips the script. He says, \"I have love for you, I want you to have love for me.\" Do you have love for your pastor? He refers to the church as his \"children.\" These are people that have learned under him personally. They should show love for him simply because of his love for them."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It's hard to love a pastor that does not love you in return. Actually, a pastor is to love FIRST before anyone loves him. Pastors love unconditionally."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are under the envelope of the list given a few verses back. They should be laying down their lives (spiritually speaking) for their congregation."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The love of a pastor motivates the love of others."},{"kind":"para","text":"If the congregants are not receiving that love first, they will not know (or desire) how to reciprocate that love."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If there is a lack of love in your church, it's probably stemmed from the leadership first."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's another theme of scripture and it's something that Paul has reminded this church about before:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil; Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth in the truth; Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things. Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let all your things be done with charity.","ref":"1 Corinthians 16:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the theme in which John got from the Lord while he was doing ministry with him:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love.","ref":"1 John 4:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is the only way in which someone can properly minister on this Earth. It is what gives us boldness and courage to lead in the proper way we were meant to:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And above all things have fervent charity among yourselves: for charity shall cover the multitude of sins.","ref":"1 Peter 4:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should love God, and then love others:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus answered him, The first of all the commandments is, Hear, O Israel; The Lord our God is one Lord: And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind, and with all thy strength: this is the first commandment. And the second is like, namely this, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. There is none other commandment greater than these.","ref":"Mark 12:29-31"}]}]},{"start":14,"end":18,"title":"Polar Opposites","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have heard people use this verse in terms of marriage, and it certainly is a good verse for that. But Paul has been talking about the church and the ministers. He has been talking about brotherly love. So this verse applies to that."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now here's the confusion though."},{"kind":"thought","text":"A lot of people take this verse completely out of context, and draw a line in the faith saying, \"We cannot have any part with a sinner. They cannot exist in the church.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Then they quote Psalm:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed is the man that walketh not in the counsel of the ungodly, nor standeth in the way of sinners, nor sitteth in the seat of the scornful.","ref":"Psalm 1:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is wrong. They misinterpret BOTH verses. This does not mean we must detach ourselves from ever being around a sinner again. That would be impossible unless you are a monk."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Rather, you should not be in \"fellowship\" with them. You should not be \"connected\" to them. No \"communion\" with the world. It's impossible to be a child of light and not shine."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It's impossible to be a child of darkness and connect to light."},{"kind":"para","text":"Darkness disappears when there is light. But if you have unsaved family, they NEED you to be around. You may not connect to them like they would want, but you can certainly be present with them. Jesus sat with sinners:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples. And when the Pharisees saw it, they said unto his disciples, Why eateth your Master with publicans and sinners?","ref":"Matthew 9:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be able to sit with sinners and not be swayed by sinners. We should be able to make the right decision even when everyone else is making the wrong one. We should know what the right decision is. We cannot know that if we are not in the Lord. He reveals all truth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come.","ref":"John 16:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"So we cannot connect (as the church) to the world, like we should to our Pastor. We cannot connect (as the church) to the world, like we should to our Savior. It's impossible."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are polar opposites. Christ is the opposite of Belial. \"Belial\" means \"Wicked.\" It's another name for Satan. They don't have any connection with one another. They CAN'T connect in the faith."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Notice that there had been several times in the gospel, though, that Jesus was AROUND Satan. Yet, He was able to do what was right even through Satan's temptation."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Infidel\" means \"faithless.\" A believer and an unbeliever cannot agree with each other. It's just not possible. They cannot connect."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the meat of it. This is where Paul is going with this message."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If you have the Spirit of God in you, then you should have no more room for any other Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the only way we are able to continue in ministry. If we don't have the Spirit of God, it will be extremely easy to just give up and reject fellowship with the right people. You are going to fellowship with something, by the way. But if you have the Lord, it will not work to fellowship with anything that goes against the Lord. You guys just won't mix. It won't even be fun for either side..."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord wants us to be separate from the world. The Corinthian church would have a very difficult time with this because there was a lot of the world calling for their attention. The world outside the church was likely making things extremely difficult for this church to live separated lives that thrive for the Lord. They were being pressured from all sides. Sadly, that's what happens to people who are not solidified in the truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, it happens to people who ARE solid in their faith as well because their Pastors do not encourage them in the right direction. It's usually the leadership (like I said) that fails first. When they fail, the rest of the church fails with him. Pastors must own up to mistakes and fix them as quickly as possible. Then they must show love, no matter what, to those that he is responsible to lead. He is to make as much effort as possible to connect with his congregants. That is what a real church looks like. That's how we can connect in the church."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18}],"verses":{"1":"We then, as workers together with him, beseech you also that ye receive not the grace of God in vain.","2":"(For he saith, I have heard thee in a time accepted, and in the day of salvation have I succoured thee: behold, now is the accepted time; behold, now is the day of salvation.)","3":"Giving no offence in any thing, that the ministry be not blamed:","4":"But in all things approving ourselves as the ministers of God, in much patience, in afflictions, in necessities, in distresses,","5":"In stripes, in imprisonments, in tumults, in labours, in watchings, in fastings;","6":"By pureness, by knowledge, by long suffering, by kindness, by the Holy Ghost, by love unfeigned,","7":"By the word of truth, by the power of God, by the armour of righteousness on the right hand and on the left,","8":"By honour and dishonour, by evil report and good report: as deceivers, and yet true;","9":"As unknown, and yet well known; as dying, and, behold, we live; as chastened, and not killed;","10":"As sorrowful, yet alway rejoicing; as poor, yet making many rich; as having nothing, and yet possessing all things.","11":"O ye Corinthians, our mouth is open unto you, our heart is enlarged.","12":"Ye are not straitened in us, but ye are straitened in your own bowels.","13":"Now for a recompence in the same, (I speak as unto my children,) be ye also enlarged.","14":"Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?","15":"And what concord hath Christ with Belial? or what part hath he that believeth with an infidel?","16":"And what agreement hath the temple of God with idols? for ye are the temple of the living God; as God hath said, I will dwell in them, and walk in them; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.","17":"Wherefore come out from among them, and be ye separate, saith the Lord, and touch not the unclean thing; and I will receive you.","18":"And will be a Father unto you, and ye shall be my sons and daughters, saith the Lord Almighty."}},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul has given us important examples and encouragement about what a church leader is to look like, and how a minister is supposed to behave. If we are going to minister properly, we must lay everything down for those we claim to love and minister to. He talked about how we were supposed to be supported by our own kind, and not look for the approval of the world, while simultaneously being IN the world, but not OF the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul talks further about accepting people into the church. We cannot shun EVERYONE away, especially if we know they are repentant and growing. We should have an \"open-door\" policy in our churches, and we should be willing to show love to those that need it, accepting a changed heart and Spirit even if they had been an issue before. We are going to really see the heart and thoughts of Paul in this chapter, and it is something to take note of when we read ANY of Paul's other letters."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul has given us important examples and encouragement about what a church leader is to look like, and how a minister is supposed to behave. If we are going to minister properly, we must lay everything down for those we claim to love and minister to. He talked about how we were supposed to be supported by our own kind, and not look for the approval of the world, while simultaneously being IN the world, but not OF the world. We have to be able to separate ourselves from being like the world, yet reach the world. In this chapter, Paul talks further about accepting people into the church. We cannot shun EVERYONE away, especially if we know they are repentant and growing. We should have an \"open-door\" policy in our churches, and we should be willing to show love to those that need it, accepting a changed heart and Spirit even if they had been an issue before. We are going to really see the heart and thoughts of Paul in this chapter, and it is something to take note of when we read ANY of Paul's other letters."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Cleanse Ourselves","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"As long as you have breath, sin will be a problem to deal with."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those that know Christ understand the problem of sin. They understand that they must constantly battle with their flesh. The church’s responsibility is not to completely shun that man. Rather, the church is to CONFRONT that man with his sin. They must explain to him why it's a sin, and tell him to repent from it. Over and over again we are commanded to do this:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Brethren, if a man be overtaken in a fault, ye which are spiritual, restore such an one in the spirit of meekness; considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted.","ref":"Galatians 6:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him.","ref":"Luke 17:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"This church had to deal with that too, on several occasions. Again, they were surrounded by adultery and lust of all kinds, and it was something that a few of them were trying to accept in the church as well. All it did was cause more problems, and Paul explained to them in the first letter how to deal with it. He talked a lot about the dangers of prostitution and sexual immorality in the first letter."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems, now, that they have dealt with it for the most part, and there were people who have been out of the church (and have changed) who want back in and want to grow. Paul tells them that they should accept those back in. He has made that clear from the beginning of this letter."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers should have the spirit of forgiveness."},{"kind":"para","text":"They should have the Spirit of discernment, and they should be constantly checking themselves as well. We are more prone to sin than others. That is something we must focus on. I think a lot of ministers mean well while they are trying to confront others’ sins, but we must take a hard look at ourselves first before we bash other people for their wrongdoings. We must cleanse ourselves first, daily."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They should have the Spirit of discernment, and they should be constantly checking themselves as well. We (as ministers) are more prone to sin than others."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":4,"title":"To Love A Church","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Believe it or not, there are a lot of churches with a \"closed door\" policy. They are closed off to non-members. It goes directly against the church, if I can be honest."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"We had invited my wife's family to church a few times (my daughter was doing some things that I think they would have liked to see). We had a few events that were coming up, and simply asked if they wanted to join us. They agreed to it, and they showed up to each event. They ended up going back to their church, and their leader told them they are not allowed to visit other churches or they will lose their membership there. That seems odd, doesn't it?"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Fellowship is lost in religion today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul wants access to the church, and he (I believe) deserves access more than any other person in the Apostles. I believe he would be shunned by most major churches and religions today. They would close the door on him in a heartbeat. What Paul is getting at here, though, is that they should be welcoming to people who desire to grow and fellowship. Whether they are of the same cloth or not, does not matter. Actually, most of these people are likely to be Gentile believers anyway. They would know how it feels to be rejected from the religionists because that is their whole life."},{"kind":"para","text":"Churches should not be in the business of rejecting people, yet that is their biggest business. I don't know how many times I have been rejected from the church leadership for so many different things. Now, I will agree that there are times to reject someone, but that should be very few and far between, and it should only be because of a sinful attitude or problem that needs to be addressed first."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Every person should be vetted before including them in ministry work, but that does not mean we should just reject them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church will be glad to open their doors for Paul, and that is exactly how they should feel towards every desirous human that wants to join the church."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were right to get rid of the problem before. If they did not address the sin that was leaking into the church, it would certainly destroy the church quickly. They would become a cult, rather than a righteous body of believers. They had enough cults around them; they needed to be set apart for the truth, not for the world."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That is what a cult is, by the way. It's stating that you have truth, yet you are living for the world rather than for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Cults are people that say one thing and do the complete opposite. They are also people that go directly against the holiness of God. Cults don't keep scripture as their main source, and they don't follow Jesus as their salvation. You need both of those to be the church of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"So Paul knows that they did a good thing by dealing with the sin in the church, but now they should let that repentant sinner back in. That is the desire for a minister, by the way."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A genuine minister does not give up on a man when they are stuck in sin."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They will reach that man over and over again and encourage them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes you might have to back off a bit, but you should love them enough to accept them the moment they turn back to you."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church has really made an impression on Paul just as much as he has made an impression on them. It cannot be more evident that Paul loves this church. He isn't afraid to help them, speak up for them, speak up to their problems, give them solutions, and bring them comfort. The comfort of the church brings comfort to Paul. The tribulation of the church brings comfort to Paul when they are doing it together in connection and unity. That's a pretty good way to test your church."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If someone is going through a trial, does everyone feel that burden, or just that person alone? If we do not all ache together, it is very likely that there is no unity in the church, and that usually stems from poor, negligent leadership. To be able to go through tribulation and be \"exceeding joyful\" is a very obvious sign of a unified church. I don't know of any churches that could show this today."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":12,"title":"Resolve the Problem","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's kind of funny to me. When I became a Christian, the preachers that I learned from were really focused on making Paul out to be this \"Christian of Christians.\" He was the best example of a bold and passionate preacher. Yet, we have seen over and over again through these two letters that Paul had a soft heart. These letters (especially this second one) are the most genuinely personal letters from Paul that we can read. He says that he has had some troubles. He said a few chapters back that he wrote to them with \"tears\":"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote unto you with many tears; not that ye should be grieved, but that ye might know the love which I have more abundantly unto you.","ref":"2 Corinthians 2:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had his moments of boldness, no doubt, but they were only given to him in times of immense distress. It was the trials of the Lord that gave him room to be bold. There were fightings on the outside, and those fightings brought fear on the inside. Paul was a human being."},{"kind":"thought","text":"All pastors, mind you, are human beings. A pastor that acts like he's not is a faulty one to say the least."}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Paul had a lot on his plate. He was dealing with a lot of evil and wicked people around him in his traveling. He dealt with rejectful people. He had to deal with all sorts of beatings and scourging and the like (which we will get to in a moment), but the thing that kept Paul going was the spiritual growth of the church."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He was not concerned about adding numbers to the church nearly as much as adding change and maturity to it. That is all a church leader SHOULD be concerned about. It should be what keeps us going."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we care about numbers more than Spirit, we should find a new form of business."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Corporations care about numbers. They care about profits. They care about the number of employees. They care about franchising, dispersing, and financing. They care about how many charities they can give to, and how many eyes are watching them, and how they can gain more customers. These are all the opposite of what a church should be focused on."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is part of it. I have no reason to draw away from the business end of church, but that is not the main focus."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A genuine minister should want to add profit to the individuals in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"They should be concerned about how to add value to your soul. They should be focused on if you are connected, and what you are growing in. Paul has made that EXTREMELY clear through both letters. Actually, he makes it clear through ALL of his letters. He cares about the spiritual needs of the church far more than the physical needs (though he addresses the physical needs as well)."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Paul felt a little bad for the letter he wrote before. He probably thought he was being too harsh. He wrote it out of a genuine desire to show love and care for the church, and when he didn't hear anything back, he got a little worried. He would have beaten himself up if the church disbanded because he chose the wrong way of addressing his concerns. Obviously, the Lord used the letter, and it drew the church to repentance. Paul didn't know how the Lord would use it, but God used it mightily, and still uses it mightily today!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, hearing about the news of the church’s repentance, Paul knows it was the right call, and this second letter was sent to address his joy for them resolving the problem. He has reason to rejoice with them! He might be at a distance, but hearing the news gives him the ability to connect to their concerns and their successes."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That is interesting to me. He is able to connect with this church without ever seemingly being there physically. Yet we have a lot of pastors who can't connect with their own churches, and they are physically present with them most of the time. There's something wrong with that picture..."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Godly sorrow brings joy. Worldly sorrow brings death. Godly sorrow brings change. Worldly sorrow brings repetition. Godly sorrow brings freedom. Worldly sorrow brings imprisonment. Godly sorrow brings salvation. Worldly sorrow brings damnation. Godly sorrow brings peace. Worldly sorrow brings destruction. Godly sorrow brings direction. Worldly sorrow brings confusion."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul illustrates even further how the sorrow of a godly man will provide an advantage to the faith:"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Godly sorrow brings carefulness, clearness, indignation, fear, vehement desire, zeal, and revenge! Now, let's look at a few of these because I know some of you are reading it and saying, \"Those don't seem totally 'godly'.\" Carefulness is undisputedly a godly thing. Yet we are told (by Paul Himself) not to be \"careful\" for anything:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God.","ref":"Philippians 4:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"The premise of that verse, though (and it would be better to go through Philippians to dig deeper into this), is that we should not be \"full of care\" for the things of this world. Don't FOCUS (rather) on meaningless things that will last for a fleeting moment and then disappear."},{"kind":"para","text":"Having a \"clearing\" of ourselves is also a godly thing. Being able to have a clear mind is something that Paul has also spoken about:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind.","ref":"2 Timothy 1:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Being sound in mind is the same as being clear-headed. A clear head sounds like a well-oiled machine, whereas a foggy head sounds like grinding gears. \"Indignation\" is also godly. It actually BELONGS to the Lord. Any time it's mentioned in Scripture, it's usually associated with the Lord's cleansing and correcting. \"Indignation\" means \"irritation.\" When we irritate the Lord, He does something about it. You'd have to be pretty consistent to irritate God, though. But if we are indignant, we should be doing something about it too. We should be making godly corrections to relieve that irritation."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Fear\" is another godly attribute. We are told over and over again to \"fear\" the Lord. That is something we have studied quite a bit through Scripture already."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge: but fools despise wisdom and instruction.","ref":"Proverbs 1:7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 12:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then we have \"vehement desire.\" That is to say, a very strong necessity to do what's right. We have a calling and passion to obey the Lord! How is that NOT a good godly thing?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have “zeal.\" Zeal is usually a pretty good thing for anyone to have, especially a child of God. Sadly, a lot of \"Christians\" abuse their zeal for the Lord. They come off as calloused and cold, and make themselves unreachable. They create bubbles for themselves that make them completely separated from the rest of the world, including their brothers in Christ. You either live up to their legalistic standard, or you find a different place to be. This church had a zeal for the Lord that was moving in the right direction. It needed to be put into control, however, because they would be inclined to reject people who were genuine in the faith and abuse their zeal if they were not careful about it. Obviously, that won't be too much of a hassle because they already have a form of godly \"carefulness\" which was addressed at the beginning of this verse."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have \"revenge.\" How do you twist \"revenge\" into something godly? \"Revenge\" means \"punishment.\" They were able to deal with the sin problem in their punishment. They took the problem out of the church and waited for a repentant spirit. The man changed his spirit though, that was clear at the beginning of this letter. So they were clear about how to deal with the problem."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think churches are more than capable of \"dealing\" with problems still today. But we still have the same problems as the church of Corinth. We can deal with the problem, but we cannot resolve it. There is no resolution to problems in the church today. We deal with it by getting rid of it, and then if it's solved, we simply shun it and reject it for the rest of our lives. That doesn't bring resolution. Actually, it brings bitterness and strife. Those are MORE problems."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Getting rid of problems is not how you resolve problems."},{"kind":"para","text":"A resolution is actually confronting and working THROUGH the problem until it is complete, not just rejecting and ignoring it until it goes away."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"You deal with a problem by rejecting it, and you resolve a problem by completing it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those are the two options for every minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"The ministers always choose to \"deal\" with a problem instead of resolve it. That is what this church of Corinth needed to do."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That's what this second letter is all about. It's about how to be a genuine minister, and genuine ministers resolve problems, not just “deal” with them."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A leader can only lead by example."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We cannot tell people what to do; we must SHOW people what to do. We must be beside them in the work if we are going to lead them well. Leaders develop leaders."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is really the bottom line. That is what Paul is doing in this letter. He is showing how to genuinely minister so they know how to resolve their problems. They dealt with the problem in the first letter; now it's time to resolve it. Paul dealt with the problem of the church in the first letter, and this second letter displays how to resolve it."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"Boasting For Them","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, Titus was sent to them with the first letter, and Paul had moved around and waited for Titus to bring word back. It took a while for Titus to get to Paul, and I think that is what Paul is addressing here. He assumed because it took him so long that things were not working out for the church. But Titus brought good news back to Paul, and it took a huge amount of weight off of his shoulders."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know how Paul feels. Waiting for something so important is a heavy weight to bear. The Lord does that a lot. This was a serious matter. I have been told that I take ministry too seriously. Honestly, I do take ministry seriously. But I don't look at ministry like these other people do."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It seems these ministers today look at ministry as a job that you check off. You go in, complete your to-do list, get paid, and go home. That is ministry in the eyes of the church leaders today. Rather, these are the SOULS OF PEOPLE we are talking about. It's not just something you can push out of the way or check off your list for the day. This is a very serious matter. We are talking about life and death, both literally and spiritually."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why should we not take that seriously? There's nothing more serious and needful than to address a human's soul matter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.","ref":"Matthew 10:28"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For in death there is no remembrance of thee: in the grave who shall give thee thanks?","ref":"Psalm 6:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For what is a man profited, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?","ref":"Matthew 16:26"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing more valuable in this world than a human soul, and yet we have ministers who treat ministry as if it's just a job you can clock in and out of. What a shame... Paul was not that way, though. Paul wasn't even getting paid for his ministering except through donations from time to time. The Philippian church was good at that, but we can talk about that later."}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul may have written a letter that seemed quite condemning, but Paul really loved this church. He loved that it existed in such a difficult territory. No one else would have dared to start a church there. No one else would have what it took to get something started there that would switch the culture. So to Titus, he had boasted about them before sending him off, and he is boasting about them now that they have listened to Paul's first letter. He's not ashamed of that fact. They have proved that his boasting of them was not in vain. We can think of boasting as a prideful thing. But it's only prideful if you turn it onto yourself."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It is completely okay to boast about the good things others have done."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, they need to hear boasting from time to time. That will provide them with some encouragement and necessity. It's a good ministry to have as well."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Pointing boasts to others who need to hear that they are doing a good job will help them to do it more."},{"kind":"para","text":"Otherwise, they may eventually feel as if they are wasting their time if all they hear is what they are doing wrong and nothing about what they are doing right. That's in all forms of leadership, but it's especially important in the church."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul didn't have too much confidence the first time around. He was not sure what was going to become of them if they didn't deal with their sin problem. Now they have proven their faith. Paul can officially sit comfortably and confidently in their moving and their ministry. It gave him immense joy and boldness to see this church take ministry seriously. That is what keeps all ministers going. That is the greatest joy a minister can experience."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16}],"verses":{"1":"Having therefore these promises, dearly beloved, let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God.","2":"Receive us; we have wronged no man, we have corrupted no man, we have defrauded no man.","3":"I speak not this to condemn you: for I have said before, that ye are in our hearts to die and live with you.","4":"Great is my boldness of speech toward you, great is my glorying of you: I am filled with comfort, I am exceeding joyful in all our tribulation.","5":"For, when we were come into Macedonia, our flesh had no rest, but we were troubled on every side; without were fightings, within were fears.","6":"Nevertheless God, that comforteth those that are cast down, comforted us by the coming of Titus;","7":"And not by his coming only, but by the consolation wherewith he was comforted in you, when he told us your earnest desire, your mourning, your fervent mind toward me; so that I rejoiced the more.","8":"For though I made you sorry with a letter, I do not repent, though I did repent: for I perceive that the same epistle hath made you sorry, though it were but for a season.","9":"Now I rejoice, not that ye were made sorry, but that ye sorrowed to repentance: for ye were made sorry after a godly manner, that ye might receive damage by us in nothing.","10":"For godly sorrow worketh repentance to salvation not to be repented of: but the sorrow of the world worketh death.","11":"For behold this selfsame thing, that ye sorrowed after a godly sort, what carefulness it wrought in you, yea, what clearing of yourselves, yea, what indignation, yea, what fear, yea, what vehement desire, yea, what zeal, yea, what revenge! In all things ye have approved yourselves to be clear in this matter.","12":"Wherefore, though I wrote unto you, I did it not for his cause that had done the wrong, nor for his cause that suffered wrong, but that our care for you in the sight of God might appear unto you.","13":"Therefore we were comforted in your comfort: yea, and exceedingly the more joyed we for the joy of Titus, because his spirit was refreshed by you all.","14":"For if I have boasted any thing to him of you, I am not ashamed; but as we spake all things to you in truth, even so our boasting, which I made before Titus, is found a truth.","15":"And his inward affection is more abundant toward you, whilst he remembereth the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling ye received him.","16":"I rejoice therefore that I have confidence in you in all things."}},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul talked about the importance of a minister checking himself for ministry. We cannot just tell people to clean up their lives if we are not first cleaning up our own lives. Then he spoke about the importance of a minister loving the church and being open to accepting repentant sinners back into the church. He talked about the importance of resolving a problem instead of just \"dealing\" with the problem. And finally, he ended the chapter with his boasting for them."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, it will seem as if Paul's ADD is acting up, and he is getting off track. He will talk about giving towards other churches and ministries. He will use the church in Macedonia as an example of proper giving and then make it an important reminder to the Corinthian church to keep their promises on giving. It will sound as if Paul is not connecting the rest of his letter here, but honestly, I think it falls under the same category as ministry. Giving is another facet of ministry. It's one that Christians have a hard time talking about, and it's certainly one they have a hard time doing. I hope to open this up and clarify where giving sits rightfully in the church and help us to understand what that might look like in our own personal walks and purposes."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul talked about the importance of a minister checking himself for ministry. We cannot just tell people to clean up their lives if we are not first cleaning up our own lives. Then he spoke about the importance of a minister loving the church and being open to accepting repentant sinners back into the church. He talked about the importance of resolving a problem instead of just \"dealing\" with the problem. And finally, he ended the chapter with his boasting for them. In this chapter, it will seem as if Paul's ADD is acting up, and he is getting off track. He will talk about giving towards other churches and ministries. He will use the church in Macedonia as an example of proper giving and then make it an important reminder to the Corinthian church to keep their promises on giving. It will sound as if Paul is not connecting the rest of his letter here, but honestly, I think it falls under the same category as ministry. Giving is another facet of ministry. It's one that Christians have a hard time talking about, and it's certainly one they have a hard time doing. I hope to open this up and clarify where giving sits rightfully in the church and help us to understand what that might look like in our own personal walks and purposes. A minister should have a heart to give."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"A Heart Network","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this is something that is still very personal to Paul as he's writing this letter. The church has been encouraged to minister responsibly, and now that is the theme of the remainder of this book. Part of ministering responsibly is through grace and giving. The ministers should have a mode of giving, and Paul says that the church in Macedonia gave during a great time of trial. This is while they didn't have much to give to begin with, mind you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our current church is not a rich one by any means. Yet we are able to afford (by God's miraculous work) to build a building and purchase a property. Our church has grown immensely, and our Pastor does not preach on giving very often. I think it's foolish for a Pastor to preach on giving frequently. If there is a need that needs to be met, then giving is certainly a good call. Whether it's giving your finances or giving yourselves, there is room for a growing church to be giving. Even if it's not much compared to some churches, giving something is better than nothing."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"When I was a teenager, I worked in construction for a few years. I helped build and reconstruct retirement homes in San Francisco, and we had to rebuild decks over there. It required us to carry a lot of lumber from one end of the parking lot all the way over to the other side of the building complex. It was a long walk. Most of the adults were carrying six 2x4 planks that were about fifteen feet long. I could not do that. I wasn't very strong. So I tried, and I did it for about a half an hour, but after that, my arms were growing far too tired. I carried around twenty-four planks of wood in thirty minutes (that's how much walking we had to do). I was exhausted, and one of the people on the job said, \"You don't have to carry that many. I can tell you are getting tired quickly, and we still have ten more hours of work. Just carry one plank of wood; one plank is better than nothing, and it still progresses the job.\" So that's what I started to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"We could apply that logic to our church giving and church work. It doesn't have to be done in a hurry; we just have to be making progress."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Small progress is always greatly used by the Lord, and it would be good for us to take one step at a time rather than long-jumping our way through ministry."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some churches don't have the privilege to be directly involved in the work. That's okay. When we know a church plant needs help, we can send laborers, and we can send funds. The help that the saints needed was something dire, I have no doubt about that, and so the church in Macedonia sent what they could as quickly as they could. They were willing to do whatever it takes to meet needs because they loved the saints in the other churches. Specifically for the sake of Paul and the likes who helped them start their church to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are at a bit of a disadvantage today because there are far more churches than there were back then. If we were going to meet the needs of our brother churches, we would have to be giving more than we are able. It would be impossible and endless. That is why there are a lot of churches who just stick to a small group of other churches to build off of each other from, or they are not involved with other churches at all. Actually, there's nothing more miserable in our churches today than to be a church that is alone in the work. There are no loving churches that are willing to carry the burden with other churches today. These churches are a great representation of what the churches are supposed to be today, though."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It's a church network, not just a church hub. Every church is an individual heartbeat."},{"kind":"para","text":"Actually, every church is better represented (according to scripture) as a beacon of hope. They use \"candlesticks\" in scripture (specifically in Revelation). The point is, we are supposed to be lighting up this world with the gospel of peace, and that is the sole responsibility for every church."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"When we were moving to Texas, we made specific stops to stay on our journey. We didn't want to spend the entire twenty-two hours driving straight through to it (especially because we were pulling a trailer full of our stuff). We made specific and purposeful stops so we had a place to rest and recover. That is what churches are to be."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are traveling in the darkness of this world, and churches are supposed to be places of safety and recovery for the Christian."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, though, most churches have a \"closed-door\" policy, and so you are not invited into their recovery unless you are part of their clique."},{"kind":"para","text":"I like the heartbeat analogy because it shows the necessity a little deeper for the church’s existence."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are multiple hearts connected to the same network. If one heart goes out, the whole network will feel it. We are the blood running through the veins of the church’s network, and we should notice when there is bradycardia (or arrhythmia)."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bradycardia\" comes from two Greek words: “bradys,\" which means \"slow,“ and “kardia,\" which means \"heart.\" We should also feel it when there is complete and utter heart failure. I think all the churches of the Apostles would have felt it if the Corinthian church went under. They might have been falling apart in a few areas, and I think the other churches were feeling that. I know for sure Paul was feeling that. It's why he prescribed them some help for their bradycardia. They needed a refresher."},{"kind":"para","text":"So this church at Macedonia shows how necessary it is to keep the heart pumping and the blood flowing even if they didn't have much to give. Almost like a hospital with limited resources. In an emergency, it's better than nothing. Of course, we want a hospital that has all the resources necessary to heal you, but sometimes you are going to have to settle with a simple, lowly hospital to keep you alive in an emergency."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"In order to give yourself properly to others, you must first give yourself to the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a good rule of thumb when making any sort of decision in life and ministry. We should be giving our choices to the Lord. Actually, we should be GETTING our choices from the Lord. In all situations, we should be asking, “Lord, what would you have me to do with this? Give me the choices, and help me to take the path that leads to your glory.\" This church gave themselves to the Lord, and then accepted the Lord's leading in giving themselves to the ministry. What they gave was an agreed-upon amount in their prayers together to the Lord."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, this giving sparked something in Paul. He says, \"Their giving increased their faith and connection to us, and that is what I want to happen with you guys.\" He hasn't been able to come to this church in a while, let's not forget. He has desired to go to them, but some things have held him back. He has come to the church twice already and is looking to come a third time now that things have grown and changed. But those bumps and obstacles are not going to keep him from doing whatever it takes to connect to this church as much as possible. This was one way that the church in Macedonia showed connection, and this is the way in which the Corinthian church could connect if they so desired."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Better than that (and this would be disagreed among modern ministers today) would be if they could be with Paul physically. Their physical work was better than their financial ones."},{"kind":"para","text":"But we should all understand that not everyone has the ability or capacity to travel like the Apostles are. In fact, the Apostles are doing this traveling and living in discomfort so the church won't have to. So we can give to those that are sent out in order for that church to progress and for the churches to work together as one unit."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This grace would be in giving. They are already giving their faith, utterance (that is encouragement), knowledge (that is growth), diligence (that is consideration), and love to the Apostles, and the one thing more they could add to that is “this grace.\" The grace of giving. That is something we should add to our abundance of faith."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Even if it's not financial, I think we all have room to give a little more grace to others (especially those in the faith)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The most graceful of us should be the ministers. They (I'm sorry to say) are the least graceful in most cases. They are too high and mighty for the lowly sinner to reach. I'm so thankful the Apostle Paul did not do that to this church."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":15,"title":"Giving Limit","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is not asking for this out of law, or out of orders from his superiors, but out of love. He wants them to show how much love they have for him and the other Apostles in return. Let us understand that this church in Corinth is rich! They have plenty to spare. It was not as if they were in the middle of a small town, or a dying village. No, as I've said before, Corinth was a very rich city. It was a lot like Las Vegas, Nevada is today. They have lots of money, and lots of sin. To plant a church right in the middle of the Las Vegas Strip would be a sight to behold. It would also be a very difficult ministry to lead. There would be so much influence from the drugs, prostitutes, alcohol, and money that is being pumped through that land that it would undoubtedly slip into the church and be nearly impossible to get out."},{"kind":"para","text":"So is Paul saying that if they don't give, they don't really love him? No. That's not what Paul is getting at here at all. He is saying that they can increase and PROVE their love in this way. There are plenty of other ways for this church to prove it, but this would be the safest and easiest way (plus, they have already promised it a year ago). Paul is not taking this money to himself, by the way. He is taking this gift to provide for other churches who need it."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Again, the Apostles were like the blood flowing through the veins of each church’s heart. They were keeping the hearts pumping. They would be giving their time, energy, and finances specifically to the ministry and growing the ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"They wanted to spend as much time as possible supporting and loving people who were far worse off than the church at Corinth. The Corinthian church could be a part of that, and they could prove their love goes even deeper than their assets on Earth. But even THIS will have to be done in faith."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"How was He rich?"},{"kind":"thought","text":"When we read about the life of Jesus through the gospel accounts, He did not live like a rich man. He didn't own any houses or land. He didn't have a big business venture. He didn't have a nice car, or live in royalty. He didn't have a bed to even sleep in. Half the time He couldn't even afford to go buy food. So how does Paul figure that Jesus was rich? Well, He was rich in three ways."},{"kind":"para","text":"The first way He was rich was when He was in eternity. He left Heaven to come to Earth and live as a poor man, that is just the truth. He lived poorer than poor, and yet He was able to minister to the poor."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second way was that the Magi that gave Him gifts, gave Him gifts that would have made Him rich. So what happened to those gifts? Well, I don't know. I assume they were used in some way by His parents. They were used to help support themselves to raise the Lord. But even if they were rich as He entered into His adulthood, He rejected the finances and walked away from it to minister to the poor. He did not take any money with Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The third, and final, way in which we could acclaim His richness was the fact that He had the wealth of God with Him every step of the way in His life. He had the Lord providing for Him, and yet He lived without that richness. He survived on the providence of God, but lived like a poor man to enrich the poor. He could have lived like a rich man, but He did not want to reach the rich. The rich wouldn't want to be reached anyway. It's the poor that He came after. He lived that way to give US riches."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Everything that He had, He gave to those that He ministered to. That is what a real minister looks like."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul points out their richness and reminds them that they could be real ministers with that. They could turn around and use it for those that need it rather than put it all back into themselves and disconnect themselves from all the other churches. They would be wise to steward their churches for others instead of for themselves. Modern churches hoard their finances. They build their fancy churches. They then fail, and the whole world laughs at them, rejects them, and calls them a cult. They might mean well, but they are selfish with their money, and all they are out for (obviously) is personal financial gain. That is not a real church. That's (once again) a business."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"My dad has a saying that resembles this but it's too inappropriate to share here. What I will say, though, is God takes commitments seriously. If a church (or a Christian for that matter) says he will do something, then he better do it. Over and over again God repeats this rule."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If a man vow a vow unto the LORD, or swear an oath to bind his soul with a bond; he shall not break his word, he shall do according to all that proceedeth out of his mouth.","ref":"Numbers 30:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay: for whatsoever is more than these cometh of evil.","ref":"Matthew 5:37"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But above all things, my brethren, swear not, neither by heaven, neither by the earth, neither by any other oath: but let your yea be yea; and your nay, nay; lest ye fall into condemnation.","ref":"James 5:12"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When thou vowest a vow unto God, defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed. Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 5:4-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is really the key verse here."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"As a Christian, every promise you make is to the Lord first, then it's to others."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is why Paul told this church that the Macedonian church gave themselves to GOD first. That is what we must do if we are going to make any sort of promise, and then we must keep it. Keeping a promise to God is far more serious (at least it should be) in our eyes than what we might promise to others. I mean, this is the all-powerful God we are talking about here."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I think that is a pretty prominent sign in a \"man of God.\" If they make promises that they never intend to keep, there is something wrong with their viewpoint on Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So I guess the real question is, is it okay for a Christian to NOT keep a promise? Is there some level of space given to a Christian for not keeping to his word? To the world, no. The world will call you a hypocrite for not doing what you say. They already have as many excuses as possible to try and put you down, and they are just looking for another one."},{"kind":"thought","text":"By not keeping your promise, you give them ammunition to say, \"I knew you would be this way. You are all the same...\""},{"kind":"para","text":"To the Christian, yes. I think there is wiggle room for forgiveness. The world cannot forgive, but the Christian certainly can. We should be giving every Christian the benefit of the doubt. However, we don't lean on the promises of Christians. We lean on the promises of God alone."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It is a real shame that there would be Christians who make a habit of not keeping promises."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a very sad theme that happens quite a lot actually. We have ministers who are not interested in keeping promises today. They are sly, and they make some very good promises, but they don't plan on keeping them. Perhaps they mean well, but they don't follow through. If you are a minister, please be sure to follow through with what you say you're going to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think Paul forgave this church for not keeping to their promise. He wasn't going to lean on this church’s providence. Ultimately, providence comes from the Lord, and if this church was not willing to give themselves over to the Lord first, that was between them and the Lord, not Paul and them. Paul is just making it a point to warn them about the necessity to keep to their word."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an important rule for all of us to follow as well."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Don't over-give. Do what you can with what you have. That includes in your service."},{"kind":"para","text":"I am guilty (as many other ministers) of giving everything I have over to the church and MORE."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"When we started attending this church in Palmdale, I thought to myself \"I will not give more than what I have. I have already done that in Texas and it was brutal. I missed out on so many family opportunities for it. I even gave up my anniversary to be at the church. I won't do that again.\" Then I ended up cancelling family plans, and moving family functions and all sorts of things just so I could help serve the church. They were always calling me and asking me to fill in somewhere. I did that for three years and you know what it got me? It got me nowhere. The church isn't any more useful with me in it than it was for me out of it."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Give yourself as much as can, but don't OVER-give yourself."},{"kind":"para","text":"Your family comes first. The people you LOVE come first. Always and forever. If you have to cancel on them for church ministry you will always miss out on the necessities and you will be ashamed for robbing your family of your much-needed presence."}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Over-giving yourself will bring a burden on you. That is not what the church ministry should bring. If you feel burdened after giving (money or time), then you are over-giving, and you should draw back. The important thing for you to do is to be WISE with what you are giving."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"This is for every minister: church is not your life. If church is your life, you are failing the Lord. God IS your life, and if He doesn't come before your church, you will add great burden to yourself."},{"kind":"para","text":"That burden will weigh down so heavily on you over time that once you get to a breaking point, you will be crushed under the pressure. Don't let it get that far. If you have chosen to let it get that far, take some time off. Take a break, and use that break to reorganize and replan for everything, putting things in proper order."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":24,"title":"Glory to God","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have had a bit of a background character this entire time in the books of Corinthians. He has been mentioned over and over again as the messenger of the church. He's run back and forth to give an account to Paul about this church. You don't do something like that unless you genuinely care and love the church as well. It seems Titus was just as much hopeful, and just as much interested in this church as Paul was. I think Titus learned to love this church because of his visiting of it. He was seeing the change in it firsthand, and that made him compassionate towards them. Paul is thankful for a co-laborer with a growing heart for people. Titus would have made a great pastor to them as well."}]},{"start":17,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know what friend Titus brought with him, but they were both excited to see this church grow and praise the Lord."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"That is, ultimately, what the ministers should want to see in a growing church. Not just growth in the number of people, but growth in the praise of God's glory."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's the encouragement from ministers."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The encouragement is that we would give to God's glory. We would work to God's glory. We would unite to God's glory. We would strive to God's glory. We would continue to God's glory. We would correct to God's glory. We would think to God's glory. We would succeed to God's glory. We would fail to God's glory. We would be humbled to God's glory. We would be lifted to God's glory. We would FOCUS to God's glory. Everything and anything worth the while in this Christian life should be to God's glory."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the ministers are given anything, it should be proven what it is being used for. The people should know that the ministers are being honest. Everything should be accounted for. The church should audit the leaders with everything they are giving. Sadly, the leaders leave some things out. I've also known some leaders that have pocketed the money. I have no doubt that the Lord will deal with those people. But Paul wants to be transparent with it. He wants to prove his honesty before God, AND before men. He wants everyone to know where that gift of ministry is going, and he wants them to know so that they can continue to be involved with what they WANT to be involved in."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Titus is a proven man. He has been proven for years. It was not as if Paul was sending a newbie to minister to this church. He was not sending an unvetted man. I have already talked about the importance of vetting ministers before putting them in ministry."},{"kind":"thought","text":"You'd be shocked at how often churches get people involved in leadership who do not belong in leadership. They have bad doctrine, they are not able to minister with compassion, and they are always in a rush. That is not a good ministry outlook to have..."},{"kind":"para","text":"But Titus was a proven man. And if anyone was sent from Titus, Titus will give an account for that person. He is responsible for picking trusted people. So if there is someone that comes through who is not trusted, the person to blame would be Titus, and then Paul would have to come into the picture to pick up the pieces."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are proven men. Paul wants this church to be a proven church. Paul has really talked this church up, and he wants them to prove his own words. He believes this is a trusted church that could have a very wide reach if they are willing to give of themselves more. But that ultimately isn't up to Paul. All Paul can do is encourage their love and faith. From there, they have to change and adapt accordingly. They have to either listen or shun the advice of Paul. They had already taken Paul's advice before though, and it worked well for them. So there would be no reason for them not to take the advice of Paul again and go even further. Paul was a wise man, and he has made it clear that he loves this church too much to let them just dangle and free-fall. So he's doing whatever it takes to get them moving forward."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"Moreover, brethren, we do you to wit of the grace of God bestowed on the churches of Macedonia;","2":"How that in a great trial of affliction the abundance of their joy and their deep poverty abounded unto the riches of their liberality.","3":"For to their power, I bear record, yea, and beyond their power they were willing of themselves;","4":"Praying us with much intreaty that we would receive the gift, and take upon us the fellowship of the ministering to the saints.","5":"And this they did, not as we hoped, but first gave their own selves to the Lord, and unto us by the will of God.","6":"Insomuch that we desired Titus, that as he had begun, so he would also finish in you the same grace also.","7":"Therefore, as ye abound in every thing, in faith, and utterance, and knowledge, and in all diligence, and in your love to us, see that ye abound in this grace also.","8":"I speak not by commandment, but by occasion of the forwardness of others, and to prove the sincerity of your love.","9":"For ye know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that, though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich.","10":"And herein I give my advice: for this is expedient for you, who have begun before, not only to do, but also to be forward a year ago.","11":"Now therefore perform the doing of it; that as there was a readiness to will, so there may be a performance also out of that which ye have.","12":"For if there be first a willing mind, it is accepted according to that a man hath, and not according to that he hath not.","13":"For I mean not that other men be eased, and ye burdened:","14":"But by an equality, that now at this time your abundance may be a supply for their want, that their abundance also may be a supply for your want: that there may be equality:","15":"As it is written, He that had gathered much had nothing over; and he that had gathered little had no lack.","16":"But thanks be to God, which put the same earnest care into the heart of Titus for you.","17":"For indeed he accepted the exhortation; but being more forward, of his own accord he went unto you.","18":"And we have sent with him the brother, whose praise is in the gospel throughout all the churches;","19":"And not that only, but who was also chosen of the churches to travel with us with this grace, which is administered by us to the glory of the same Lord, and declaration of your ready mind:","20":"Avoiding this, that no man should blame us in this abundance which is administered by us:","21":"Providing for honest things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men.","22":"And we have sent with them our brother, whom we have oftentimes proved diligent in many things, but now much more diligent, upon the great confidence which I have in you.","23":"Whether any do enquire of Titus, he is my partner and fellowhelper concerning you: or our brethren be enquired of, they are the messengers of the churches, and the glory of Christ.","24":"Wherefore shew ye to them, and before the churches, the proof of your love, and of our boasting on your behalf."}},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul spoke pretty prominently about giving from a church’s point of view. It seems that the church in Corinth had made a prior promise to give something to the church in Jerusalem, and Paul reminded them about that and keeping their promise. He talked about the necessity of promise-keeping, but he also talked about how good it is to desire to give."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul hits that same theme again, but he includes it in a ministry standpoint. Why does the church need the support? It's not just for personal gain or for better worldly living. The support for the church goes BACK into the church. The church will be supplied by the Lord, but the ministers should want to give to their brothers’ needs to progress the ministry further."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul spoke pretty prominently about giving from a church’s point of view. It seems that the church in Corinth had made a prior promise to give something to the church in Jerusalem, and Paul reminded them about that and keeping their promise. He talked about the necessity of promise-keeping, but he also talked about how good it is to desire to give. In this chapter, Paul hits that same theme again, but he includes it in a ministry standpoint. Why does the church need the support? It's not just for personal gain or for better worldly living. The support for the church goes BACK into the church. The church will be supplied by the Lord, but the ministers should want to give to their brothers’ needs to progress the ministry further. Giving from the Lord will be a blessing from the Lord."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Fear or Faith","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Superfluous\" means \"over and above, more than is necessary.\" We have made it a clear and obvious picture that Paul has written about their great efforts in ministering. That's the premise of this letter. He has already mentioned the necessary steps that a minister should take to minister properly."},{"kind":"thought","text":"So writing further about this is above and beyond the need, but I am so thankful he writes more about it. What an extremely useful letter for the churches today."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So a year ago, Corinth promised to give. Paul used that promise to the church in Macedonia, and that church was encouraged by the promise of Corinth. That is what Paul means by Achaia, by the way. Achaia was where the church was located in Corinth."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Blissful and blessed zeal is contagious, mind you. When one person has the boldness to do something bigger than themselves, it encourages others to desire that as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"The problem is, most people won't follow through with it until they see someone else do it. The Macedonian church gave based on boldness, though, not on proof. The church in Corinth was going to have to prove themselves now that Paul has boasted about them and revealed their promise. This is another reason you shouldn't make a promise you can't keep. Promises will get other people involved, and it will be more than one person that is discouraged by a broken promise if you are not careful."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is incredible here."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul puts himself and Titus as part of THEIR promise. If they don't keep their promise, Paul and Titus will share in the responsibility of the damage that will be done. They don't want to cause damage though, and that is why Paul is making it a critical point that the church KEEPS their promise. If not for the saints that are going to suffer for their negligence, at the very least for the Lord whose name they are dishonoring."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the name in which you make your promises if you don't remember. That's what the last chapter was about."},{"kind":"para","text":"Breaking promises in the name of the Lord is a disgraceful action and deserves to be scrutinized and judged. The CHURCH should be the judges of such matters though. Sadly, the church just lets it go. They deal with the problem, but they don't resolve it (something we've already talked about a couple of chapters back). So the next judges are the world. It's funny, because the world says, \"You can't judge me!\" Yet that is all they do to the Christian. They get upset at Christians for making judgments, and then use all their free time to judge the Christians."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If we do judge, we should start with ourselves first, and then make our judgments from there."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if we would judge ourselves, we should not be judged. But when we are judged, we are chastened of the Lord, that we should not be condemned with the world.","ref":"1 Corinthians 11:31-32"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is not wanting their money for themselves. Paul just wants them to set the example and keep to their promises. That is a very useful theme to the minister."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers should keep promises."},{"kind":"para","text":"I can't stress this enough. Paul can't stress it enough either. It really has nothing to do with what Paul wants. He would be fine if the church never offered anything, but the church did, and they have to keep to their promise of support. So Paul is sending Titus and the likes to go and help this church keep to their end of the deal. Not out of \"covetousness\" but out of \"bounty.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"That is how the church said they would give. They are not giving out of necessity, they are giving out of want. They WANT to help other churches. They WANT to help the ministers serve. They WANT them to do it MORE, not less. That is the only reason we should be giving as well."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We should give because we WANT to, not because we HAVE to, or we may feel FORCED to."},{"kind":"para","text":"The church in Corinth had a desire, but they did not keep to that desire."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation: the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.","ref":"Matthew 26:41"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think desire is important. Having a want to do something for the glory of God is certainly noble and respected. Following through is the issue with all of us, though. We usually don't follow through because we are worried. Fear keeps us from doing what we want to do. It's okay to be afraid, mind you. It's okay to have a healthy fear. It's okay to fear skydiving without a parachute. It's okay to fear swimming with sharks or playing on a busy freeway. It's okay to fear being trapped underwater without oxygen. It's okay to fear being stranded in the desert with no water. These are healthy fears. But these fears should not keep you back from exploring and trying. That is what fear does to a Christian, though. They are afraid to be rejected, so they don't try at all. They are afraid that they will regret their decision, so it's better to just sit in comfort than to put yourself out there. That is not the way to address your fear. Don't let fear hold you back. Let God bring you forward."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":15,"title":"Gifted and Gifting Ministers","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very simple thought. Honestly, it should not even be said."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We should all understand that the best way to receive increase is by giving more. If you throw two seeds out and water those two seeds, you are probably not going to get very many fruits growing. If you throw out twenty, you will probably have a few more than if you were to throw out two. Increase that more and more, and your chances are far greater. That is how it is in the faith as well."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If you want to receive a greater faith, you must give a greater amount of yourself."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is such a hard thing for us to do. It's a hard thing for me to do. If you want to minister more, you have to give more of yourself. You have to take more rejection and pain. That is the minister’s life. How horribly difficult that is. Yet, how abundantly rewarding it will be in the future."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"I have been beaten down by so many members of the church, yet it's the ones that I have given myself to (above and beyond) that show benefits from my ministering. I wouldn't know that impact until after being hurt and rejected. The teens in Texas taught me a lot about what I taught about. They would bring things up that I don't even remember teaching, and would remind me that it was ME that taught them that. Sometimes I need to hear my own lessons again."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a wonderful thing to hear from people that have learned from you, using what they have learned. The more you do it, the more it pays off. That is the same with giving anything to anyone or to the church."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"scripture","text":"For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.","ref":"Matthew 6:21"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Your heart for giving is based on your heart for the Lord. If the Lord does not have your heart, you will not give FROM the heart."},{"kind":"para","text":"If it were possible for you to give from the heart, it would not be a humble heart. You would be giving from a heart of pride, and that won't be worth much. Even if you gave millions of dollars to the church, you would actually be hurting the reputation of the church by your heartless giving."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I think it's dangerous to accept money from non-members of a church. I think if we are going to receive any gift in the church, it should be from proven and vetted members. I hold that to be true in placing them in ministry, and I think it's just as important for their giving. I think the message that the Pastors should be preaching is, \"If you are not a member of any church, do not give to our church.\" We should only be accepting \"cheerful\" givers. That can only happen from a heart given to the Lord first. Paul has pounded that thought in over and over again in this letter. How dangerous it is to accept a heartless gift into the church’s finances and ministry."}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"God blesses a faithful giver. They don't have to be consistent givers; they just have to give by faith."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I want us to recognize that I have not talked about tithing once since the beginning of this \"giving\" ministry was mentioned. I think tithing is a very different topic that we can talk about in a different setting. This is just talking about \"giving\" specifically."},{"kind":"para","text":"God loves people that are willing to give for the ministry and God's work. God blesses those that are faithful."},{"kind":"para","text":"So those that are not full of faith are not going to receive a blessing from the Lord. Why would they? They don't believe the Lord is going to provide for them anyway. I don't know why people that are not of the faith give to a church. It's a very strange thing for them to do."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I think many of them believe they are going to receive a blessing if they do it. They won't. The only blessing they can receive is through their salvation, and if they are not willing to take that, then the blessings stop right there no matter how much they give. You don't give to earn something from the Lord. You give because you already LOVE the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is written in Psalm 112 by the way:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He hath dispersed, he hath given to the poor; his righteousness endureth for ever; his horn shall be exalted with honour.","ref":"Psalm 112:9"},{"kind":"thought","text":"When a farmer plants seed, he does it by faith that it will grow. It might not be a complete trust that the seed will do it because the seed has said it would, but it's a complete trust that something will happen, and they are expecting that the seed will do what it's supposed to. Sometimes it doesn't work, but that doesn't stop the farmer from trying. Other times it works more than he thought was capable. Either way, the farmer continues to plant, and continues to hope for what he has planted. That is how giving to the Lord’s work looks. We sow seeds trusting the Lord will do something with it, and we know (for a fact) that God WILL do something with it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven, and returneth not thither, but watereth the earth, and maketh it bring forth and bud, that it may give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater: So shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it.","ref":"Isaiah 55:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"He WILL bless when we go into His Work with HIM in mind and faith in His Will. If we go in without a belief, we are wasting our time and money."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God proves His bountifulness. He proves His \"enrichment.\" He GIVES us reason to thank Him."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If you are a Christian, and you give to the Lord and it doesn't pan out the way you had hoped, and you find reason to NOT thank Him, you were not giving to Him for the right reason. That will be your fruit. Your fruit will be the fruit of bitterness. You sowed bitterness to begin with, so that will be what you harvest."}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Giving to ministers is a great way to encourage their work. If they are doing good work, then it's a good idea to give a good amount of time and money to their continued labor."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Ministers are abused. Real ministers usually are. There is no amount of money that could resolve that or help them take the hit."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"But when you give your time or money to a minister, you are saying, \"Thank you for taking the hit for me. Thank you for always being willing to lay down your life so that I can live comfortably in mine. Thank you for taking the time and effort to pour into me so that I can have it a little bit easier in my walk with the Lord.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"If they are not doing things to help you in your faith walk, then it's probably best that you don't give to their needs. They aren't doing anything to really help you, so why pay them to be lazy un-ministers? That is what I will call them from here on out. Fake ministers are \"un-ministers.\" They undo what the Lord has been working in the lives of believers for so long. They are actually enemies of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Real ministers are as Paul labels them here. They are abundant servers; they are grace suppliers; they are ministry experimenters (people that are willing to try whatever they can to reach more people, even putting their lives in harm’s way). They are God glorifiers; they are professed subjectors (willing to go out of their way for people, even if people don't come to them first). They are gospel preachers; they are consistent prayers; they are people who live for the ministry, and not just for themselves. They honestly can't help themselves. They are people who have known the gift of God (that is, His salvation) and they want more than anything to share that gift with others through serving others:"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Fake ministers are \"un-ministers.\" They undo what the Lord has been working in the lives of believers for so long. They are actually enemies of the Lord. Real ministers are as Paul labels them here. They are abundant servers; they are grace suppliers; they are ministry experimenters (people that are willing to try whatever they can to reach more people, even putting their lives in harm’s way). They are God glorifiers; they are professed subjectors (willing to go out of their way for people, even if people don't come to them first). They are gospel preachers; they are consistent prayers; they are people who live for the ministry, and not just for themselves."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a great verse! I don't have to say any more than that."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"God's gift is the greatest gift, and there is no way we can ever out-give what He has already given to us."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15}],"verses":{"1":"For as touching the ministering to the saints, it is superfluous for me to write to you:","2":"For I know the forwardness of your mind, for which I boast of you to them of Macedonia, that Achaia was ready a year ago; and your zeal hath provoked very many.","3":"Yet have I sent the brethren, lest our boasting of you should be in vain in this behalf; that, as I said, ye may be ready:","4":"Lest haply if they of Macedonia come with me, and find you unprepared, we (that we say not, ye) should be ashamed in this same confident boasting.","5":"Therefore I thought it necessary to exhort the brethren, that they would go before unto you, and make up beforehand your bounty, whereof ye had notice before, that the same might be ready, as a matter of bounty, and not as of covetousness.","6":"But this I say, He which soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly; and he which soweth bountifully shall reap also bountifully.","7":"Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.","8":"And God is able to make all grace abound toward you; that ye, always having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work:","9":"(As it is written, He hath dispersed abroad; he hath given to the poor: his righteousness remaineth for ever.","10":"Now he that ministereth seed to the sower both minister bread for your food, and multiply your seed sown, and increase the fruits of your righteousness;)","11":"Being enriched in every thing to all bountifulness, which causeth through us thanksgiving to God.","12":"For the administration of this service not only supplieth the want of the saints, but is abundant also by many thanksgivings unto God;","13":"Whiles by the experiment of this ministration they glorify God for your professed subjection unto the gospel of Christ, and for your liberal distribution unto them, and unto all men;","14":"And by their prayer for you, which long after you for the exceeding grace of God in you.","15":"Thanks be unto God for his unspeakable gift."}},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: Paul continued his talk on the ministry of giving in the last chapter. He talked about what a giving minister was to look like, and how we could have a stronger faith by giving to others who need it. We are giving to increase faith, not giving to HAVE it or keep it."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul seems to switch it up kind of drastically. He is still talking about ministering, but he's talking to a small group in the Corinthian church that seems to be utterly against Paul and the last letter he wrote. He talks about what he meant by the last letter, and how much he wants to connect even to those people that hate him. He wants them to work in the ministry WITH Paul, not against Paul. He still shows his love for them even though they don't have a love for him. That is the minister's example he was talking about a few chapters back."}],"previously":["Paul continued his talk on the ministry of giving in the last chapter. He talked about what a giving minister was to look like, and how we could have a stronger faith by giving to others who need it. We are giving to increase faith, not giving to HAVE it or keep it. In this chapter, Paul seems to switch it up kind of drastically. He is still talking about ministering, but he's talking to a small group in the Corinthian church that seems to be utterly against Paul and the last letter he wrote. He talks about what he meant by the last letter, and how much he wants to connect even to those people that hate him. He wants them to work in the ministry WITH Paul, not against Paul. He still shows his love for them even though they don't have a love for him. That is the minister's example he was talking about a few chapters back."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":18,"title":"Boasting of God","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, Paul says (right off the start here) that he is going to say the rest of this letter in meekness and gentleness. So if people are offended by what he says, he is saying it in meekness and gentleness. He doesn't mean anything negative or hurtful by this. There were some (no doubt) that were offended by his first letter. He was talking to them in meekness and gentleness before as well. He said it in love. He said it in fervent desire that they would take the correction in faith and carefulness. Some likely did not. So he reminds them in the way he is addressing them. It's done in love and compassion."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He says, \"When I am with you, I am showing you love and an example. When I'm not physically with you, I am being bold towards you because you don't have the physical example.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"They probably saw him as a bit of a hypocrite. He wasn't. He just doesn't have a need to correct them, when he can just SHOW them in person. He can't show them through a letter. He can only tell them. That's why it seems so much bolder through word rather than deed."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He apparently did some work while he was there that made people question his genuineness. Perhaps it was a work that had nothing to do with the church. Maybe it was more like reaching people or taking care of a personal need. I think a lot of people believe that Christian leaders are not allowed to do anything else but serve their church. Most people think that pastors are paid by their church. Some churches do pay their pastors, but that is not always the case. Paul wasn't able to be completely dedicated 24/7 to church ministry. He had to take care of his other needs too."},{"kind":"para","text":"But perhaps he went out to reach other people and minister to them, and that was what people were complaining about. I'd bet any money they complained because he was going to start other churches and he wasn't dedicated to this specific church in Corinth. If he's so busy with other churches (where he can't even come to visit them), then he must not actually care for this church. That is not how he feels at all."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It would be a different story if he was their pastor, but he was just their missionary."},{"kind":"para","text":"He had a lot of churches to take care of and build up in the faith. He was their minister alone, and that was all they should have expected from him. It seems they expected more though."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the worst feeling as a minister. Some churches abuse this, by the way. A minister does the most they can and gives the most they can, and it's still not enough for the people. Instead of making a minister mad, it makes them feel guilty. But it's abuse from the congregants. Ultimately, that's all we can call it. But we must remember that ministers are not eternal beings. As much as we would love to be, we just are not that yet. We have personal things that need to be taken care of, and we are only ONE person."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers are rare. They are even more rare when people abuse them..."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"Again, ministers are not eternal beings. We are human beings. If you want an eternal being to minister to you, ask the angels. Don't even concern yourself with church ministers. Let me know how that works."},{"kind":"para","text":"As long as we are on Earth, we are going to be walking in the flesh. We have flesh and bone. We have a deteriorating body. That is just the truth of being alive. We are flesh, but we are warring against it."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's funny, because as Paul says, \"Our weapons are not carnal. They are not things we have here. They are eternal.\""},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are human beings, but our weapons are eternal."},{"kind":"para","text":"What weapons do we have? Well, Ephesians 6 gives us a good list, but to make it a little more simplified, we can give a pretty useful list here. This is not exclusive, but it will be helpful."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"An eternal weapon we can use (and this is very useful to us) is God's Word:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the word of God is quick, and powerful, and sharper than any twoedged sword, piercing even to the dividing asunder of soul and spirit, and of the joints and marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.","ref":"Hebrews 4:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just His Word alone stabs. It alone convicts. That's a great useful tool for the minister."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Another useful eternal weapon is prayer:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit, and watching thereunto with all perseverance and supplication for all saints;","ref":"Ephesians 6:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Prayer is a weapon that relies solemnly on God. He alone answers prayer. So when we pray to the Lord, the Lord uses what we pray for, and we respect the way in which He uses it. It might not be the way you expect, but it will be the way that He needs to do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another eternal weapon we have access to is wisdom."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wisdom is better than weapons of war: but one sinner destroyeth much good.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 9:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom helps us make the proper decisions without wavering. We might second-guess personal decisions, or decisions about things that are just daily thoughts, but when we make spiritual decisions, wisdom affirms us in the decision and we don't have to doubt what we KNOW is Godly."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We also have the eternal weapon of the Spirit’s fruit;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"All of these attributes can be used as a weapon against the flesh and the spiritual."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We also (as Paul has pointed out a lot through this letter) have the eternal weapon of ministry;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore if thine enemy hunger, feed him; if he thirst, give him drink: for in so doing thou shalt heap coals of fire on his head.","ref":"Romans 12:20"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"These are all weapons that are not used very much. We think we are doing quite well with these weapons, yet we never train in them. We never grow in them. We never utilize them like we should. How frustrated the Lord must be watching us struggle and neglect God's gift of these eternal weapons."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is just the surface. There is so much more we can uncover, but these are great and important weapons to utilize today and now. Do not think you are fully finished growing in these areas. The moment you do, you will fail whenever a battle comes because you have not tested them."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Another eternal weapon we have access to is wisdom:"}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is ultimately what the spiritual weapons are for. They are for \"casting down imaginations.\" What does that mean? \"Imaginations\" means \"a judgment.\" The weapons are for getting rid of judgement. Judgement from others, and false judgement from ourselves."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"They are for destroying pride, both from yourself, and from others who are too high-minded. They are for bringing your thoughts into the captivity and submission to the Lord. That's to say, we are constantly minded on the things of God rather than the things of self. They are for revenging those that are disobedient to the Lord, but only towards those that are part of the family of God."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We cannot use revenge on unsaved people (that is a very important theme to note)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Recompense to no man evil for evil. Provide things honest in the sight of all men. If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men. Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord. Therefore if thine enemy hunger, feed him; if he thirst, give him drink: for in so doing thou shalt heap coals of fire on his head. Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good.","ref":"Romans 12:17-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"This church at Corinth was learning that very slowly through Paul's letters. They were having a hard time in their judgement towards others. They were having an easy time judging, but not in Godly judgement. They were not forgiving towards those that they were judging, so they had to learn how to do this properly, and work together in that ministry. They had to be obedient to the Lord FIRST before making judgment towards others properly. Sadly, there are a lot of people who judge people, but they are not even living in obedience to the Lord themselves."},{"kind":"thought","text":"By the way, only Christians have the right to judge people. We have the standard of the Lord of judgement to judge by."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we are going to judge a man, we must judge him on his spiritual matter. We are so busy judging the outward appearance that we don't seem to care or worry about his spiritual health. This church was pretty good about judging Paul's appearance, but they did not consider his spiritual ministering."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason that Paul can say the things he is saying in his letters was for edifying the church. He was given authority by the Lord. The Lord personally gave Paul authority for it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Lord said unto him, Go thy way: for he is a chosen vessel unto me, to bear my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel: For I will shew him how great things he must suffer for my name's sake.","ref":"Acts 9:15-16"},{"kind":"thought","text":"He called Paul in His own voice to do these things. He did not call Paul to be a holy powerhouse. He called Paul to be an authoritative minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"Not everyone had this same privilege. The church in Corinth didn't have the privilege of that kind of authority. Titus didn't have that privilege."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul is not using the power given to him to try and destroy these people; he's using it as the Lord intended, and that was to encourage and build the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think he had heard that there were some people in the church who felt bad about the first letter that was sent to them. They thought Paul was abusing his authority. He wasn't. He wasn't \"boasting\" about his own apostleship. In fact, Paul made that known in his first letter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, and Sosthenes our brother, Unto the church of God which is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that in every place call upon the name of Jesus Christ our Lord, both theirs and ours:","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:1-2"},{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul was called to be an Apostle; the church was called to be saints."},{"kind":"para","text":"He was not expressing his authority as \"I am an Apostle because I said so.\" No, he said, \"I am an Apostle because the Lord wants me to be.\" I know for a fact that Paul did not want to be an Apostle. Just read the top of Acts 9:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Saul, yet breathing out threatenings and slaughter against the disciples of the Lord, went unto the high priest,","ref":"Acts 9:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"So he is the last person to abuse that position. He even said that he's not worthy to be CALLED an Apostle specifically to THIS CHURCH in his first letter:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I am the least of the apostles, that am not meet to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"So there was no reason for them to assume that he was playing some sort of power high with his apostleship. The Lord made sure to cover every base before the letter was sent. Yet, they still seemed to find reason to accuse Paul of some things that just simply weren't true. This letter would prove his love for this church, and there would still be people in the church that would accuse Paul of being too mean. People are strange creatures."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They saw Paul as weak, but his letters as bold. That's funny. You would probably think the same way about me if I were honest. I'm not much to look at. I wouldn't be able to take on Goliath, that's for sure. Yet I love to write. I don't do it to hurt people. I don't purposely try to put down other religions. I just use what they teach to prove how much it goes against scripture. It's not my fault that they have made it a part of their religion. I am going to point out the lies though, and show the reader (through God's Word) what the truth really is."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now in person, I might be a little different. I can collect my thoughts a little better when I'm writing rather than when I'm in a physical conversation with someone. But I have no problem standing up for the faith even in person. I have never backed down from conversing about scripture, whether in writing or face to face. It's just a little easier in writing. That is exactly how Paul was to this church."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church thought he was just writing a big game. He was all bark, no bite. But Paul could bite. He has done it before;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But when Peter was come to Antioch, I withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed. For before that certain came from James, he did eat with the Gentiles: but when they were come, he withdrew and separated himself, fearing them which were of the circumcision. And the other Jews dissembled likewise with him; insomuch that Barnabas also was carried away with their dissimulation. But when I saw that they walked not uprightly according to the truth of the gospel, I said unto Peter before them all, If thou, being a Jew, livest after the manner of Gentiles, and not as do the Jews, why compellest thou the Gentiles to live as do the Jews?","ref":"Galatians 2:11-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the Apostle PETER that he's talking about here. He wasn't afraid to call out an original Apostle on his faults when the need called for it."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"You would probably think the same way about me if I were honest. I'm not much to look at. I wouldn't be able to take on Goliath, that's for sure. Yet I love to write. I don't do it to hurt people. I don't purposely try to put down other religions. I just use what they teach to prove how much it goes against scripture. It's not my fault that they have made it a part of their religion. I am going to point out the lies though, and show the reader (through God's Word) what the truth really is. Now in person, I might be a little different. I can collect my thoughts a little better when I'm writing rather than when I'm in a physical conversation with someone. But I have no problem standing up for the faith even in person. I have never backed down from conversing about scripture, whether in writing or face to face. It's just a little easier in writing."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says, \"The person you read in the letters is the same person you are going to see when I come. Give me a reason to correct you, and I most certainly will.\" Paul doesn't want to come to them to correct them, though. He wants to come to them in love and edification. He wants to have joy in the ministry with their joy. This small group of believers who had a problem with Paul would have to get on board with the rest of the churches’ excitement for Paul. Paul will be an example of the proper minister when he comes to them. He will live how he is telling them to live. He's already living this way as a minister, so they cannot accuse him of being a hypocrite."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a hard-hitting verse."},{"kind":"thought","text":"I think a lot of ministers compare themselves today. There are pastors that try and say, \"You need to climb the ladder. You are here when you should be here. You need to start doing this and that and start making a better name for yourself.\" They might look at someone else in the church and say, “Well, look at him. He's doing very well, and he hasn't been here nearly as long.\" That is a shame. It's unwise (as Paul says). We will look at the people around us and think we are somehow falling behind in the ministry. If we aren't in that person’s position, then we must not be doing something right."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, the church culture makes you feel that way. All it does is bring stress on the ministry, and it makes it unbearable to work in."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We are no longer doing it out of a love for souls. We are not doing it so that we can lay our own lives down. We are doing it for reputation. We are doing it to build our own lives up. That is robbing the spirits of people."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"We are spirit robbers if we are looking to compete with the ministers."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only thing worse than that is unlearned and false preachers."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Paul would not have measured up in his apostleship. He would have fallen way short."},{"kind":"thought","text":"When we think of apostles, we think of Peter. We think of John, or James. We think of Matthew, or Thomas. We don't usually go straight to Paul (unless you are more familiar with the Bible). During that time, they probably saw Peter as a great apostle. Paul probably saw Peter as a great apostle. If Paul was going to measure himself up to any other apostle, it would probably be Peter or John. And you know what? He would fall way short. Yet, it seems like it's Paul that is used the most in ministry. The Lord blessed them all, but it seems that Paul got a double blessing in many ways. He was a greatly used apostle, but it was only because he was not trying to measure his ministry to anyone else's."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have a ministry today, it does not matter how many people are in it compared to someone else's. Be content with what the Lord has given. That is sufficient. It should amaze us that God has allowed us a ministry at all."}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"thought","text":"None of the Apostles made themselves unreachable. We have a lot of \"celebrity\" pastors today. I would have considered my pastor at Lancaster Baptist a \"celebrity\" pastor. They are unreachable. They have a security team so that simple people can't come up and speak to them. They have their own special room in restaurants so they aren't bothered by the normies. They have security cars following them when they travel anywhere. They are (in my opinion) far above and beyond the class of the Apostles. It's because they are so unreachable that most of the church’s congregants feel under-measured. They could never measure up to these celebrity pastors."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I wouldn't want to."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I lose respect for a pastor that makes himself so unreachable. It's a shame that they would even be a pastor. They need moments where they can come down from their pedestal and meet us lowly folk to talk shop about ministry highs and lows."},{"kind":"para","text":"They will never get that though, because the only people that they will have time for are those that they have put on staff at their church. So what's the next best thing for people to measure up to? The Sunday school teacher? The Christian friend? Perhaps the world?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Compared to the world, we are in pretty good standing. Our measurement is far above and beyond. Paul had lowered himself enough that this church could reach his measurement. He was not asking them to do much more than he knew they were capable of doing. It really isn't that hard to follow, honestly."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Deal with the sin in the church, fellowship with the believers, and help keep the divisions from growing, and then be consistent in preaching the Word of truth. We now add giving and ministering into the mix, and none of this is still really that hard."},{"kind":"para","text":"These are things that we should all already be desiring to do, seeing as that is the literal example and life of our Lord and Savior. If He lived that way to give us an example, why would we not want to follow that example?"}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a great privilege to us. We can preach the gospel. That is a gift as well. We can GIVE the gospel. It's an eternal weapon, but it's also a gift that is given. It's a FREE gift that anyone who knows it can give to others. There are a lot of boasters in the world."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I will be honest, I used to boast about my music all the time. My music didn't save people though. My dad would boast about what celebrity houses he would build cabinets for. His cabinets wouldn't save people though. Pastors boast all the time about how many people were sitting in the church on a given Sunday. Sitting in church won't save people though. We have nothing to boast about, except for the fact that God has allowed us to read His Word, and give His Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul clarifies more:"}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I can get all sorts of pats on the back from all sorts of people. I can shake hands with celebrities and earn lots of money. But nothing matters more to me (and nothing should matter more to you) than to be commended by the Lord. To have the Lord's approval, presence, leading, and power is the most astounding thing a human being can ever witness."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"There's nothing in this world more full of awe than to know God personally."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's nothing that should give the Christian more energy and hype than to spend time with the Lord and spend time with others who want to spend time with the Lord. That is the most glorious thing we can ever experience in this life."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18}],"verses":{"1":"Now I Paul myself beseech you by the meekness and gentleness of Christ, who in presence am base among you, but being absent am bold toward you:","2":"But I beseech you, that I may not be bold when I am present with that confidence, wherewith I think to be bold against some, which think of us as if we walked according to the flesh.","3":"For though we walk in the flesh, we do not war after the flesh:","4":"(For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds;)","5":"Casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every thought to the obedience of Christ;","6":"And having in a readiness to revenge all disobedience, when your obedience is fulfilled.","7":"Do ye look on things after the outward appearance? if any man trust to himself that he is Christ's, let him of himself think this again, that, as he is Christ's, even so are we Christ's.","8":"For though I should boast somewhat more of our authority, which the Lord hath given us for edification, and not for your destruction, I should not be ashamed:","9":"That I may not seem as if I would terrify you by letters.","10":"For his letters, say they, are weighty and powerful; but his bodily presence is weak, and his speech contemptible.","11":"Let such an one think this, that, such as we are in word by letters when we are absent, such will we be also in deed when we are present.","12":"For we dare not make ourselves of the number, or compare ourselves with some that commend themselves: but they measuring themselves by themselves, and comparing themselves among themselves, are not wise.","13":"But we will not boast of things without our measure, but according to the measure of the rule which God hath distributed to us, a measure to reach even unto you.","14":"For we stretch not ourselves beyond our measure, as though we reached not unto you: for we are come as far as to you also in preaching the gospel of Christ:","15":"Not boasting of things without our measure, that is, of other men's labours; but having hope, when your faith is increased, that we shall be enlarged by you according to our rule abundantly,","16":"To preach the gospel in the regions beyond you, and not to boast in another man's line of things made ready to our hand.","17":"But he that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord.","18":"For not he that commendeth himself is approved, but whom the Lord commendeth."}},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In the last chapter, Paul spoke about his boldness to write to them. He wanted to come to them in joy and resolution, instead of bitterness and correction. If Paul hadn’t written his first letter to them, things would not have gone so well in the ministry when he returned (if there was even a church to return to at that point). He was very pointed about there being a small group of believers in the Corinthian church trying to soil his name. That wasn't going to work on Paul, though. He talks about how little he is comparatively, and tries to prove his care and love for this church (whether they believed it or not)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul proves it even further. We get a very deep look at the mind of Paul here. We've been opening that up for a few chapters now, but this chapter really digs deep into what he has experienced and how he has felt about it."}],"previously":["In the last chapter, Paul spoke about his boldness to write to them. He wanted to come to them in joy and resolution, instead of bitterness and correction. If Paul hadn’t written his first letter to them, things would not have gone so well in the ministry when he returned (if there was even a church to return to at that point). He was very pointed about there being a small group of believers in the Corinthian church trying to soil his name. That wasn't going to work on Paul, though. He talks about how little he is comparatively, and tries to prove his care and love for this church (whether they believed it or not). In this chapter, Paul proves it even further. We get a very deep look at the mind of Paul here. We've been opening that up for a few chapters now, but this chapter really digs deep into what he has experienced and how he has felt about it. There is no other chapter where we get this specifically personal to Paul."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Bearing Burdens","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The vulnerableness in this verse is unmatched."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I think it takes a lot for a minister to admit that they are human. Some ministers are so prideful."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the APOSTLE Paul that is speaking, mind you. He is making it very clear that he is not a perfect person."},{"kind":"thought","text":"“Saint” does not mean perfect. If it DID mean perfect, he certainly wouldn't be calling the people at the church in Corinth \"saints.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"They were the furthest from perfect. But he says in our last chapter that he has not done anything to intentionally hurt people, or to ruin a testimony. He was not trying to measure himself up to any man, and he made sure that the measurements he was giving to others were reachable. He set the standard pretty low."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now he says, \"I will take responsibility for any harm that is done, just please bear with me. Be patient and Godly to me.\""},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A minister should own up to their mistakes."},{"kind":"para","text":"They should be willing to admit that they are going to make them. I've learned early on in Ministry that if there is some sort of grievance that a person has with me, to apologize as quickly as possible. Even if I am not guilty of what they are accusing me of, I should just apologize. Take the guilt. Take the blame."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"One particular time there was a lady who had asked me to move a couch from out of the foyer of the church entrance. She wanted it done for some sort of decorating that I was not aware of. She told me to do it, and I let her know that I would get to it as soon as possible. Well, that was not soon enough for her. She wanted me to do it right then and now with my Sunday suit on. She was so frustrated with me. I said, “ I will get it now.” I walked over to the next room and took my suit jacket off, untucked my shirt, and rolled up my sleeves (three hundred dollar suit jackets are not worth damaging for a couch that didn't even have to be moved for another two weeks). By the time I came back, she was trying to move the couch on her own in a fit of rage. I moved the couch the rest of the way, and she just started yelling at me and telling me how useless I was. I didn't know what to say. So I started crying. I don't cry much, but this was all so sudden and so random. It was over a couch being moved. She was extremely impatient with me, and I would have gladly gotten it over the week, but for some reason, she wanted it done right then and there before the evening service. The Pastor called me into his office and asked me what was wrong with me. I said, \"I didn't think anything until now. I suddenly realized that I am not suited for this position. I can't seem to make these people happy, and it's always over stupid small things.\" He told me not to worry about it, and it wasn't a big deal. It WAS a big deal to me though. I called that lady the next day and apologized profusely to her about not taking immediate action. I was serious about my apology too. Why? Well, I didn't want her to be hurt. Even if the situation was for a stupid reason, I wanted her to know that I cared. I was willing to take the full brunt of that drama so that she could feel the minister’s heart. That's all I wanted."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is giving them a minister’s heart here. He is saying that he wants them to have patience with him as he has had immense patience for them."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This has a lot of implications in this one verse. If you don't remember, this church had a hard time with prostitution. I think they may have had a hard time with polygamy too, but I know for sure adultery was rampant in this community. So Paul says, \"I have given you the occasion and reason to be with ONE spouse, rather than messing around in ungodly lust.\" But the greater implication is that they are married to the Lord specifically."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The church is Christ's bride. We are engaged to HIM alone. We do not belong to any other gods or idols. We don't belong to our own fleshly impulses or desires. We don't belong to ungodly women and unfaithful men. We belong to the Lord alone, and that is our holiest matrimony. The moment we accept Christ, we become His bride. WE (the people) are the church, not the building."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I've spoken about this quite a few times, but this is the way in which Satan always comes to people. He comes in “subtly.\" He slithers in like a snake would. A snake is a perfect representation of Satan in the garden. He couldn't have picked a better vessel to signify his own being. He comes in, and you know what he does? He confuses what should be simple..."},{"kind":"para","text":"See the gospel of Christ, and the life of Christ are quite simple to understand and follow. But Satan overcomplicates it, and it makes the believer feel like they have to do more than what the Lord has already bid us. That is why I have such a deep problem with religion."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Religion says, \"Here are the things you have to DO now that you are in the faith.\" But the gospel says, \"Here is what Christ already DID so you can BE in the faith.\""},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Our faith does not belong to the Lord by works. Our faith belongs to the work the Lord already did! We are trusting that He is telling the truth when He said \"It is finished.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"We are trusting the eyewitness accounts of the gospel message being physically shown. We are trusting that the Lord actually raised from the dead according to the scriptures. We are trusting as Paul put it in 1 Corinthians 15:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I delivered unto you first of all that which I also received, how that Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures; And that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day according to the scriptures:","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Look at how SIMPLE that is. Yet, religions complicate it. SATAN complicates it. And he does it in subtlety. He makes you feel like it's more complicated than it actually is. Satan is good at pretending. He's good at stretching the truth. This church would have been easily swayed back into the lies of Satan if Paul hadn’t sent them the first letter."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Paul is still a little worried about it, obviously. He doesn't want their hard work in the Lord to go to waste because of the few in the church who are still being rambunctious. It's easy for any of them to fall into the complex mind of Satan. It's easy for us too. It's easy for me. It's easy for you. It's easy for our pastors and preachers. It's easy for all ministers everywhere. It was even easy for the Apostle Paul."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Satan is always looking to get a foothold into a believer’s testimony in some shape or form."},{"kind":"para","text":"The funny thing is, though, that God can USE those sensitive moments to FURTHER our testimonies if we turn back to Him and follow Him. He can say, \"Look where you were, and now look where I have taken you.\" We would see it and say, \"Woah. I was far off the mark, and I'm so glad the Lord redirected me. Look at the proof of God's providence. Follow Him.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul illustrates Eve in this picture here. Eve was tempted by Satan to eat fruit that was forbidden to eat. Satan knew what he was doing when he tempted her. God knew what He was doing by allowing it. That's the testimony that this church could learn from."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The forbidden fruit for them was prostitution, but they have turned from that sin, and they can use that testimony to prove that it's possible to turn from adultery. For anyone that is accessing something forbidden by the Lord, take the testimony of this church upon your heart. Triumph IS possible."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the Jesus you believe is not the Jesus of the Holy Scriptures, you are being preached about ANOTHER Jesus. It is not the same Jesus a Christian worships."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are a lot of people who preach another Jesus today. They preach a Jesus that was not enough for you and me. A Jesus who does not care about you. A Jesus who loves you and loves your sins. A Jesus who accepts your unrepentant spirit. A Jesus with a different kind of gospel message. A Jesus who does not provide conviction through the Holy Ghost. That's the Jesus this world wants. A Jesus they can control and warp to their own pleasures and needs. But that is not the Jesus of scripture."},{"kind":"para","text":"What we get in scripture ALONE about Christ is who Jesus REALLY is. Nothing less, nothing more. Jesus is God in the flesh, and He is the Savior of the world, but He also preached repentance and Holy Living. Paul preached the message of Jesus. He was able to be trained by the Apostles of Jesus. He learned the way of Jesus through the following of Jesus, and only based on the WORD of Jesus. That was the Jesus Paul preached, and that is the Jesus we should want to follow."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason the other Apostles are titled \"Apostles\" was simply because of their presence with the Lord. They were physically commissioned by Christ to build His church."},{"kind":"thought","text":"You cannot have that today. Jesus will not physically tell you to build His church. He will tell you through your eyes, but not through your ears. Because of that, we must be content with being Saints, or disciples of the Lord and nothing more. We are simple learners. We should not want to be any more than that, honestly."},{"kind":"para","text":"I often picture what it was like to be an Apostle of Christ. I think, \"What if I was born during that time? Would I be an Apostle? What if I was Peter? or Paul? or John?\" Then I think, \"Boy, they all died painfully. They went through some serious trials and they never had time to rest from building the church. They hardly had time to supply for their own needs.\" I come to the conclusion that I am quite glad that the Lord did not make me an Apostle. What a heavy load of ministry one would have to carry. I'm glad He has not made me a Pastor either."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The Pastors I have learned under are all quite miserable. I am sufficient being a teacher. It's sufficient to just simply be called a saint, or disciple. I'd prefer that over the \"Apostle\" title any day."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is an Apostle just as much as any other Apostle. He was called in the same way as they were. He was used in the same ways as they were. He preached the same gospel as they did. He worked the same ministry as them. He had the same power that they had. Jesus spoke to Paul the same way He spoke to the twelve. Paul is an Apostle, whether this church (or any other for that matter) wanted to accept it or not."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul's harsh critiquing is not going to change the fact that he is still an Apostle. People might not like the way he says things, but he is simply speaking the truth to them. He is not doing it in \"rudeness”; he is doing it in \"knowledge.\" That is important to note."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There are plenty of ministers who give correction in rudeness. There are countless congregants who are downright rude about correcting the leadership of the church. They are not looking to give you knowledge. They are (honestly) looking to rob you of some knowledge. They are looking to steal hope and joy of ministry from you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether they mean to do that or not is up in the air, but I know it's a common problem in the church today. It seems that is what some at the Corinthian church were thinking to do to Paul. Why make the ministry harder? I suppose the church will never know... Either way, Paul wants to bear their burdens, and so he is just simply begging them to bear HIS burdens as well."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":11,"title":"Questioning Conduct","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Good question. It's a question that everyone should be asking. Why are people offended by a minister who is laying his own life, time, and health down to lift up other people's spirits to the Lord? He could have just avoided Corinth altogether, honestly. Why are they offended that he is preaching the gospel of God to them freely? Why are they offended that he would be giving them direction and correction? Why are they offended that Paul cares so much about their souls’ needs and is willing to go out of his way and take some of his time to write to them?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now the same question falls on you!"},{"kind":"conversation","text":"I had made an event for the church one particular time, and someone got mad at me. They said, \"What's the point of this? Why are we doing something like this when we can just have church on Sundays and Wednesdays? Why make an extra effort like this and waste everyone's time?\" Now, if you know me, you know that I will always give a Bible lesson of some sort no matter what the event might call for. So it's not like it was a waste of time by any means. The real question is, \"Why do you care so much about this event when it is reaching the lost? Why would you want to have LESS opportunities to minister to others instead of more? Why does that upset you?\""},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what Paul is getting at here."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There is no good reason why people should be upset about a minister teaching God's Word every chance he gets. Yet there are people who will undoubtedly get upset. There are even LEADERS IN THE CHURCH who will get upset about it. How little and pathetic their souls must be. How calloused and cold their hearts have grown."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Other churches supported the Apostle, but the Corinthian church was seemingly having a hard time giving support. He felt like the support he was getting from these other churches (just to be able to MINISTER to the Corinthian church) was robbery. These churches were very prominent about the gospel being spread in Corinth. That is how the Corinthian church started. Paul had the boldness to preach to them and minister to them, but the churches had the boldness to send finances and encouragers to support Paul."},{"kind":"para","text":"Corinth (once again) was a RICH church. It had the ability to reach far wider in the world, and yet they didn't feel like they should support Paul for one reason or another. Paul is questioning it here, and he illustrates that they would not be a church today if it wasn't for OTHER churches giving to THEIR needs. They were an \"all take, no give\" type of church. It could be understood if they were a poor church, but they were not. Even the POOR churches were giving to Paul's needs."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover, brethren, we do you to wit of the grace of God bestowed on the churches of Macedonia; How that in a great trial of affliction the abundance of their joy and their deep poverty abounded unto the riches of their liberality.","ref":"2 Corinthians 8:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Deep poverty\" means what you think it means. They had nothing to give, yet they still did. They did for the sake of churches like that in Corinth."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If a church truly believes in what the minister is doing, they will gladly give to his needs."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul couldn't ask for help from this church in Corinth. That is a very sad thought."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A church should be a place where you can easily and freely come ask for help."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are not many churches that are willing to do that though."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"Some churches don't even want to help their own congregants except in very limited resources that the church (itself) gets to pick. “Hey, can you guys help me with rent?\" Asks the five-year church veteran. \"No, we cannot do that. We can give you a $5 gift card to the grocery store though.\" Says the minister. A gift is a gift, but $5 is nothing compared to what the needs actually are (and the church is usually responsible to help with it and has the ability to do so). Better yet (and I have sadly seen this happen a couple of times in churches), someone comes in and asks for help with groceries. \"Sure thing!\" Says the Pastor, \"I will get it to you this weekend.\" Three weeks go by and there are still no groceries. There is something very wrong with that picture."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"A church should be a trusted place for Christians to go to get help in times of need."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christians should not abuse that help, but they should feel comfortable going to get that help. If a Christian doesn't feel comfortable with that, then it is likely because the church has made them feel uncomfortable with that decision."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul wasn't worried about asking for help from this church because he knew that God would stir the hearts of other believers to help supply for his needs. And that is what happened. That is something we should depend on too."},{"kind":"thought","text":"(This is sad for me to say, but it’s true nevertheless.) Don't depend on your church. Depend on the Lord. God will ALWAYS provide."},{"kind":"para","text":"It might be a little scary from time to time, and it might leave you late on a payment or two, but through those times of testing, God WILL provide if you lean and depend on Him. It's very strange how it always works out for the Christian in need."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had needs, but his dependency was on Christ, and that is what kept him going in a land that was largely rejecting his message. The Lord provided, and they cannot say, “Well, it's because we gave you this or that.\" No. Even if they desired to give something to Paul, Paul would have rejected it because they were not living correctly for the Lord. He did not want that money or help to be a cause for them to say, “Well, now you owe us, Paul. We did this for you, so now you have to be a slave to us. You can't say mean things to us now, Paul.\" Rather, Paul gave himself with nothing in return from this church, and the church benefited, but now is complaining about that benefit. How sad is that?"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's not because Paul doesn't LOVE this church that he didn't ask for help. Obviously, it's the opposite. It's BECAUSE he loves this church that he didn't request any help from them. He wanted to prove to this church that it is entirely possible to rely on the Lord rather than their own good works and finances. They could easily let it go because Paul demonstrated how that looks and works. They could not say, “Well, obviously the Lord won't take care of us because He didn't take care of Paul.\" They couldn't argue or deny the fact that God was taking care of His ministers who were truly His ministers, and that was without the support of the local church."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is something I need to realize as well. I often think that if my church does not get behind my ministry, then there is no ministry at all. When what I should be doing is just simply ministering and allowing the Lord to provide. I've had it happen, though, where I've started a ministry, and the church takes it away. Time will tell how that works out for them. The Lord will open up something new again, though. He always has, and He always will. I just don't know how He will do it. Whether this church in Corinth wanted to support Paul or not wouldn't ultimately matter in the end. God will provide for Paul, but it would certainly be a wise decision to support Paul so THEY can be a part of the Lord’s blessings on Paul and the churches abroad."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":15,"title":"The Unministry","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are no excuses for the wayward and calloused Christian. People have asked me why I don't want to be in paid ministry again. The truth is, I wouldn't mind it, but it has to be on the right terms. I want to prove to everyone that it's possible to minister without a paycheck. I don't want a paycheck to be an excuse for rejecting ministry. I've seen that happen in a church before, and it brings shivers down my spine. It literally makes me queasy."},{"kind":"thought","text":"How can a self-proclaimed \"Christian\" think that ministry is only a calling for them if they have a church paycheck?"},{"kind":"para","text":"That baffles me."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul gladly ministered for free. He just wanted them to prosper and grow so that they could turn around and start their own ministry. They would be a great resource as well, but that was secondary to actually being good, capable, and unified ministers to the needs of the people around them. And trust me, there were plenty of needs in Corinth. They might have been rich, but they also had plenty of poor and hurting people around them. They had abused and addicted souls that needed healing and direction. Providing for those people was something that Paul thought very strongly about, and that is ultimately why he wanted this church to grow. Supporting other churches would just be extra encouragement if they were willing to be a blessing beyond their little city. But he was not willing to do the work of ministry for a price. Why? Well, he tells us what kind of people they (paid required ministers) are in the next verse:"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are phony ministers. They are (as we've said before) \"unministers.\" They are taking away what a minister is supposed to add. They are linked to one more thing:"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are no better than Satan. He pretends like he's so innocent, but Satan is not. Everyone should know that Satan is the bad guy. He is looking to try and cause destruction and pain to the world. Not just the Christians, but to all creation."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"God has created all things \"good\" and that is what Satan wants to attack. He wants to attack God's goodness."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the one thing that Satan hates more than anything else. He HATES Gods goodness."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"An \"unminister\" hates the goodness of God too. They might not know they do, but when they abuse people's trust by ministering to them for something in return, they are openly saying they hate God's goodness. God did something good to someone, and unministers want to take that away. Unministers are the ministers of Satan. Ministers are the ministers of the Lord. It should be pretty simple for Christians to spot the difference."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You get what you ask for. If you have been ministering for Satan, you will get the fruit of that unministry. If you have been ministering for the Lord, you will get the fruit of that ministry. It's really not that difficult to understand. But again, Satan is the master at complicating the simplicity of God's goodness."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":33,"title":"Paul's Testimony","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is not trying to come off as foolish, but if people want to call him that, they can at least hear his foolishness out first. They need to just humor him for a moment. This is a humble way of saying, \"I'm trying to give you the truth here. Whether you want to believe it or not is completely up to you. But if you don't want to believe it, at least hear what I have to say instead of rejecting me before I say it.\" That's what they did to Jesus. They didn't want to give His Words the time of day. The religionists had already made up their minds about the Lord before the Lord said or did a single thing. They hated Him, and there was no amount of good that the Lord could do that would get them to think anything different."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. He says, “You already think I'm a fool, so I'm going to say something foolish for you to understand.” Paul is about to boast about something that he considers quite foolish, but they are boasting about it in themselves already. If they want to measure each other, here's how the measurement will look:"}]},{"start":18,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a bit of a jab, and it's a good one. \"I'm a fool, you know,\" Paul says, \"You guys are so very wise, and so I will address your wisdom with my foolishness. You guys can glory; I will take this moment to show you what I will glory in as well. I'll glory in my foolishness.\""}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"An immature Christian suffers from the attacks of man. A mature Christian will only care about what the Lord thinks of him."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have mentioned that before. But the immature (that is, the foolish, but Paul is calling them wise to stroke their egos a bit here because they don't see themselves as fools; he is playing on their intellect), they will take the account of man and suffer from it if a man attacks them or robs them, or prides themselves, or hits them."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They care so much about what MAN can do that they miss what the LORD is capable of."},{"kind":"para","text":"Because of this, a person will miss the power of God and subsequently miss the blessings from it as well."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is using their own terms against them. They think they are bold. They think they are real ministers of the faith. They think they have done more than anyone else for the work of God. I have no doubt they have done a lot in the land they were in. There is a lot of pressure from the world around this church. But Paul is using their own boldness as a measuring stick."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He says, \"Foolish people think they are more bold than others. Foolish people think they are more godly and more ministry-oriented than others.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Now he will talk about his own ministry, and this is good:"}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul can boast just as much as anyone else. We can look through all the letters of Paul, and we never see him boast about his ministry. We see him as a very humble Christian calling himself \"the chief of sinners.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","ref":"1 Timothy 1:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet he is using his ministry as an illustration of how small-minded this church really is. This small group of people against Paul, accusing Paul of being less than he is, are going to see that Paul has had some scares from much scarier propositions than they. They were child's play compared to these other things he has faced. It's a foolish thing to compare ourselves to others. I have been in ministry for almost seven years now, and this certainly puts me to shame in every way possible... This church has only existed (since Paul first visited) for about three to five years. They probably had more trials than me even STILL, and I've been in ministry longer. But nothing compares to Paul's hurt and journey. It is quite incredible (and it's very pointedly accurate according to Acts, and not even that book illustrates all of it):"}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Can I just pause here for a moment and address one spot really quick (there is much more that I want to lump here, but I don't want us to miss this one important note): Paul was \"in perils by\" his \"own countrymen...\" These are people that Paul respected and knew very well. They were his own people. His own family. They did not like his life being sold out for the Lord. So they threatened and attacked him. They hurt him and rejected him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Jews (today) have a funeral for a family member that follows the Christian faith. To them, that person is dead. They are no longer part of the family. What a sad and pathetic bout of cultism. The Jewish faith today is just a cult. It is not what it once was in the Old Testament. They are the New Testament cult that Paul is addressing here, and they were attacking Paul for his faith in Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great reminder to us that have family members who are not saved. You will be rejected too for your faith. You do not stand alone. It hurts, and obviously it hurt Paul because he added it to this very lengthy and difficult list of hurts that Paul faced for the ministry of Christ."},{"kind":"thought","text":"We will find that this list is not here to prove the misery of the ministry. Rather, it's here to show the endurance of a minister who truly believes in what Christ said."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul continues:"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are things that didn't happen often, but they all did take place in his ministering. They happened because of his ministering. Now Paul will address the daily troubles of a minister:"}]},{"start":28,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's what \"foolish\" Paul will glory in... He will glory in the fact that he took the hit for these churches to thrive. He glories in the fact that these churches could complain about anything at all because Paul fought for them. He glories in the fact that he gave himself over to ministry so that they can learn how to minister. He glories in the abuse that the churches give to him so they can grow in the Lord. He's a human being. He's nothing special. But he has done everything he can to give this church a fighting chance, and if there are people in this church who have a problem with that, then Paul says he will gladly take the hit for it by them so that they can continue to grow."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Paul's a minister, remember? He is going to give himself to ministry, and that includes being abused by confused, calloused, and immature Christians who don't know any better."},{"kind":"para","text":"If that is what it takes for these Christians to grow up, so be it."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"If it takes that same effort and pain for YOUR church to grow, and they must abuse the ministers to get there, then I guess we should be comfortable with the abuse."},{"kind":"para","text":"But they can never say, \"Oh, Paul doesn't care. He isn't here. He is just talking a big game but he isn't actually ministering to us.\" He has done everything in his power to minister to them. They are unministering, though, and that is where the tension and problems come in."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If they don't believe him, it doesn't matter because the Lord knows. The Lord has rewarded Paul for his ministry. It was obvious and evident to the other churches; the only people that didn't believe it were the unministers. Paul always had a group of unministers wherever he went. They were the religionists. This small group of people in Corinth were no different, and they should take that up with the Lord if they have a problem."}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says, \"Oh yeah! One more thing! My life was in so much turmoil that the religionists ( political leaders) in Damascus wanted to kill me, so the disciples had to sneak me out of the city in a basket so I don't die.\" There was a pretty big proof of that. The Damascus governor was an eyewitness to this account. So if they didn't want to believe the rest of Paul's testimony, they could get at least this bit straight from a prominent leader of a city that Paul preached to. It was at this city, in fact, that Paul was to come and destroy the Christians living there. This is where his life changed. It was severely evident to ALL the Christians there because they knew that Paul was a man of immense hate towards their faith. Yet, he has joined himself to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of eyewitnesses to the great turmoil that Paul experienced in ministry. And you know what? Paul wouldn't trade ANY of it for the world. He would do it again in a heartbeat (and then some) if it meant that churches would be planted, and people would be saved. That was all he was concerned about."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He was not concerned about his own life; he was concerned about the lives of others."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's foolish to worry about anything other than that…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33}],"verses":{"1":"Would to God ye could bear with me a little in my folly: and indeed bear with me.","2":"For I am jealous over you with godly jealousy: for I have espoused you to one husband, that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ.","3":"But I fear, lest by any means, as the serpent beguiled Eve through his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.","4":"For if he that cometh preacheth another Jesus, whom we have not preached, or if ye receive another spirit, which ye have not received, or another gospel, which ye have not accepted, ye might well bear with him.","5":"For I suppose I was not a whit behind the very chiefest apostles.","6":"But though I be rude in speech, yet not in knowledge; but we have been throughly made manifest among you in all things.","7":"Have I committed an offence in abasing myself that ye might be exalted, because I have preached to you the gospel of God freely?","8":"I robbed other churches, taking wages of them, to do you service.","9":"And when I was present with you, and wanted, I was chargeable to no man: for that which was lacking to me the brethren which came from Macedonia supplied: and in all things I have kept myself from being burdensome unto you, and so will I keep myself.","10":"As the truth of Christ is in me, no man shall stop me of this boasting in the regions of Achaia.","11":"Wherefore? because I love you not? God knoweth.","12":"But what I do, that I will do, that I may cut off occasion from them which desire occasion; that wherein they glory, they may be found even as we.","13":"For such are false apostles, deceitful workers, transforming themselves into the apostles of Christ.","14":"And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.","15":"Therefore it is no great thing if his ministers also be transformed as the ministers of righteousness; whose end shall be according to their works.","16":"I say again, let no man think me a fool; if otherwise, yet as a fool receive me, that I may boast myself a little.","17":"That which I speak, I speak it not after the Lord, but as it were foolishly, in this confidence of boasting.","18":"Seeing that many glory after the flesh, I will glory also.","19":"For ye suffer fools gladly, seeing ye yourselves are wise.","20":"For ye suffer, if a man bring you into bondage, if a man devour you, if a man take of you, if a man exalt himself, if a man smite you on the face.","21":"I speak as concerning reproach, as though we had been weak. Howbeit whereinsoever any is bold, (I speak foolishly,) I am bold also.","22":"Are they Hebrews? so am I. Are they Israelites? so am I. Are they the seed of Abraham? so am I.","23":"Are they ministers of Christ? (I speak as a fool) I am more; in labours more abundant, in stripes above measure, in prisons more frequent, in deaths oft.","24":"Of the Jews five times received I forty stripes save one.","25":"Thrice was I beaten with rods, once was I stoned, thrice I suffered shipwreck, a night and a day I have been in the deep;","26":"In journeyings often, in perils of waters, in perils of robbers, in perils by mine own countrymen, in perils by the heathen, in perils in the city, in perils in the wilderness, in perils in the sea, in perils among false brethren;","27":"In weariness and painfulness, in watchings often, in hunger and thirst, in fastings often, in cold and nakedness.","28":"Beside those things that are without, that which cometh upon me daily, the care of all the churches.","29":"Who is weak, and I am not weak? who is offended, and I burn not?","30":"If I must needs glory, I will glory of the things which concern mine infirmities.","31":"The God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, which is blessed for evermore, knoweth that I lie not.","32":"In Damascus the governor under Aretas the king kept the city of the damascenes with a garrison, desirous to apprehend me:","33":"And through a window in a basket was I let down by the wall, and escaped his hands."}},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul made sure to speak to the small church group that was looking to discredit him. Instead of Paul using astounding things to prove his ministry, he uses his pains and trials in ministry. It's really sad and frustrating that a minister would have to prove himself like this to a church just so they can respect him or connect with him... Sometimes that is what church leaders are expecting. They want proven people to prove themselves further. They can’t accept the simple truth and proof that has already been permitted."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he proves it further. He uses a great illustration to make a very good point that I hope we will see and use in our own conduct in ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter will hone in on that and (hopefully) help us see how valuable a trial can be."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul made sure to speak to the small church group that was looking to discredit him. Instead of Paul using astounding things to prove his ministry, he uses his pains and trials in ministry. It's really sad and frustrating that a minister would have to prove himself like this to a church just so they can respect him or connect with him... Sometimes that is what church leaders are expecting. They want proven people to prove themselves further. They can’t accept the simple truth and proof that has already been permitted. It’s strange that a leader would require proof from someone that has ministered for the church for so many years, and yet it’s still not enough to connect with him… In this chapter, he proves it further. He uses a great illustration to make a very good point that I hope we will see and use in our own conduct in ministry. There is a purpose for pain. There's a purpose for trials and hurts. There's a purpose for the pushback you receive for doing what is good and Godly. This chapter will hone in on that and (hopefully) help us see how valuable a trial can be."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"The Proof of Weakness","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Did you read the list from the last chapter? Paul says that none of these things bring him any glory. He has no reason to find glory in these difficulties and stresses. There really is no glory in our own doings. Actually, let's take a look at who glory SHOULD be ascribed to according to Scripture:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whether therefore ye eat, or drink, or whatsoever ye do, do all to the glory of God.","ref":"1 Corinthians 10:31"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Who being the brightness of his glory, and the express image of his person, and upholding all things by the word of his power, when he had by himself purged our sins, sat down on the right hand of the Majesty on high;","ref":"Hebrews 1:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto her, Said I not unto thee, that, if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God?","ref":"John 11:40"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon thee.","ref":"Isaiah 60:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For of him, and through him, and to him, are all things: to whom be glory for ever. Amen.","ref":"Romans 11:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the glory that is represented through scripture (and this is only a few) is to the Lord."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The Lord deserves glory. We cannot glory in anything except that we have Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But let him that glorieth glory in this, that he understandeth and knoweth me, that I am the LORD which exercise lovingkindness, judgment, and righteousness, in the earth: for in these things I delight, saith the LORD.","ref":"Jeremiah 9:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Those things listed in our last chapter were listed for the sake of testimony. These people think that Paul is not worth their time, yet Paul has done nothing but give his time for people and the ministry. He's exhausted all of his resources for it, and that is not to his own glory, that is to the LORD'S glory. The Lord even grants him with \"visions\" and \"revelations\" and that is not by his own power, it's through the gift and providence of God alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"We hold the Apostles with such high respect today. There are some churches that even worship them (falsely, might I add). Yet, this church in Corinth has seen Paul, and they have no reason to pay any respect to him. How could they not, though, when he is the one that set them up for their work?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But continue thou in the things which thou hast learned and hast been assured of, knowing of whom thou hast learned them;","ref":"2 Timothy 3:14"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"I think we all owe some respect to the people who have taught us in our faith."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting turn; Paul will use this as an example. How do we know that this man was telling the truth? Well, Paul is giving the account, so I trust that Paul is telling the truth. Some scholars are under the impression that Paul is speaking about himself here. I don't think I agree with that notion. I think this is an entirely separate person that is giving the account. It does sound unbelievable though, and so if I were Paul, I probably wouldn't take anyone else's word on it unless I experienced it myself. But God may have revealed something to him. That is why he will keep saying \"God knoweth.\" Let God be the judge of it."}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If a man came to you and said, \"I went to Heaven, and I heard unspeakable words (words that were SO astounding) that I can't even speak about it to you; and then I came back to life to tell you this!\" Would you believe him? I probably wouldn't. That might be why scholars believe that Paul is speaking about himself here. He's not giving himself credit for it though, because people already didn't believe in Paul as it was."},{"kind":"para","text":"I like to think that Paul is certainly speaking about a different person who was proven to him. Whether or not it was true is not up for Paul to judge. If it is true, that is certainly a reason for Paul to glory in. He can glory in that (even though it's about another man's Heavenly vision). But Paul does not have such testimony. So the only glory that he will have is in his infirmities."},{"kind":"thought","text":"That list of trials we read in the last chapter, he glories in that because it was given to him by the Lord. No doubt he would glory if he had the privilege to go to Heaven and see the Lord (that is what the third Heaven is by the way. The sky is our first Heaven, outer-space is our second, and eternity is the third), but the only thing the Lord provided for Paul was ministry trials."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a great reason to glory."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"That the Lord would use any of us at all, in any way is a glory in itself."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a mystery really."},{"kind":"para","text":"He could use a testimony like visiting Heaven to a pretty serious advantage honestly. But that is not the testimony the Lord gave him. The stronger testimony (really) was his suffering for the Lord. That is a VERY strong testimony. If you go through a trial, remember that it was a testimony for the Lord. It will be used to prove God's glory."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Try and prove God's glory through a story of visiting Heaven, and I will prove God's glory through a story of great hardship and victory."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Paul could speak about far bigger things than his trials. He has experienced healings, speaking in tongues, exorcisms, and all sorts of miracles that would blow this church’s minds if they believed him. But it's the trials that prove his testimony. Take it upon yourself; It's your trials and heartbreaks that prove God's working. It's not your miracles and personal experiences that can prove anything. Miracles are great, but they can't be proven."},{"kind":"para","text":"For instance, YOU might have experienced the miracle of money falling from the sky, but you cannot prove that anyone else will experience that miracle. Or perhaps you experienced the miracle of being healed from a deadly disease, but you cannot prove that anyone else will experience that miracle. Let's go even more astounding; If I told you that I experienced a man being shot in the chest point-blank from a shotgun blast and didn't have a scratch on him, I have no doubt there are people that would believe me, and people that wouldn't. That is certainly a miracle, and yet I cannot prove God's glory in that. But a trial... you can bet any money that others will experience the same trials you have, and you can ALWAYS prove that God was present through a season of pain."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I've heard a lot about this proverbial \"thorn in the flesh.\" Pastors have used it for personal testimony, and I've heard preachers preach on this specific pain several times over, and everyone has a different look at it. Most of the ministers that I have spoken to about this have claimed that it is a spiritual pain, rather than a physical one. There are few that believe it's physical. I think it's physical. It could be a LITERAL thorn, but at the very least it's a LITERAL pain of some sort. \"Thorn\" means \"a pointed piece of wood.\" It seems strange that Paul would use the term \"thorn\" and it not be literal."},{"kind":"para","text":"But I can go with the \"spiritual\" application as well (mostly because of the \"messenger of Satan\" remark). Satan can give you both physical and spiritual pains. But not without the Lord's approval. This physical (or spiritual) pain is ANOTHER example of God's glory though. Paul is not going to just say, \"I went to Heaven but I can't tell you anything about it.\" He's going to say, \"I had a pain, and I will tell you how the Lord saw me through it.\""}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"It\" is another keyword that denotes a physical pain. But I won't hang on to that for the sake of the message that Paul is getting at here. Three's a charm! Except for when it comes to Paul's thorn. He must have been in absolute pain, whether physical or spiritual doesn't matter. It was enough for him to PLEAD with the Lord about it three times before the Lord finally answers:"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"Both physical and spiritual weakness are what the Lord will utilize. The Lord can PERFECT His strength through a weak vessel."},{"kind":"para","text":"He can prove His power through someone that doesn't seem like much. Paul would rather look weak to have the Lord than to be strong and not have Him. All of us should desire this. Paul drives it home further:"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is only in your most NEEDFUL times that the Lord will come to you. If you are doing well, you have no need for the Lord's power and presence. If you are falling apart, God will absolutely use that. He WANTS to use your weakness. That's how we can see impossible circumstances become possible. That's how we can see unbelievable testimonies come to life right before us. It's the stories of victory that give us the most hope. The only way to have victory is if it looks like all hope is lost, and suddenly there's a change in the tide..."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":18,"title":"We Are Nothing","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These people had no problem glorying in their own strengths. Paul says it's foolish for him to even glory in his own weaknesses. But in order to prove to them that the LORD deserves the glory, he had to list off his achievements for Christ. They could have just helped him in the ministry, but they would not put themselves out there for this Apostle."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Yet, even through all of this, he still thinks of himself as nothing. He is able to stand toe-to-toe with the most tried and true of the Apostles (whichever one they deem worthy enough to be the best Apostle) and yet he still feels like the weakest one."},{"kind":"para","text":"Testimony would say otherwise."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, Paul could have used any of these means to prove himself, but that was not what he was at this church for. That was not how he wanted this church to conduct itself. He wanted this church to thrive on the necessity of God's Word and compassion for lost souls. That is what all good churches thrive on. That is what all good churches need to worry about. A church that is concerned about anything other than those two things (rather, anything MORE than those two things) is not a \"good\" church. I don't even know if I'd consider it a \"godly\" church."},{"kind":"para","text":"Churches would do well to accept the painful testimonies of its congregants as a true example of God's glorious providence. Honestly, it doesn't even have to be something huge that the Lord did. It can be small things, and we can make a big deal about that. Again, the fact that the Lord would even use us at all is something to glory over."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He wanted this church to thrive on the necessity of God's Word and compassion for souls. That is what all good churches thrive on. That is what all good churches need to worry about. A church that is concerned about anything other than those two things (rather, anything MORE than those two things) is not a \"good\" church. I don't even know if I'd consider it a \"godly\" church."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church had everything. They had no reason to complain about what the Apostles did for them in helping them start the church. The only thing they had right to complain about was the hurtful words that Paul said in his last letter, and even then it was a stretch to complain about. They needed to grow up, truth be told. But obviously, Paul is not going to say that, because they are already sensitive from much more serious topics in his first letter. So instead, he just asks for their forgiveness. He takes the fall for it (we spoke about that before)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is looking at this church as his children. All the Apostles seem to do this with the churches they have helped build. John writes three letters to the churches and only refers to them as his children. In this verse, he is saying he doesn't expect them to prepare things for him because they are simply children. Rather, as their spiritual father, he will prepare things for them because they need the help. That is what a father does, mind you. He prepares for their children’s future (at least a good father does). He is still going to put in the ministry work even though they are making things difficult for Paul."},{"kind":"thought","text":"A real father will do whatever it takes to prepare his children for their future, even if his children are unruly and disrespectful towards him."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ouch. What a remark. This seems to be evident through this letter, though. This small group was very resentful of Paul's love. They didn't want it, and the more he tried to love them, the more they tried to reject him."},{"kind":"thought","text":"He was willing to spend himself for them, though they would never fathom doing something like that for him. What a terrible thing for a church to do to such a sacrificial minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the case, honestly, with churches still today. It's the sad theme of MOST (if not ALL) the churches in our day and age. It's about taking as much as possible from the ministers and never giving anything in return."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Churches should NOT abuse their ministers. I repeat, churches should NOT abuse their ministers. This will still be lost on the readers, though... May this be the minister's creed."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul didn't care about the richness of the church. He cared about the PEOPLE of the church. That was what he was after. \"Guile\" means \"deceit.\" They were deceitful, not the Apostle Paul. They were trying to put Paul in this line of thievery from their church and congregants. They wanted to pin Paul to nothing more than a leech. That is not why Paul was there, though. Again, he had mentioned that he did not ask for anything from them because he didn't want this accusation to come up. Yet, it seems, they were still bringing it up anyway."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't even send anyone specifically to ask for something from them. This letter is the only time that he is making mention of their promise to give. Even when he sends this letter, the person that delivers it will not have instructions to speak about the support they promised to give before. They are not part of the Mafia. This letter will do all the talking, and I assume the church leader is going to read it out to the entire congregation. Either that or they will pass it around the church? Whatever the case, everyone is going to hear about it."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul knows that Titus was not going there to try and take anything from this church. It is a strange thing that this church would accuse Paul and his companions of being anything more than servants to them. That is all they wanted to be, and that is all they will be. It shouldn't have to be done towards rejectful spirits, but that is sometimes the only way ministry can be done."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Sadly, (and I say this from a loving heart) you will only be ministering to people who are calloused in heart and are looking to just abuse the minister. Even leadership will abuse the ministers..."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":21,"title":"Church Bully's","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is not going to shy away from them either. Just because they are trying to bully him, does not scare Paul. There are people who need his ministering, and who will gladly take it when Paul is going to come. It would be really nice if this small group would take part in that too and help Paul serve. But if they are not going to help serve, and if they are going to try and push Paul away, it will not keep Paul from doing what he has been called to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"Bullys should not keep you away from serving the Lord. Bullys will try, but you should simply sidestep them, and serve the Lord without them if they are going to try and make it difficult. Even if it's the Pastor or Ministry LEADERS who are trying to keep you from it. There are still people who NEED your ministering."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Bully's should not keep you away from serving the Lord. Bully's will try, but you should simply sidestep them, and serve the Lord without them if they are going to try and make it difficult. Even if it's the Pastor or Ministry LEADERS who are trying to keep you from it. There are still people who NEED your ministering."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this church has some very serious pressure all around them. If this small group has any sort of influence on the church, they will let the outside pressures win, and the innocent ministry-oppressed members will suffer the consequences of it. It is not a fun thing to have divisions in the church. The reason that Paul wrote to them to begin with was to help them stay unified. He wanted them to be unified under a specific authority, though."},{"kind":"thought","text":"You can unify in the world and in sin, but it won't be anything solid to unify under. But if you unified in righteousness and in the service of the Lord (like good ministers of the faith), then you would have a lasting unity that outmatches even the strongest forces of the world and culture around you."},{"kind":"para","text":"The same goes for this church in Corinth."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"It is not expedient for me doubtless to glory. I will come to visions and revelations of the Lord.","2":"I knew a man in Christ above fourteen years ago, (whether in the body, I cannot tell; or whether out of the body, I cannot tell: God knoweth;) such an one caught up to the third heaven.","3":"And I knew such a man, (whether in the body, or out of the body, I cannot tell: God knoweth;)","4":"How that he was caught up into paradise, and heard unspeakable words, which it is not lawful for a man to utter.","5":"Of such an one will I glory: yet of myself I will not glory, but in mine infirmities.","6":"For though I would desire to glory, I shall not be a fool; for I will say the truth: but now I forbear, lest any man should think of me above that which he seeth me to be, or that he heareth of me.","7":"And lest I should be exalted above measure through the abundance of the revelations, there was given to me a thorn in the flesh, the messenger of Satan to buffet me, lest I should be exalted above measure.","8":"For this thing I besought the Lord thrice, that it might depart from me.","9":"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me.","10":"Therefore I take pleasure in infirmities, in reproaches, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses for Christ's sake: for when I am weak, then am I strong.","11":"I am become a fool in glorying; ye have compelled me: for I ought to have been commended of you: for in nothing am I behind the very chiefest apostles, though I be nothing.","12":"Truly the signs of an apostle were wrought among you in all patience, in signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds.","13":"For what is it wherein ye were inferior to other churches, except it be that I myself was not burdensome to you? forgive me this wrong.","14":"Behold, the third time I am ready to come to you; and I will not be burdensome to you: for I seek not yours but you: for the children ought not to lay up for the parents, but the parents for the children.","15":"And I will very gladly spend and be spent for you; though the more abundantly I love you, the less I be loved.","16":"But be it so, I did not burden you: nevertheless, being crafty, I caught you with guile.","17":"Did I make a gain of you by any of them whom I sent unto you?","18":"I desired Titus, and with him I sent a brother. Did Titus make a gain of you? walked we not in the same spirit? walked we not in the same steps?","19":"Again, think ye that we excuse ourselves unto you? we speak before God in Christ: but we do all things, dearly beloved, for your edifying.","20":"For I fear, lest, when I come, I shall not find you such as I would, and that I shall be found unto you such as ye would not: lest there be debates, envyings, wraths, strifes, backbitings, whisperings, swellings, tumults:","21":"And lest, when I come again, my God will humble me among you, and that I shall bewail many which have sinned already, and have not repented of the uncleanness and fornication and lasciviousness which they have committed."}},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the importance of weakness. He talked about how the Lord can use our weakness better than He can use our strengths."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are humbled before the Lord, we can be used of the Lord immensely and we can have a heart for others (even in their abuse towards us) if we are focused on the work being done for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we finally come to the end of the letter. I have really enjoyed going through this letter and studying it out deeply. It has felt personal to me, and I know that Paul didn't even have me in mind when he wrote it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I wish it weren't so, but we NEED this letter today. It's an inspiring one for sure. In this last chapter, Paul will talk about what a real reprobate is, and gives a final send-off before coming to visit them again."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the importance of weakness. He talked about how the Lord can use our weakness better than He can use our strengths. Our strengths simply put eyes on us, but our weaknesses force eyes to focus on the Lord. When we are humbled before the Lord, we can be used of the Lord immensely and we can have a heart for others (even in their abuse towards us) if we are focused on the work being done for the Lord. In this chapter, we finally come to the end of the letter. I have really enjoyed going through this letter and studying it out deeply. It has felt personal to me, and I know that Paul didn't even have me in mind when he wrote it. I think Paul wanted the church to be a great enough example of how to fix these issues that there would be no churches ever again that would ever face these same issues. Little does he know that these issues are still very much a problem in our modern church. I wish it weren't so, but we NEED this letter today. It's an inspiring one for sure. In this last chapter, Paul will talk about what a real reprobate is, and gives a final send-off before coming to visit them again."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"The Reprobates","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, there is plenty of proof of God's ministry, but if they weren't willing to take the physical proof (which is a very strange proof to ignore), then maybe they will take the eyewitness proof. The proof of Paul’s burden isn't enough. The proof of the other people sent by Paul isn't enough. So maybe as he's working among them, they will accept the proof in the labor of love he is going to provide as an example. He will out-minister even the best of them because of their callousness to his care for them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This will be the third time he has made it to them, and three's a charm, right? It doesn't seem like three's a charm in the Apostles’ case, but hopefully this one will be. Three witnesses is a charm, and three visits is a charm, so there should be no more quarrels or accusations or pushback from anybody after this. I wish ministry were that easy. Ministry is never that easy."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There will ALWAYS be people who have something to complain about in your ministry. You will always have people who are looking to tear you down for your work in the Lord. They are okay with other people doing it. They are okay with the prideful and selfish Christians ministering. They are okay with that because THEY get something out of it. But when a minister comes without any sort of expectancy, and they can't seem to get their hands in the minister’s pockets, it's suddenly not okay."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Some people just want to control the ministers, and if they can't pay the ministers, they can't control them."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's literally politics."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's funny that they are expecting Paul to prove himself. He has so much more authority than they do. They might have a bold and solid gospel presentation, but they will be put to shame by Paul's approach quickly. He truly meant it when he said that he was bold also:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I speak as concerning reproach, as though we had been weak. Howbeit whereinsoever any is bold, (I speak foolishly,) I am bold also.","ref":"2 Corinthians 11:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"But this measuring of boldness is foolish, let's not forget. He will gladly become a fool for them though if it means that they will connect and work ministry together. Paul could just be wasting his time. So if it's boldness they want, it will be boldness they get. He is not going to hold anything back when he is in front of these men. He is ready to equip them and correct them without remorse. Then they will know the seriousness of Paul's ministry, and that seriousness they should be putting into their own ministry for the Lord."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Honestly though, boldness in others is contagious. It produces boldness in us."},{"kind":"thought","text":"There is a lot of “fake” boldness in Christianity today. I know some “unministers” who have no problem showing their face on stage. But when it comes to the lesser ministry (making visits, making calls, meeting needs, counseling, soulwinning, etc.) they are suddenly unequipped. They are only “equipped” if it’s to make a face. They LIKE the spotlight on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s a serious problem. I have no respect for a pastor or preacher who can just get on stage and preach the Word without a worry beforehand. Boldness should only come as soon as you get behind the pulpit. If it comes any sooner, you are a fake and untrustworthy in my opinion. Paul has even spoken about how scared he was to speak to certain churches. Even THIS church."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everything in this mortal body is weak compared to the Heavenly. God's power is best revealed through a weak, trusting person. He can use the people who are dependent upon Him. Even the crucifixion is considered \"weakness.\" It was weak because it killed this mortal body."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The resurrection is where the power of God truly is. It's the LIFE that displays the power of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Praise the Lord! He gives us life when we have Him. We are given NEW life, remember?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"I love it when Paul brings it full circle."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Reprobates\" means \"unfit\" or \"not standing the test.\" Honestly, a reprobate CAN'T be tested. They are usually too far gone to be someone that is able to take a test."},{"kind":"conversation","text":"I knew a boy in high school who smoked a lot of weed. He showed up every single day to school high as a kite. I don't think he knew where he was half the time. If you had a conversation with him, you would have to talk slowly and methodically for him to keep up in the conversation. He would fail his class work, show up late to class, or (sometimes) not show up to class at all. The only thing he was ever on time for was lunch."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are some “reprobates” that look and act normal too. They may even preach a message in your church. They will CRUMBLE under the weight of ministry though."},{"kind":"para","text":"This boy from high school could be thought of as a reprobate. You couldn't test him because he just simply wouldn't care. It's like giving a test to a sloth. It might seem as if the sloth is smart enough to take the test, but they won't know how to answer the questions. Solomon has a lot to say about slothful men."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The hand of the diligent shall bear rule: but the slothful shall be under tribute.","ref":"Proverbs 12:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The way of the slothful man is as an hedge of thorns: but the way of the righteous is made plain.","ref":"Proverbs 15:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The desire of the slothful killeth him; for his hands refuse to labour.","ref":"Proverbs 21:25"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I went by the field of the slothful, and by the vineyard of the man void of understanding;","ref":"Proverbs 24:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By much slothfulness the building decayeth; and through idleness of the hands the house droppeth through.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 10:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even still, the slothful man can get more done than the reprobate. The reprobate would be too far gone. Paul asks them to examine themselves. A reprobate can't even do that."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Sadly, I think a lot of Christians have a hard time examining themselves, and they are perfectly normal. They don't want to examine themselves because they know they have a lot to work on. So they try to ignore it, and they think it will just go away if they ignore their problems long enough."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's not how it works. To some, Paul was a problem, and he is still going to go visit them even with their reprobate thoughts."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the Apostles were reprobates, then we are all doomed. We should be much smarter if that were the case, though. We should be able to think far higher than what God's Word says. It's impossible to do, though."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"God's Word gives life. When we speak, it's usually to TAKE life."},{"kind":"para","text":"The people that were complaining about Paul's first letter felt as if they had their life being robbed. They misinterpreted his letter. It's God's Word that Paul sent to them the first time. That means it gives them life."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"It is the spirit that quickeneth; the flesh profiteth nothing: the words that I speak unto you, they are spirit, and they are life. But there are some of you that believe not. For Jesus knew from the beginning who they were that believed not, and who should betray him. And he said, Therefore said I unto you, that no man can come unto me, except it were given unto him of my Father. From that time many of his disciples went back, and walked no more with him.","ref":"John 6:63-66"},{"kind":"thought","text":"Thousands of people stopped following Jesus from His life-giving Words, so it's not much of a surprise that there would be people who stopped following Paul's ministry for the SAME Words."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not because Paul is a reprobate."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"It's because people NEED to fight against the truth. You don't see them fighting nearly as hard against lies, however."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even if people think of the Apostles as reprobates, they should still live lives that are right before the Lord. It's not about how Paul lives his own life. It's about how YOU live YOUR own life. If Paul was not living as an example, it still doesn't make sin the right lifestyle. They KNOW that prostitution is a sin, yet they are willing to overrule that sin and say, “Well, just because Paul says it's a sin doesn't mean it actually is; he's a reprobate.\" They know it's a sin. They know it from their conscience."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Which shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness, and their thoughts the mean while accusing or else excusing one another;)","ref":"Romans 2:15"},{"kind":"thought","text":"The conscience helps us know what is good and what is bad. We have to purposely overrule our own conscience if we are going to want to do something sinful."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even still, if anyone wanted to consider Paul a reprobate, he was still going to do what was good and honest before everyone. It didn't matter what kind of lies they might want to spread about him. He is going to minister to those that WANT and NEED the ministry."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a good verse to underline. Even if we tried to do something against the truth, it would not succeed."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"The truth ALWAYS wins. The truth is ALWAYS revealed."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of political liars today. They think they are sitting quite pretty on their hill of lies, but there are people who know the truth. There are people who are showing the proof of their lies."},{"kind":"thought","text":"They are getting found out, but they throw money at the problem to keep it as quiet as possible. Political leaders are good at that. But, even still, smart people will find out the truth. Stupid people will fall for the lies."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here, I'll go back to what we were referring to them as before."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministers will find out the truth. Unministers will fall for the lies."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now I say that Jesus Christ was a minister of the circumcision for the truth of God, to confirm the promises made unto the fathers: And that the Gentiles might glorify God for his mercy; as it is written, For this cause I will confess to thee among the Gentiles, and sing unto thy name.","ref":"Romans 15:8-9"},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Finding the truth is actually quite easy. Does it make a lot of hateful people angry? Well then, that's probably the truth."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"highlight","text":"I want a weak, humble minister, not a strong, prideful one."},{"kind":"para","text":"A prideful minister is an unminister. I speak for the whole church when I say, we want a minister that is willing to admit that he has a lot of shortcomings to work on. I want a minister who will say, \"I don't know everything. I am willing to simply say, 'I don't know.'\" Strangely enough, there are some ministers who tout that message, but they don't really mean it. They say, \"I will be the first to tell you I don't know everything\" when they are being the pulpit, but then you go to them in counseling, and it seems that they have an answer for literally everything. Even things you KNOW FOR SURE they don't have the answer to, they twist words around to make it sound like they are answering your question when, in actuality, they are just rephrasing the question back to you or repurposing the question to something else so you think that a DIFFERENT thing is the issue. It's okay... nay... it's PREFERRED that Bible teachers don't know something."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want to sit down with a Pastor and learn something with him instead of him pretending like he already has it all figured out. I want him to be up front with me and just say, \"I don't know? What do you think about it?\" Even if he DOES know, just humor me. That is not very likely to be the case in our modern church society, however. I don't think it was very common back then either except from the Apostles. Perhaps not even all the Apostles were willing to humble themselves like this either, though. Maybe it's only Paul that is willing to do it."},{"kind":"thought","text":"A prideful minister is an unminister. I speak for the whole church when I say, we want a minister that is willing to admit that he has a lot of shortcomings to work on. I want a minister who will say, \"I don't know everything. I am willing to simply say, 'I don't know.'\" Strangely enough, there are some ministers who tout that message, but they don't really mean it. They say, \"I will be the first to tell you I don't know everything\" when they are behind the pulpit, but then you go to them in counseling, and it seems that they have an answer for literally everything. Even things you KNOW FOR SURE they don't have the answer to, they twist words around to make it sound like they are answering your question when, in actuality, they are just rephrasing the question back to you or repurposing the question to something else so you think that a DIFFERENT thing is the issue."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul writes this letter to prepare them for when he comes. He desperately wants to come with a spirit of equipping and growth. He wants to help them, not hurt them. But if they are going to be this way before he gets there, then it's not going to be fun for either one of them."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"Ministry should be joyous. Edification should be the theme."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, it's usually sharpness and destruction that ministers must deal with the most..."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":13,"title":"A Summation","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, this is a perfect summary of everything we have read in this letter. It's been the hope for them this entire time. I usually take some time to recap what we have studied, but Paul has already done that for us."},{"kind":"highlight","text":"This is the hope for ministers. It is that we would be perfect (or complete in the Lord), comforted (especially together through ministry needs and work), of one mind (that is connected for the ministry), and living in peace with each other (able to love and minister to EACH OTHER FIRST before ministering to anyone else). If we can do all of that in our personal ministries, the Lord will grant us grace and peace. Yet, in modern church society, those two things are missing. We have no grace. We have no peace."},{"kind":"para","text":"It stems from the unministers. It stems from the people in the church who think that they are doing a service, when ultimately they are just looking to be served. They want to undo and unravel anything that is not going to meet their own personal needs."},{"kind":"thought","text":"If they serve someone and get nothing out of it, it does not suit them well. They must have something in return. That's painful..."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We don't have to do that in our churches anymore. A simple handshake will suffice. I've made mention of this before. Kisses are fine in Paris and from your grandma, but not here in America and from your brothers in Christ. Leave the holy kisses in Corinth."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"ALL the saints are pulling for this church. They want so badly to see this church succeed. They want the church to connect to all the other churches through supporting this apostle. Even with a group of Paul haters, I think the majority of the Christian brothers still want to see the church turn and go in the right direction."},{"kind":"thought","text":"It doesn't matter how far gone a church may be, it is sad to see church doors shut in this world."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"title":"Final Send Off","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Communion.\" I think that is a great word to end off on. We spoke about communion in the first letter."},{"kind":"thought","text":"The Lord's table is a perfect picture of communion. It's a perfect picture of unity and togetherness in ministry. It's the whole church agreeing about something. It's the entire church agreeing at once about the death and resurrection of our Lord. I can't think of anything more connecting than that."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is one of the deepest letters we will ever read from ANY of the Apostles, but especially from Paul. This is the letter to get to know the heart of Paul. It's the letter that proves His Apostleship. But it's also a letter that is easily used for our own ministering. I, personally, have a lot to think on. I have a lot to go back through and reread, and add to these notes. There is so much more we can get into in these notes that I know will be a benefit."},{"kind":"thought","text":"Every real minister of the Lord needs some encouragement through their giving of themselves in ministry. We all feel pretty used up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has (thankfully) given the ministers hope in this small letter. It should be one that we can come back to over and over again to get encouragement to continue in ministry, and continue to be spent for the people of the church (even if there is some who don't appreciate your work in the Lord)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14}],"verses":{"1":"This is the third time I am coming to you. In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word be established.","2":"I told you before, and foretell you, as if I were present, the second time; and being absent now I write to them which heretofore have sinned, and to all other, that, if I come again, I will not spare:","3":"Since ye seek a proof of Christ speaking in me, which to you-ward is not weak, but is mighty in you.","4":"For though he was crucified through weakness, yet he liveth by the power of God. For we also are weak in him, but we shall live with him by the power of God toward you.","5":"Examine yourselves, whether ye be in the faith; prove your own selves. Know ye not your own selves, how that Jesus Christ is in you, except ye be reprobates?","6":"But I trust that ye shall know that we are not reprobates.","7":"Now I pray to God that ye do no evil; not that we should appear approved, but that ye should do that which is honest, though we be as reprobates.","8":"For we can do nothing against the truth, but for the truth.","9":"For we are glad, when we are weak, and ye are strong: and this also we wish, even your perfection.","10":"Therefore I write these things being absent, lest being present I should use sharpness, according to the power which the Lord hath given me to edification, and not to destruction.","11":"Finally, brethren, farewell. Be perfect, be of good comfort, be of one mind, live in peace; and the God of love and peace shall be with you.","12":"Greet one another with an holy kiss.","13":"All the saints salute you.","14":"The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Ghost, be with you all. Amen."}},
  },
};
